  'A data science team needs to build an image classification model but has a limited amount of labeled data. They want to leverage existing knowledge to accelerate development and improve accuracy. Which approach is MOST appropriate?',
  'mcq',
  '["Build a custom Convolutional Neural Network (CNN) from scratch and train it on their limited dataset.","Use a pre-trained Image Classification model from Amazon SageMaker JumpStart and fine-tune it with their specific data.","Apply the Amazon SageMaker k-means algorithm to cluster the images and then manually label the clusters.","Train an Amazon SageMaker Linear Learner model on the image pixel values directly."]'::json,
  '{1}'::int[],
  'Option B (correct) is the most appropriate approach. Using a pre-trained model from Amazon SageMaker JumpStart and fine-tuning it leverages transfer learning, which is highly effective for tasks with limited data. Option A, building a custom CNN from scratch, would likely lead to overfitting and poor performance with limited data (anti-pattern ap-2.1-001). Option C, k-means, is an unsupervised clustering algorithm and not suitable for supervised image classification. Option D, Linear Learner, is not designed for complex image data.',
