INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_15_m_15_2_64',
  'Which SageMaker feature helps automate the process of finding the best hyperparameters for a model?',
  'mcq',
  '["Amazon SageMaker Experiments, for tracking runs.","Amazon SageMaker Automatic Model Tuning (HPO), for optimization.","Amazon SageMaker Debugger, for monitoring training.","Amazon SageMaker Clarify, for bias detection."]'::json,
  '{1}'::int[],
  'Option B (correct) is Amazon SageMaker Automatic Model Tuning (HPO), which automates the process of finding the best combination of hyperparameters for a model, significantly improving model performance. Option A (Amazon SageMaker Experiments) tracks experiment metadata. Option C (Amazon SageMaker Debugger) monitors training job internals. Option D (Amazon SageMaker Clarify) is for bias detection and explainability.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain15_3',
  NOW(),
  75,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_15_2',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_15_m_15_2_65',
  'What is the primary purpose of SageMaker Managed Spot Training?',
  'mcq',
  '["To ensure that training jobs always run on dedicated instances.","To reduce the cost of training jobs by using EC2 Spot Instances, with checkpointing for fault tolerance.","To automatically scale the number of instances based on training workload.","To provide real-time monitoring of training job metrics and resource utilization."]'::json,
  '{1}'::int[],
  'Option B (correct) describes the primary purpose of SageMaker Managed Spot Training, which is to significantly reduce training costs by leveraging Amazon EC2 Spot Instances, while using checkpointing to ensure interrupted jobs can resume from the last saved state. Option A is incorrect, as it uses Spot Instances, not dedicated. Option C describes auto-scaling, which is a different feature. Option D describes monitoring tools like SageMaker Debugger or CloudWatch.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain15_3',
  NOW(),
  76,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_15_2',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_15_m_15_2_66',
  'Which technique helps prevent overfitting by adding a penalty to the loss function based on the magnitude of model weights?',
  'mcq',
  '["Early stopping, to halt training prematurely.","L1 or L2 regularization, to constrain model complexity.","Data augmentation, to increase dataset size.","Cross-validation, for robust evaluation."]'::json,
  '{1}'::int[],
  'Option B (correct) refers to L1 (Lasso) or L2 (Ridge) regularization, which are techniques that add a penalty term to the loss function based on the magnitude of the model''s weights, effectively constraining model complexity and preventing overfitting. Option A (Early stopping) is a different regularization technique. Option C (Data augmentation) is a data preprocessing technique. Option D (Cross-validation) is an evaluation strategy.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain15_3',
  NOW(),
  77,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_15_2',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_15_m_15_2_67',
  'What is the main benefit of using SageMaker Experiments?',
  'mcq',
  '["To deploy models to production endpoints.","To automatically provision training infrastructure.","To organize, track, compare, and evaluate machine learning experiments.","To detect data and model bias after training."]'::json,
  '{2}'::int[],
  'Option C (correct) describes the main benefit of Amazon SageMaker Experiments, which provides a systematic way to organize, track, compare, and evaluate different machine learning experiments, including their inputs, parameters, and results. Option A describes SageMaker Endpoints. Option B describes SageMaker Training Jobs. Option D describes SageMaker Clarify.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain15_3',
  NOW(),
  78,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_15_2',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_15_m_15_2_68',
  'Which distributed training strategy involves splitting the training data across multiple instances?',
  'mcq',
  '["Model parallelism, where the model itself is split.","Data parallelism, where each instance processes a subset of the data.","Pipeline parallelism, for sequential processing.","Hybrid parallelism, combining different strategies."]'::json,
  '{1}'::int[],
  'Option B (correct) is data parallelism, a distributed training strategy where each training instance receives a subset of the training data, computes gradients locally, and then aggregates them to update the model. Option A (Model parallelism) involves splitting the model itself across instances. Options C and D are more advanced or combined strategies.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain15_3',
  NOW(),
  79,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_15_2',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_15_m_15_2_69',
  'What is early stopping primarily used for during model training?',
  'mcq',
  '["To increase the learning rate dynamically.","To prevent overfitting by stopping training when performance on a validation set degrades.","To ensure the model trains for a fixed number of epochs.","To introduce noise into the training data."]'::json,
  '{1}'::int[],
  'Option B (correct) describes early stopping, a regularization technique used to prevent overfitting by monitoring the model''s performance on a validation set and halting training when the validation performance stops improving or starts to degrade. Option A relates to learning rate schedules. Option C is a fixed training approach. Option D is related to data augmentation or noise injection, not early stopping.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain15_3',
  NOW(),
  80,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_15_2',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_15_m_15_2_70',
  'A data scientist wants to reduce the cost of long-running, fault-tolerant training jobs. Which SageMaker feature should be utilized?',
  'mcq',
  '["Amazon SageMaker Experiments, for cost tracking.","Amazon SageMaker Managed Spot Training, for cost savings with resilience.","Amazon SageMaker Debugger, for performance optimization.","Amazon SageMaker Automatic Model Tuning, for faster convergence."]'::json,
  '{1}'::int[],
  'Option B (correct) is Amazon SageMaker Managed Spot Training, which leverages cost-effective Amazon EC2 Spot Instances and provides checkpointing to ensure fault tolerance for long-running training jobs, significantly reducing costs. Option A (Amazon SageMaker Experiments) tracks metadata, not directly reduces cost. Option C (Amazon SageMaker Debugger) optimizes performance, which can indirectly save cost but isn''t its primary function. Option D (Amazon SageMaker Automatic Model Tuning) optimizes hyperparameters for better model performance, not primarily cost reduction.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain15_3',
  NOW(),
  81,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_15_2',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_15_m_15_2_71',
  'Which SageMaker feature allows a data scientist to compare different training runs, including metrics and hyperparameters?',
  'mcq',
  '["Amazon SageMaker Clarify, for bias comparison.","Amazon SageMaker Experiments, for comprehensive tracking and comparison.","Amazon SageMaker Debugger, for real-time metric visualization.","Amazon SageMaker JumpStart, for pre-built solution comparison."]'::json,
  '{1}'::int[],
  'Option B (correct) is Amazon SageMaker Experiments, which provides a centralized way to track, compare, and evaluate different training runs, including their metrics, hyperparameters, and artifacts, facilitating reproducibility and model selection. Option A (Amazon SageMaker Clarify) is for bias. Option C (Amazon SageMaker Debugger) is for real-time monitoring during a single training job. Option D (Amazon SageMaker JumpStart) provides pre-built solutions, not a comparison tool for custom runs.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain15_3',
  NOW(),
  82,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_15_2',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_15_m_15_2_72',
  'What is a common regularization technique that randomly sets a fraction of neuron outputs to zero during training?',
  'mcq',
  '["L1 regularization, for weight sparsity.","Early stopping, to prevent overfitting.","Dropout, to reduce co-adaptation of neurons.","Batch normalization, for stable training."]'::json,
  '{2}'::int[],
  'Option C (correct) is Dropout, a powerful regularization technique where a random fraction of neurons are ''dropped out'' (their outputs set to zero) during each training step, preventing complex co-adaptations and reducing overfitting. Option A (L1 regularization) adds a penalty to weights. Option B (Early stopping) halts training. Option D (Batch normalization) normalizes layer inputs to stabilize training.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain15_3',
  NOW(),
  83,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_15_2',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_15_m_15_2_73',
  'A machine learning team needs to train a very large model that cannot fit into the memory of a single GPU. Which distributed training strategy is most appropriate?',
  'mcq',
  '["Data parallelism, to distribute data across GPUs.","Model parallelism, to split the model layers across multiple GPUs.","Synchronous training, to ensure all GPUs process data at the same time.","Asynchronous training, for faster but potentially less stable updates."]'::json,
  '{1}'::int[],
  'Option B (correct) is model parallelism, which is most appropriate when a model is too large to fit into the memory of a single GPU. It involves splitting the model''s layers or components across multiple GPUs or instances. Option A (Data parallelism) distributes data, not the model, and assumes the model fits on a single device. Options C and D describe synchronization strategies for data parallelism, not model distribution.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain15_3',
  NOW(),
  84,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_15_2',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_15_m_15_2_74',
  'A data scientist is running multiple training jobs with varying hyperparameters and wants to keep track of all inputs, outputs, and results for reproducibility. Which SageMaker service should they use?',
  'mcq',
  '["Amazon SageMaker Clarify, for model explainability.","Amazon SageMaker Debugger, for real-time training analysis.","Amazon SageMaker Experiments, for comprehensive experiment tracking.","Amazon SageMaker Automatic Model Tuning, for automated hyperparameter search."]'::json,
  '{2}'::int[],
  'Option C (correct) is Amazon SageMaker Experiments, which is designed to help data scientists organize, track, compare, and evaluate all aspects of their machine learning experiments, including inputs, outputs, parameters, and results, ensuring reproducibility. Option A (Amazon SageMaker Clarify) is for bias and explainability. Option B (Amazon SageMaker Debugger) monitors training job internals. Option D (Amazon SageMaker Automatic Model Tuning) automates hyperparameter search, but Experiments tracks the results of those searches.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain15_3',
  NOW(),
  85,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_15_2',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_15_m_15_2_75',
  'What is the primary purpose of Amazon SageMaker Automatic Model Tuning (HPO)?',
  'mcq',
  '["To automatically find the best combination of hyperparameters for a machine learning model.","To manage the deployment of trained machine learning models to production endpoints.","To provide a fully managed environment for data preprocessing and feature engineering.","To track and compare the results of different machine learning experiments."]'::json,
  '{0}'::int[],
  'Option A (correct) accurately describes the primary purpose of Amazon SageMaker Automatic Model Tuning, which automates the process of finding the optimal hyperparameters for a model, improving its performance. Option B is handled by SageMaker Endpoints. Option C is typically done with SageMaker Processing Jobs or other data services. Option D is the function of SageMaker Experiments.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain15_3',
  NOW(),
  86,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_15_2',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_15_m_15_2_76',
  'What is a significant benefit of using Amazon SageMaker Managed Spot Training for long-running training jobs?',
  'mcq',
  '["It guarantees a fixed training duration regardless of instance availability.","It can reduce training costs by up to 90% by utilizing EC2 Spot Instances, with checkpointing to resume interrupted jobs.","It automatically scales the number of GPU instances based on data volume.","It eliminates the need for hyperparameter tuning by optimizing model parameters during training."]'::json,
  '{1}'::int[],
  'Option B (correct) accurately describes the significant benefit of SageMaker Managed Spot Training, which leverages EC2 Spot Instances to reduce costs substantially. With checkpointing, interrupted jobs can resume, minimizing lost work (anti-pattern ap-2.2-002). Option A is incorrect; Spot Instances do not guarantee fixed duration due to potential interruptions. Option C is related to distributed training, not specifically Spot Training''s primary benefit. Option D is incorrect; Spot Training does not eliminate the need for hyperparameter tuning.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain15_3',
  NOW(),
  87,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_15_2',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_15_m_15_2_77',
  'Which set of techniques is commonly used to prevent overfitting during the training of machine learning models?',
  'mcq',
  '["Increasing the model''s complexity and adding more layers.","Using a wider hyperparameter search space and fewer trials.","Applying regularization techniques such as L1, L2, or dropout.","Training the model for a significantly longer duration with more epochs."]'::json,
  '{2}'::int[],
  'Option C (correct) lists common regularization techniques like L1, L2, and dropout, which are effective in preventing overfitting by penalizing complex models or randomly dropping units during training. Option A would likely exacerbate overfitting. Option B can lead to suboptimal hyperparameter configurations. Option D, training for longer, can also lead to more overfitting if not combined with early stopping or other regularization.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain15_3',
  NOW(),
  88,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_15_2',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_15_m_15_2_78',
  'What is the core concept of data parallelism in distributed machine learning training?',
  'mcq',
  '["Distributing different portions of the training data across multiple instances, with each instance training a full copy of the model.","Splitting the model''s layers or parameters across multiple instances, with each instance processing a portion of the model.","Running the entire training process on a single, very powerful instance to maximize throughput.","Using a single GPU to process all data sequentially to ensure consistency."]'::json,
  '{0}'::int[],
  'Option A (correct) accurately defines data parallelism, where the training dataset is divided among multiple instances, and each instance trains a complete replica of the model. Gradients are then aggregated to update the model. Option B describes model parallelism. Option C and D describe single-instance or sequential processing, not distributed training strategies.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain15_3',
  NOW(),
  89,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_15_2',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_15_m_15_2_79',
  'What is a best practice for defining the search space and number of trials when using Amazon SageMaker Automatic Model Tuning (HPO) to find optimal hyperparameters efficiently?',
  'mcq',
  '["Manually test a very wide range of hyperparameters without using HPO.","Use Bayesian optimization with narrow search ranges based on domain knowledge to converge faster.","Run HPO with an extremely wide search space and a minimal number of trials to save costs.","Increase the number of training epochs significantly for each trial to ensure thorough exploration."]'::json,
  '{1}'::int[],
  'Option B (correct) describes the best practice for efficient HPO. Using Bayesian optimization helps explore the search space more intelligently, and narrowing ranges based on domain knowledge or previous experiments allows for faster convergence to optimal hyperparameters. Option A is inefficient and time-consuming. Option C (anti-pattern ap-2.2-001) is inefficient and likely to miss optimal configurations. Option D, increasing epochs, is not directly related to HPO search space definition and can increase training time unnecessarily.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain15_3',
  NOW(),
  90,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_15_2',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_15_m_15_2_80',
  'What is the primary purpose of cross-validation strategies in model evaluation?',
  'mcq',
  '["To simplify the model architecture to reduce computational cost.","To select the most relevant features for model training.","To provide a more robust and reliable estimate of model performance by training and testing on different subsets of data.","To deploy the model to multiple production environments simultaneously."]'::json,
  '{2}'::int[],
  'Option C (correct) accurately describes the primary purpose of cross-validation, which helps to assess how well a model generalizes to an independent dataset by repeatedly splitting the data into training and validation sets. This provides a more robust estimate of performance than a single train-test split. Option A is model simplification. Option B is feature engineering. Option D is model deployment.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain15_3',
  NOW(),
  91,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_15_2',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_15_m_15_2_81',
  'A machine learning team is training a very large deep learning model that exceeds the memory capacity of a single Amazon EC2 GPU instance. Which distributed training strategy should they implement?',
  'mcq',
  '["Utilize data parallelism to distribute the training data across multiple instances.","Reduce the model''s complexity significantly to fit within a single instance.","Implement model parallelism to distribute the model''s layers or parameters across multiple instances.","Increase the learning rate to accelerate convergence on a single instance."]'::json,
  '{2}'::int[],
  'Option C (correct) is model parallelism, which is the appropriate strategy when the model itself is too large to fit into the memory of a single GPU. It involves splitting the model''s layers or parameters across multiple instances. Option A, data parallelism, is for distributing data, not for models that are too large. Option B might compromise model performance. Option D is a hyperparameter choice and does not address the memory constraint.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain15_3',
  NOW(),
  92,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_15_2',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_15_m_15_2_82',
  'What is ''early stopping'' as a regularization technique in machine learning?',
  'mcq',
  '["A method to automatically stop data preprocessing when anomalies are detected.","A technique that halts model training when performance on a validation dataset stops improving, preventing overfitting.","A strategy for quickly deploying a model to an inference endpoint after initial training.","A process to reduce the number of features in a dataset before training begins."]'::json,
  '{1}'::int[],
  'Option B (correct) accurately defines early stopping, a regularization technique that monitors a model''s performance on a validation set and stops training when performance plateaus or degrades, thereby preventing the model from overfitting to the training data. Options A, C, and D describe unrelated concepts in the ML lifecycle.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain15_3',
  NOW(),
  93,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_15_2',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_15_m_15_2_83',
  'A data scientist wants to compare the performance of different model architectures and hyperparameter sets systematically across multiple training runs. Which Amazon SageMaker service is best suited for organizing and tracking these experiments?',
  'mcq',
  '["Amazon SageMaker Automatic Model Tuning","Amazon SageMaker Experiments","Amazon SageMaker Debugger","Amazon SageMaker Clarify"]'::json,
  '{1}'::int[],
  'Option B (correct) is Amazon SageMaker Experiments, which provides tools to organize, track, compare, and evaluate machine learning experiments, including different model architectures and hyperparameter sets. Option A, Automatic Model Tuning, is for finding optimal hyperparameters, not tracking arbitrary experiments. Option C, Debugger, is for analyzing training job issues. Option D, Clarify, is for bias detection and explainability.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain15_3',
  NOW(),
  94,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_15_2',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

