  'quiz_domain16_3',
  NOW(),
  48,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_16_1',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_16_m_16_1_68',
  'Which Amazon SageMaker inference option is best suited for scenarios requiring immediate responses to individual requests with minimal latency?',
  'mcq',
  '["Amazon SageMaker Real-time Inference","Amazon SageMaker Batch Transform","Amazon SageMaker Asynchronous Inference","Amazon SageMaker Serverless Inference"]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A (Amazon SageMaker Real-time Inference) is the correct choice. Real-time inference endpoints are designed for low-latency, high-throughput inference where immediate responses are critical, such as in web applications or interactive services. Option B (Amazon SageMaker Batch Transform) is for processing large datasets offline. Option C (Amazon SageMaker Asynchronous Inference) is for large payloads or long processing times where immediate responses are not required. Option D (Amazon SageMaker Serverless Inference) is for intermittent workloads with unpredictable traffic, but real-time offers the lowest latency for consistent, interactive use cases.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain16_3',
  NOW(),
  49,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_16_1',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_16_m_16_1_69',
  'A company needs to process millions of customer records daily to generate personalized recommendations. The processing can run overnight, and there is no strict real-time latency requirement. The primary concern is cost-effectiveness for processing a large, non-interactive dataset. Which Amazon SageMaker inference option should be chosen?',
  'mcq',
  '["Amazon SageMaker Real-time Inference","Amazon SageMaker Batch Transform","Amazon SageMaker Serverless Inference","Amazon SageMaker Asynchronous Inference"]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (Amazon SageMaker Batch Transform) is the correct choice. Batch Transform is designed for processing large datasets offline. It spins up compute resources, processes the data, and then terminates the resources, making it highly cost-effective for non-interactive, large-scale inference jobs without strict latency requirements. Option A (Amazon SageMaker Real-time Inference) uses persistent instances and is costly for batch workloads (anti-pattern ap-3.1-001). Option C (Amazon SageMaker Serverless Inference) is for intermittent, unpredictable real-time traffic, not large batch processing. Option D (Amazon SageMaker Asynchronous Inference) is for large payloads with longer processing times, but Batch Transform is generally more cost-effective for very large, scheduled batch jobs.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain16_3',
  NOW(),
  50,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_16_1',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_16_m_16_1_70',
  'Which Amazon SageMaker inference option is ideal for workloads with intermittent or unpredictable traffic patterns, automatically scaling compute capacity up and down to zero, and charging only for the duration of inference requests?',
  'mcq',
  '["Amazon SageMaker Real-time Inference","Amazon SageMaker Batch Transform","Amazon SageMaker Serverless Inference","Amazon SageMaker Asynchronous Inference"]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (Amazon SageMaker Serverless Inference) is the correct choice. Serverless Inference automatically provisions and scales compute capacity based on traffic, including scaling down to zero when idle. This makes it highly cost-effective for intermittent or unpredictable workloads, as you only pay for the compute time used for inference requests. Option A (Amazon SageMaker Real-time Inference) uses provisioned instances that incur costs even when idle. Option B (Amazon SageMaker Batch Transform) is for offline processing of large datasets. Option D (Amazon SageMaker Asynchronous Inference) is for large payloads and long processing times, but still requires provisioned capacity.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain16_3',
  NOW(),
  51,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_16_1',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_16_m_16_1_71',
  'A machine learning application experiences highly variable inference traffic throughout the day, with significant peaks and troughs. The team wants to ensure high availability during peak times while minimizing costs during low traffic periods. Which feature should be configured for the Amazon SageMaker real-time endpoint?',
  'mcq',
  '["SageMaker Neo compilation","Multi-model endpoint deployment","Endpoint auto-scaling","AWS Inferentia instance types"]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (Endpoint auto-scaling) is the correct feature. Configuring Application Auto Scaling for Amazon SageMaker endpoints allows the endpoint to automatically adjust the number of instances based on demand, ensuring sufficient capacity during traffic spikes and scaling down during low traffic to minimize costs (addressing anti-pattern ap-3.1-002). Option A (SageMaker Neo compilation) optimizes models for specific hardware. Option B (Multi-model endpoint deployment) allows hosting multiple models on a single endpoint. Option D (AWS Inferentia instance types) are for cost-optimized inference acceleration, but do not inherently manage variable traffic patterns without auto-scaling.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain16_3',
  NOW(),
  52,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_16_1',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_16_m_16_1_72',
  'Which AWS service or feature is specifically designed to provide cost-optimized machine learning inference by using custom-designed chips?',
  'mcq',
  '["AWS Inferentia","Amazon SageMaker Neo","AWS Lambda","Amazon EC2 P3 instances"]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A (AWS Inferentia) is the correct answer. AWS Inferentia is a machine learning inference chip custom-designed by AWS to provide high performance and cost-effective inference. Option B (Amazon SageMaker Neo) compiles models to run faster on various hardware, but doesn''t refer to a specific chip. Option C (AWS Lambda) is a serverless compute service. Option D (Amazon EC2 P3 instances) are GPU-accelerated instances, typically used for training, and while they can do inference, Inferentia is specifically designed for cost-optimized inference.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain16_3',
  NOW(),
  53,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_16_1',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_16_m_16_1_73',
  'A machine learning model is designed to process large image files (up to 100 MB) and perform complex analysis that can take several minutes per request. Users do not require immediate responses but expect the results to be available within a reasonable timeframe. Which Amazon SageMaker inference option is most suitable for this scenario?',
  'mcq',
  '["Amazon SageMaker Real-time Inference","Amazon SageMaker Batch Transform","Amazon SageMaker Serverless Inference","Amazon SageMaker Asynchronous Inference"]'::json,
  '{3}',
  'Option D (Amazon SageMaker Asynchronous Inference) is the correct choice. It is designed for workloads with large payload sizes (up to 1 GB), long processing times (up to 15 minutes), and where immediate responses are not required. It queues requests and processes them as capacity becomes available, then stores results in Amazon S3. Option A (Amazon SageMaker Real-time Inference) is for low-latency, small-payload requests. Option B (Amazon SageMaker Batch Transform) is for large offline datasets, not individual requests. Option C (Amazon SageMaker Serverless Inference) is for intermittent real-time traffic with smaller payloads and faster response times.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain16_3',
  NOW(),
  54,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_16_1',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_16_m_16_1_74',
  'Which Amazon SageMaker feature allows you to optimize machine learning models for deployment on various hardware targets, including edge devices and different instance types, by compiling them to run faster and with less memory?',
  'mcq',
  '["Amazon SageMaker Model Monitor","Amazon SageMaker Feature Store","Amazon SageMaker Neo","Amazon SageMaker Ground Truth"]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (Amazon SageMaker Neo) is the correct feature. SageMaker Neo compiles machine learning models to optimize them for specific hardware targets, resulting in faster inference and reduced memory footprint. Option A (Amazon SageMaker Model Monitor) is for monitoring model quality in production. Option B (Amazon SageMaker Feature Store) is for storing and serving ML features. Option D (Amazon SageMaker Ground Truth) is for data labeling.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain16_3',
  NOW(),
  55,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_16_1',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_16_m_16_1_75',
  'A data scientist is considering using Amazon SageMaker Real-time Inference for a nightly job that processes millions of records. This job does not require immediate results and can tolerate several hours of processing time. What is the primary reason this approach is an anti-pattern?',
  'mcq',
  '["Real-time endpoints are too slow for large datasets.","Real-time endpoints are designed for interactive, low-latency use cases and are costly for non-interactive batch workloads.","Real-time endpoints lack the necessary security features for batch processing.","Real-time endpoints do not support custom Docker images for batch jobs."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (Real-time endpoints are designed for interactive, low-latency use cases and are costly for non-interactive batch workloads) correctly identifies the anti-pattern (ap-3.1-001). Amazon SageMaker Real-time Inference endpoints maintain persistent instances, incurring continuous costs. For large, non-interactive batch jobs, Amazon SageMaker Batch Transform is significantly more cost-effective as it provisions ephemeral compute resources only for the duration of the job. Option A is incorrect; real-time endpoints are fast for individual requests. Option C and D are incorrect; real-time endpoints support security features and custom Docker images.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain16_3',
  NOW(),
  56,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_16_1',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_16_m_16_1_76',
  'A financial institution requires a machine learning model to provide fraud detection with extremely low latency (under 50ms) for every transaction. The model will receive consistent, high-volume traffic. Which Amazon SageMaker inference option and instance type characteristic should be prioritized?',
  'mcq',
  '["SageMaker Batch Transform for cost-effectiveness.","SageMaker Real-time Inference with GPU-accelerated instances for high throughput and low latency.","SageMaker Serverless Inference for automatic scaling to zero.","SageMaker Asynchronous Inference for large payload support."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (SageMaker Real-time Inference with GPU-accelerated instances for high throughput and low latency) is the correct choice. For extremely low latency and high-volume, consistent traffic, Amazon SageMaker Real-time Inference is necessary. Choosing GPU-accelerated instances (like ml.p3 or ml.g4dn) can provide the required computational power for high throughput and minimal latency. Option A (SageMaker Batch Transform) is for offline processing and does not meet latency requirements. Option C (SageMaker Serverless Inference) is for intermittent traffic and might not guarantee consistent low latency under high, sustained load. Option D (SageMaker Asynchronous Inference) is for large payloads and longer processing times, not low latency.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain16_3',
  NOW(),
  57,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_16_1',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_16_m_16_1_77',
  'Which Amazon SageMaker feature allows you to chain multiple containers together to perform sequential steps, such as data pre-processing, model inference, and post-processing, all within a single real-time endpoint?',
  'mcq',
  '["Multi-model endpoints","Inference pipelines","Batch Transform jobs","Serverless Inference"]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (Inference pipelines) is the correct feature. Amazon SageMaker Inference Pipelines allow you to combine multiple processing steps (e.g., data pre-processing, model inference, and post-processing) into a single endpoint. Each step runs in its own container, and data flows sequentially between them. Option A (Multi-model endpoints) host multiple models on a single endpoint, but each model typically runs independently. Option C (Batch Transform jobs) are for offline processing. Option D (Serverless Inference) is an endpoint type for intermittent traffic, not a mechanism for chaining processing steps.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain16_3',
  NOW(),
  58,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_16_1',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_16_m_16_1_78',
  'A startup is launching a new machine learning-powered feature with an unpredictable user base and highly variable traffic patterns. They want to minimize operational overhead and only pay for inference when requests are made. Which Amazon SageMaker inference option should they choose?',
  'mcq',
  '["Amazon SageMaker Real-time Inference with fixed instance count","Amazon SageMaker Batch Transform for daily processing","Amazon SageMaker Serverless Inference for automatic scaling and pay-per-use billing","Amazon SageMaker Asynchronous Inference with a large queue"]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (Amazon SageMaker Serverless Inference for automatic scaling and pay-per-use billing) is the correct choice. Serverless Inference is ideal for unpredictable and intermittent traffic because it automatically scales compute capacity up and down, including to zero, and you only pay for the inference duration. This minimizes operational overhead and costs. Option A (Amazon SageMaker Real-time Inference with fixed instance count) would lead to over-provisioning and higher costs during low traffic or under-provisioning during peak traffic. Option B (Amazon SageMaker Batch Transform) is for offline processing, not real-time features. Option D (Amazon SageMaker Asynchronous Inference) is for large payloads and longer processing times, not for minimizing operational overhead and pay-per-use for unpredictable real-time traffic.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain16_3',
  NOW(),
  59,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_16_1',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_16_m_16_1_79',
  'A large enterprise is developing a new fraud detection model that needs to process millions of transactions daily. The model''s inference workload is highly variable, with significant spikes during peak business hours and very low traffic overnight. The company requires a solution that automatically scales to meet demand, minimizes operational costs by only paying for inference capacity when needed, and ensures high availability without manual intervention. The model artifacts are stored in Amazon S3.',
  'mcq',
  '["Deploy the model to an Amazon SageMaker real-time endpoint with a fixed instance count to handle peak load.","Deploy the model to an Amazon SageMaker Serverless Inference endpoint.","Deploy the model to an Amazon SageMaker asynchronous inference endpoint with auto-scaling enabled.","Deploy the model to an Amazon SageMaker real-time endpoint and configure Application Auto Scaling based on invocation metrics."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (correct) is the most suitable solution. Amazon SageMaker Serverless Inference automatically scales compute capacity based on traffic patterns, allowing the company to pay only for the duration of inference requests and the amount of data processed, which perfectly aligns with the requirement for variable workloads and cost minimization. It eliminates the need to choose instance types or manage scaling policies.
Option A (incorrect) would lead to significant over-provisioning and high costs during low-traffic periods, failing the cost-effectiveness requirement. A fixed instance count cannot adapt to variable demand.
Option C (incorrect) is designed for large payload sizes and long processing times, where clients don''t need immediate responses. While it supports auto-scaling, the scenario describes ''variable inference traffic'' which implies potentially real-time or near real-time responses, and Serverless Inference is a more direct fit for cost-effective scaling for variable interactive workloads.
Option D (incorrect) is a valid approach for real-time endpoints with variable traffic, but it still involves managing instance types and configuring auto-scaling policies. Amazon SageMaker Serverless Inference offers a fully managed, ''pay-per-inference'' model that inherently handles scaling without explicit configuration, providing the LEAST operational overhead and MOST cost-effective solution for highly variable, intermittent workloads compared to managing auto-scaling for a real-time endpoint.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain16_3',
  NOW(),
  60,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_16_1',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_16_m_16_1_80',
  'A financial services company processes daily batch files containing millions of customer transactions for fraud detection. The inference job runs once every 24 hours, typically taking several hours to complete, and the results are stored back into Amazon S3 for downstream processing. The company needs to minimize compute costs for these non-interactive, large-scale inference tasks and avoid provisioning persistent infrastructure. The current solution uses a custom EC2 cluster that is manually scaled up and down.',
  'mcq',
  '["Migrate the model to an Amazon SageMaker real-time endpoint and invoke it with a custom script that processes the batch files in parallel.","Utilize Amazon SageMaker Batch Transform to process the daily transaction files.","Deploy the model to an Amazon SageMaker Serverless Inference endpoint and send individual transaction records for processing.","Containerize the model and deploy it to an Amazon ECR repository, then run it on an Amazon ECS cluster with AWS Fargate for batch processing."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (correct) is the most appropriate solution. Amazon SageMaker Batch Transform is specifically designed for non-interactive, large-scale inference on entire datasets. It provisions compute resources, processes the input data, and then automatically shuts down, ensuring cost-efficiency by only paying for the duration of the job. This directly addresses the requirement to minimize compute costs for a daily batch job without persistent infrastructure.
Option A (incorrect) represents an anti-pattern (ap-3.1-001). Using a real-time endpoint for large batch scoring jobs is inefficient and costly because real-time endpoints are designed for persistent, low-latency, interactive inference and would incur costs even when idle or between batch invocations.
Option C (incorrect) is suitable for variable, intermittent, real-time inference, but not for processing millions of transactions in a single large batch. Sending individual records would incur high invocation overhead and might not be cost-effective for a massive batch job compared to Batch Transform.
Option D (incorrect) is a viable option for containerized batch processing, but it introduces more operational overhead compared to Amazon SageMaker Batch Transform, which is a fully managed service specifically tailored for ML batch inference, handling infrastructure provisioning and scaling automatically.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain16_3',
  NOW(),
  61,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_16_1',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_16_m_16_1_81',
  'A healthcare provider is developing a new medical imaging analysis model that requires significant computational resources for inference, often processing large image files (up to 500MB each) that can take several minutes per image. Users submit images and expect results within an hour, but not instantaneously. The solution must be cost-effective, scale automatically based on the queue of incoming images, and handle potential processing failures gracefully with retries. The model is already trained and stored in Amazon S3.',
  'mcq',
  '["Deploy the model to an Amazon SageMaker real-time endpoint and increase the instance count to handle the large files.","Implement an Amazon SageMaker Batch Transform job that is triggered periodically to process new images.","Deploy the model to an Amazon SageMaker Asynchronous Inference endpoint.","Containerize the model and deploy it to an AWS Lambda function with a high memory configuration and extended timeout."]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (correct) is the most suitable choice. Amazon SageMaker Asynchronous Inference is designed for models with large payload sizes, long processing times, and when clients do not need an immediate response. It automatically scales based on the queue of incoming requests, handles retries, and integrates with Amazon S3 for input/output, aligning perfectly with the requirements for large image files, multi-minute processing, and cost-effective scaling without immediate response needs.
Option A (incorrect) would be extremely expensive and inefficient for long-running, large-payload inferences. Real-time endpoints are optimized for low-latency, small-payload, interactive inference, and keeping instances warm for multi-minute processing would be cost-prohibitive.
Option B (incorrect) would require periodic triggering, which might not meet the ''within an hour'' expectation if the trigger interval is too long or if the queue builds up significantly between runs. Asynchronous Inference offers more dynamic, event-driven processing for individual requests.
Option D (incorrect) is generally not suitable for processing large image files that take several minutes. AWS Lambda has strict payload size limits (6MB direct invocation, up to 50MB via /tmp, or S3 for larger) and a maximum execution duration of 15 minutes, which would likely be exceeded by the ''several minutes per image'' processing time for 500MB files.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain16_3',
  NOW(),
  62,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_16_1',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_16_m_16_1_82',
  'A data science team is developing multiple machine learning models for various business units. Each model has unique dependencies and requires a dedicated inference environment. The team wants to deploy these models to a single Amazon SageMaker endpoint to optimize resource utilization and reduce operational overhead, especially for models with low individual traffic but high aggregate traffic. The solution must allow independent updates for each model without affecting others.',
  'mcq',
  '["Deploy each model to its own dedicated Amazon SageMaker real-time endpoint with individual auto-scaling policies.","Utilize an Amazon SageMaker Multi-Model Endpoint to host all models.","Create an Amazon SageMaker Inference Pipeline to chain multiple models together.","Deploy all models as separate containers to an Amazon ECR repository and manage them with an Amazon ECS cluster."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (correct) is the ideal solution. An Amazon SageMaker Multi-Model Endpoint allows hosting multiple models on a single endpoint, sharing the underlying compute instances. This significantly optimizes resource utilization and reduces operational overhead, especially for scenarios where individual models have low traffic but aggregate traffic is substantial. It also supports dynamic loading and unloading of models, enabling independent updates without affecting other models on the same endpoint.
Option A (incorrect) would lead to higher operational overhead and potentially higher costs, as each endpoint would require its own set of instances, even if traffic is low, contradicting the goal of optimizing resource utilization.
Option C (incorrect) is used for chaining multiple models or pre-processing steps together to form a single inference request, not for hosting independent models that serve different purposes or business units.
Option D (incorrect) would provide a similar multi-model hosting capability but would require the team to manage the underlying Amazon ECS infrastructure, including scaling, patching, and monitoring, which increases operational overhead compared to the fully managed SageMaker Multi-Model Endpoint.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain16_3',
  NOW(),
  63,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_16_1',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_16_m_16_1_83',
  'A startup is deploying a new deep learning model for image classification that requires high throughput and low latency. The model is computationally intensive, and the company is very sensitive to inference costs. They have identified that the model''s architecture is compatible with specialized hardware acceleration. The goal is to achieve the lowest possible inference cost while maintaining performance.',
  'mcq',
  '["Deploy the model to an Amazon SageMaker real-time endpoint using GPU-accelerated instances (e.g., ml.g4dn).","Compile the model using Amazon SageMaker Neo and deploy it to an Amazon SageMaker real-time endpoint on an AWS Inferentia instance.","Deploy the model to an Amazon SageMaker Serverless Inference endpoint to pay only for actual invocations.","Optimize the model using quantization and pruning techniques, then deploy it to a standard CPU instance (e.g., ml.m5) on a SageMaker real-time endpoint."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (correct) is the most cost-effective solution for deep learning inference that requires high throughput and low latency, especially when cost is a primary concern and the model is compatible with specialized hardware. AWS Inferentia instances are purpose-built by AWS for high-performance, low-cost deep learning inference. Compiling the model with Amazon SageMaker Neo optimizes it for these specific hardware accelerators, leading to significant cost savings and improved performance compared to general-purpose GPUs.
Option A (incorrect) uses GPU instances, which provide high performance but are generally more expensive than AWS Inferentia for inference tasks, especially when cost optimization is a key driver.
Option C (incorrect) is excellent for variable or intermittent workloads but may not provide the lowest cost or highest throughput for a consistently high-traffic, computationally intensive deep learning model, as the per-invocation cost might add up quickly compared to optimized Inferentia instances.
Option D (incorrect) involves model optimization techniques (quantization, pruning) which are good practices, but deploying to a standard CPU instance will likely not meet the high throughput and low latency requirements for a computationally intensive deep learning model as effectively as specialized hardware like Inferentia, even with optimization.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain16_3',
  NOW(),
  64,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_16_1',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_16_m_16_1_84',
  'A large media company processes vast amounts of video content daily to generate metadata using a machine learning model. The inference workload involves processing large video files, with each file taking several minutes to process. The results are not needed immediately but must be available within a few hours. The company needs a cost-effective solution that can handle a fluctuating volume of video files, scale automatically, and process requests reliably without requiring persistent compute resources.',
  'mcq',
  '["Deploy the model to an Amazon SageMaker real-time endpoint with a high instance count to handle large files and long processing times.","Implement an Amazon SageMaker Batch Transform job that runs on a fixed schedule to process all new video files.","Deploy the model to an Amazon SageMaker Asynchronous Inference endpoint.","Containerize the model and deploy it to an Amazon ECS cluster with AWS Fargate, managing a custom queue for video files."]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (correct) is the most suitable solution. Amazon SageMaker Asynchronous Inference is specifically designed for models with large payload sizes (like video files), long processing times (several minutes per file), and when clients do not need an immediate response. It automatically scales based on the queue of incoming requests, handles retries, and integrates with Amazon S3 for input/output, providing a cost-effective and reliable solution for fluctuating volumes without persistent compute.
Option A (incorrect) would be extremely expensive and inefficient for long-running, large-payload inferences. Real-time endpoints are optimized for low-latency, small-payload, interactive inference, and keeping instances warm for multi-minute processing would be cost-prohibitive.
Option B (incorrect) would require a fixed schedule, which might not be optimal for fluctuating volumes and could lead to delays if files arrive unevenly. Asynchronous Inference offers more dynamic, event-driven processing for individual requests as they arrive.
Option D (incorrect) is a viable option for containerized batch processing, but it introduces more operational overhead for managing the ECS cluster, Fargate tasks, and the custom queue compared to Amazon SageMaker Asynchronous Inference, which is a fully managed service tailored for this specific ML inference pattern.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain16_3',
  NOW(),
  65,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_16_1',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_16_m_16_1_85',
  'A startup is launching a new AI-powered chatbot service. The underlying natural language processing (NLP) model needs to be deployed for real-time inference, handling highly variable user traffic. During peak hours, thousands of requests per second are expected, while off-peak hours see minimal activity. The primary goal is to minimize operational costs by only paying for the inference capacity used, and to ensure the service remains responsive during traffic spikes without manual scaling adjustments.',
  'mcq',
  '["Deploy the NLP model to an Amazon SageMaker real-time endpoint with a provisioned instance count that can handle average peak traffic.","Deploy the NLP model to an Amazon SageMaker Serverless Inference endpoint.","Deploy the NLP model to an Amazon SageMaker real-time endpoint and configure Application Auto Scaling with aggressive scaling policies.","Deploy the NLP model to an Amazon SageMaker Asynchronous Inference endpoint to handle the variable traffic."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (correct) is the most appropriate solution. Amazon SageMaker Serverless Inference is ideal for intermittent or highly variable traffic patterns, such as a chatbot service. It automatically scales compute capacity up and down based on demand, allowing the startup to pay only for the duration of inference requests and the amount of data processed. This eliminates idle costs and the need for manual scaling adjustments, directly addressing the requirements for cost minimization and responsiveness during spikes without operational overhead.
Option A (incorrect) would lead to significant over-provisioning and high costs during low-traffic periods, failing the cost-effectiveness requirement for highly variable workloads.
Option C (incorrect) is a valid approach for real-time endpoints with variable traffic, but it still involves managing instance types and configuring auto-scaling policies. While aggressive policies can help, Serverless Inference provides a fully managed, ''pay-per-inference'' model that inherently handles scaling without explicit configuration, offering superior cost-efficiency and simplicity for highly variable, intermittent workloads.
Option D (incorrect) is designed for large payloads and long processing times where immediate responses are not required. A chatbot service typically requires low-latency, real-time responses, making Asynchronous Inference unsuitable for this use case.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain16_3',
  NOW(),
  66,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_16_1',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_16_m_16_1_86',
  'A data science team is experimenting with a new recommendation model that is very sensitive to latency. They need to deploy this model to an Amazon SageMaker real-time endpoint and ensure it can handle fluctuating traffic while maintaining strict latency requirements. To optimize costs, the endpoint should scale out during peak hours and scale in during off-peak hours automatically. The team wants to use the most efficient auto-scaling mechanism available.',
  'mcq',
  '["Deploy the model to a SageMaker real-time endpoint with a fixed instance count that can handle the maximum expected peak traffic.","Deploy the model to a SageMaker real-time endpoint and manually adjust the instance count based on observed traffic patterns.","Deploy the model to a SageMaker real-time endpoint and configure Application Auto Scaling based on target utilization metrics like invocations per instance or CPU utilization.","Deploy the model to an Amazon SageMaker Serverless Inference endpoint to handle the fluctuating traffic."]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (correct) is the recommended approach for handling fluctuating traffic on Amazon SageMaker real-time endpoints while optimizing costs and maintaining performance. Application Auto Scaling for SageMaker endpoints allows configuring scaling policies based on various metrics (e.g., invocations per instance, CPU utilization, memory utilization). This ensures that the endpoint automatically scales out during peak hours to meet demand and scales in during off-peak hours to minimize costs, without manual intervention.
Option A (incorrect) represents an anti-pattern (ap-3.1-002). Deploying with a fixed maximum instance count would lead to significant over-provisioning and high costs during low-traffic periods, failing the cost optimization requirement.
Option B (incorrect) is a manual process that is not scalable, error-prone, and cannot react quickly enough to sudden traffic fluctuations, failing the requirement for automatic scaling.
Option D (incorrect) is suitable for intermittent or highly variable traffic where latency requirements are less strict or where the ''cold start'' latency of serverless is acceptable. For models with strict, consistent low-latency requirements under fluctuating but continuous load, a real-time endpoint with well-configured auto-scaling is often preferred.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain16_3',
  NOW(),
  67,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_16_1',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_16_m_16_1_87',
  'A startup is deploying a new deep learning model for real-time image recognition. The model is computationally intensive, and the company needs to achieve the lowest possible inference cost while maintaining high throughput and low latency. They have confirmed that their model architecture is compatible with AWS''s specialized inference hardware. The solution must be fully managed.',
  'mcq',
  '["Deploy the model to an Amazon SageMaker real-time endpoint using general-purpose GPU instances (e.g., ml.g5).","Compile the model using Amazon SageMaker Neo and deploy it to an Amazon SageMaker real-time endpoint on an AWS Inferentia instance.","Deploy the model to an Amazon SageMaker Serverless Inference endpoint to benefit from pay-per-inference pricing.","Optimize the model using pruning and quantization techniques, then deploy it to a standard CPU instance (e.g., ml.c6i) on a SageMaker real-time endpoint."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (correct) is the most cost-effective and performant solution for deep learning inference when specialized hardware is an option and cost is a primary concern. AWS Inferentia instances are purpose-built by AWS for high-performance, low-cost deep learning inference. Compiling the model with Amazon SageMaker Neo optimizes it for these specific hardware accelerators, leading to significant cost savings and improved performance compared to general-purpose GPUs, while remaining a fully managed SageMaker deployment.
Option A (incorrect) uses GPU instances, which provide high performance but are generally more expensive than AWS Inferentia for inference tasks, especially when cost optimization is a key driver.
Option C (incorrect) is excellent for variable or intermittent workloads but may not provide the lowest cost or highest throughput for a consistently high-traffic, computationally intensive deep learning model, as the per-invocation cost might add up quickly compared to optimized Inferentia instances.
Option D (incorrect) involves model optimization techniques (pruning, quantization) which are good practices, but deploying to a standard CPU instance will likely not meet the high throughput and low latency requirements for a computationally intensive deep learning model as effectively as specialized hardware like Inferentia, even with optimization.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain16_3',
  NOW(),
  68,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_16_1',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_16_m_16_1_88',
  'A data science team needs to process millions of records daily for offline predictions, where low latency per individual prediction is not a strict requirement. The primary goal is cost-effectiveness and efficient processing of large datasets.',
  'mcq',
  '["Deploy the model to an Amazon SageMaker real-time inference endpoint with auto-scaling.","Utilize Amazon SageMaker Batch Transform to process the datasets.","Deploy the model to an Amazon SageMaker Serverless Inference endpoint.","Implement an Amazon SageMaker Asynchronous Inference endpoint for the model."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (correct) is the most cost-effective solution for processing large datasets for offline predictions. Amazon SageMaker Batch Transform is specifically designed for this purpose, spinning up ephemeral compute resources, processing the data, and then terminating, which is significantly cheaper than persistent endpoints for non-interactive batch workloads. Option A (incorrect) describes Amazon SageMaker real-time inference, which is designed for interactive, low-latency predictions and is costly for large batch jobs, representing anti-pattern ap-3.1-001. Option C (incorrect) describes Amazon SageMaker Serverless Inference, which is suitable for infrequent or bursty real-time workloads, but not optimized for large, continuous batch processing. Option D (incorrect) describes Amazon SageMaker Asynchronous Inference, which is ideal for large payload sizes or long processing times for individual requests, but Batch Transform is better suited for processing entire large datasets.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain16_3',
  NOW(),
  69,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_16_1',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_16_m_16_1_89',
  'A machine learning model deployed on an Amazon SageMaker real-time endpoint experiences highly variable inference traffic throughout the day, with significant peaks and troughs. The operations team wants to ensure high availability during peak times while minimizing costs during off-peak hours.',
  'mcq',
  '["Manually adjust the number of instances for the SageMaker endpoint based on traffic forecasts.","Configure Application Auto Scaling for the Amazon SageMaker endpoint based on utilization metrics.","Deploy the model to a fixed number of Amazon SageMaker real-time instances to handle peak load.","Migrate the model to an Amazon SageMaker Serverless Inference endpoint to handle variable traffic."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (correct) is the most effective approach. Application Auto Scaling for Amazon SageMaker endpoints automatically adjusts the number of instances based on predefined metrics (like CPU utilization or invocation count), ensuring high availability during peak traffic and reducing costs during low traffic periods. Option A (incorrect) involves manual intervention, which is inefficient, prone to errors, and does not react dynamically to sudden traffic changes. Option C (incorrect) describes deploying a fixed number of instances, which leads to over-provisioning and higher costs during low traffic, and potential under-provisioning and availability issues during unexpected spikes, representing anti-pattern ap-3.1-002. Option D (incorrect) suggests Amazon SageMaker Serverless Inference, which is suitable for infrequent or bursty workloads, but for an existing real-time endpoint with predictable variable patterns, auto-scaling is the direct solution for cost and availability.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain16_3',
  NOW(),
  70,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_16_1',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_16_m_16_1_90',
  'A company has deployed several deep learning models for image classification that require high-throughput inference at the lowest possible cost. These models are already optimized for deployment.',
  'mcq',
  '["Deploy the models to Amazon SageMaker endpoints using GPU instances.","Deploy the models to Amazon SageMaker endpoints using AWS Inferentia instances.","Deploy the models to Amazon SageMaker endpoints using CPU instances.","Compile the models using Amazon SageMaker Neo and deploy to standard CPU instances."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (correct) is the most cost-effective solution for high-throughput deep learning inference. AWS Inferentia instances are purpose-built by AWS for high-performance, cost-optimized machine learning inference. Option A (incorrect) involves GPU instances, which offer high performance but are generally more expensive than Inferentia for inference tasks. Option C (incorrect) suggests CPU instances, which are typically less performant and less cost-effective than specialized hardware like Inferentia for deep learning inference. Option D (incorrect) describes Amazon SageMaker Neo, which optimizes models for various hardware targets, but AWS Inferentia is the specific hardware designed for the lowest cost at high throughput for deep learning inference.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain16_3',
  NOW(),
  71,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_16_1',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

