) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_16_m_16_3_59',
  'What is the purpose of using Amazon SageMaker Model Registry in an MLOps pipeline?',
  'mcq',
  '["To maintain a centralized repository for model versions and their metadata.","To store large datasets used for training and inference.","To monitor the health and performance of deployed models.","To provide a collaborative environment for data scientists."]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A (To maintain a centralized repository for model versions and their metadata) is the core purpose of Amazon SageMaker Model Registry. It enables versioning, tracking, and managing the lifecycle of machine learning models, including approval workflows for deployment. Option B describes services like Amazon S3. Option C describes SageMaker Model Monitor. Option D describes SageMaker Studio.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain16_3',
  NOW(),
  81,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_16_3',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_16_m_16_3_60',
  'Which AWS service is used to automate the entire software release process, from code commit to deployment, for machine learning applications?',
  'mcq',
  '["AWS CodePipeline","AWS CodeBuild","Amazon EventBridge","Amazon SageMaker Pipelines"]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A (AWS CodePipeline) is the fully managed continuous delivery service that automates release pipelines. It orchestrates the steps from source code changes through building, testing, and deploying, making it suitable for automating the entire release process for ML applications. Option B (AWS CodeBuild) is for building and testing code. Option C (Amazon EventBridge) is an event bus for event-driven architectures. Option D (Amazon SageMaker Pipelines) orchestrates ML-specific workflows, often integrated within a broader CodePipeline.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain16_3',
  NOW(),
  82,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_16_3',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_16_m_16_3_61',
  'Which AWS service provides event-driven capabilities to trigger Amazon SageMaker Pipelines based on events like data updates in Amazon S3 or a specific time schedule?',
  'mcq',
  '["Amazon EventBridge","AWS Step Functions","Amazon SNS","Amazon SQS"]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A (Amazon EventBridge) is the correct service. EventBridge is a serverless event bus that can receive events from various sources (including Amazon S3, schedules, or custom applications) and route them to targets like Amazon SageMaker Pipelines to trigger automated workflows. Option B (AWS Step Functions) orchestrates workflows but is a more general-purpose state machine. Option C (Amazon SNS) is a messaging service for publishing messages to subscribers. Option D (Amazon SQS) is a message queuing service.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain16_3',
  NOW(),
  83,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_16_3',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_16_m_16_3_62',
  'A machine learning model''s performance is known to degrade over time due to changes in the underlying data distribution. The team wants to automatically retrain the model whenever significant changes in the input data characteristics are detected. Which trigger strategy for automated retraining should be implemented?',
  'mcq',
  '["Scheduled retraining at fixed intervals","Manual retraining initiated by data scientists","Data drift detection triggering retraining","Performance degradation monitoring triggering retraining"]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (Data drift detection triggering retraining) is the correct strategy. When a model''s performance degrades due to changes in the input data distribution (data drift), automatically triggering retraining based on detected drift ensures the model remains relevant and accurate. Option A (Scheduled retraining) is useful for regular updates but might miss sudden data shifts. Option B (Manual retraining) is not automated. Option D (Performance degradation monitoring triggering retraining) is also a valid strategy, but the question specifically mentions ''changes in the input data characteristics'' which points more directly to data drift.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain16_3',
  NOW(),
  84,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_16_3',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_16_m_16_3_63',
  'Which deployment strategy for machine learning endpoints involves running two identical production environments, where traffic is switched entirely from the old version to the new version only after the new version is fully validated?',
  'mcq',
  '["Canary release","A/B testing","Blue/green deployment","Shadow deployment"]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (Blue/green deployment) is the correct strategy. In a blue/green deployment, two identical environments (blue for the old version, green for the new) run simultaneously. Traffic is entirely shifted to the green environment only after it has been thoroughly validated, allowing for quick rollback to the blue environment if issues arise. Option A (Canary release) involves gradually shifting a small percentage of traffic. Option B (A/B testing) compares two versions of a model or feature to see which performs better. Option D (Shadow deployment) sends production traffic to a new model without affecting users, primarily for testing.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain16_3',
  NOW(),
  85,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_16_3',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_16_m_16_3_64',
  'A machine learning team wants to automatically trigger an Amazon SageMaker Pipeline whenever new training data files are uploaded to a specific Amazon S3 bucket. Which AWS service can be used to set up this event-driven trigger?',
  'mcq',
  '["AWS CodeBuild","Amazon EventBridge","AWS Lambda","Amazon SQS"]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (Amazon EventBridge) is the correct service. Amazon EventBridge is a serverless event bus that makes it easy to connect applications together using data from your own applications, integrated SaaS applications, and AWS services. It can be configured to detect S3 object creation events and then trigger an Amazon SageMaker Pipeline execution. Option A (AWS CodeBuild) is for continuous integration. Option C (AWS Lambda) could be used, but EventBridge provides a more direct and managed way to route events. Option D (Amazon SQS) is a message queuing service, not an event bus for triggering pipelines directly from S3 events.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain16_3',
  NOW(),
  86,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_16_3',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_16_m_16_3_65',
  'What is the primary benefit of using a canary release strategy when deploying a new version of an Amazon SageMaker model endpoint?',
  'mcq',
  '["It allows for immediate rollback to the previous version if issues occur.","It ensures 100% of traffic is routed to the new model instantly.","It reduces the risk of widespread production issues by gradually rolling out the new model to a small subset of users.","It completely isolates the new model from production traffic for extended testing."]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (It reduces the risk of widespread production issues by gradually rolling out the new model to a small subset of users) is the primary benefit of a canary release. This strategy involves directing a small percentage of live traffic to the new model version, allowing for real-world testing and monitoring before a full rollout. This helps detect issues early and prevents widespread impact (addressing anti-pattern ap-3.3-001). Option A is a benefit of blue/green deployments, though canary also allows rollback. Option B describes a direct 100% deployment, which is risky. Option D describes a shadow deployment.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain16_3',
  NOW(),
  87,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_16_3',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_16_m_16_3_66',
  'A machine learning team wants to automate model deployment but only if the new model''s performance, as measured by a specific metric (e.g., accuracy), exceeds a predefined threshold during evaluation. Which feature of Amazon SageMaker Pipelines allows for this conditional execution?',
  'mcq',
  '["Pipeline parameters","Pipeline steps","Pipeline conditions","Pipeline triggers"]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (Pipeline conditions) is the correct feature. Amazon SageMaker Pipelines allows you to define conditional steps, where subsequent steps (like model registration or deployment) are only executed if certain conditions, such as a model''s evaluation metric meeting a threshold, are met. Option A (Pipeline parameters) are for passing input values to a pipeline. Option B (Pipeline steps) define the individual tasks within the workflow. Option D (Pipeline triggers) initiate the pipeline execution.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain16_3',
  NOW(),
  88,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_16_3',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_16_m_16_3_67',
  'A machine learning model is used for seasonal forecasting and needs to be retrained every quarter to incorporate the latest market trends. Which trigger strategy for automated retraining should be configured to ensure the model is updated regularly?',
  'mcq',
  '["Data drift detection","Performance degradation","Scheduled retraining","Manual retraining"]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (Scheduled retraining) is the correct strategy. For models that require regular updates at fixed intervals, such as quarterly, scheduled retraining ensures that the model is automatically retrained on new data at the specified frequency. Option A (Data drift detection) and Option B (Performance degradation) are reactive strategies based on observed changes. Option D (Manual retraining) is not an automated approach.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain16_3',
  NOW(),
  89,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_16_3',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_16_m_16_3_68',
  'During a blue/green deployment of an Amazon SageMaker model endpoint, the new ''green'' environment is found to have a critical bug after traffic has been partially shifted. What is a key advantage of the blue/green strategy in this situation?',
  'mcq',
  '["It allows for immediate rollback to the stable ''blue'' environment with minimal impact.","It automatically fixes the bug in the ''green'' environment.","It ensures that all traffic is routed to the ''green'' environment regardless of issues.","It prevents any traffic from ever reaching the ''green'' environment."]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A (It allows for immediate rollback to the stable ''blue'' environment with minimal impact) is a key advantage. In a blue/green deployment, the old ''blue'' environment remains active and ready to receive 100% of the traffic. If issues are detected in the new ''green'' environment, traffic can be instantly switched back to the stable ''blue'' environment, minimizing downtime and user impact. Option B is incorrect; blue/green doesn''t fix bugs automatically. Option C is incorrect as it defeats the purpose of safe deployment. Option D is incorrect as the goal is to eventually shift traffic to ''green''.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain16_3',
  NOW(),
  90,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_16_3',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_16_m_16_3_69',
  'A machine learning engineer is designing an Amazon SageMaker Pipeline that needs to be reusable for different datasets without modifying the pipeline definition. How can the engineer achieve this flexibility?',
  'mcq',
  '["By hardcoding dataset paths within the pipeline steps.","By defining pipeline parameters that can be passed at runtime.","By creating a separate pipeline for each dataset.","By using AWS Lambda to dynamically generate pipeline definitions."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (By defining pipeline parameters that can be passed at runtime) is the correct approach. Amazon SageMaker Pipelines support parameters, allowing you to define variables (e.g., input S3 bucket, output prefix) that can be provided when a pipeline execution is started. This makes the pipeline definition generic and reusable across different datasets or configurations. Option A (By hardcoding dataset paths) makes the pipeline inflexible. Option C (By creating a separate pipeline for each dataset) increases operational overhead and violates the principle of reusability. Option D (By using AWS Lambda to dynamically generate pipeline definitions) is overly complex and unnecessary, as parameters provide the required functionality.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain16_3',
  NOW(),
  91,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_16_3',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_16_m_16_3_70',
  'A fraud detection model''s accuracy is critical, and the team wants to automatically retrain it if its performance metrics (e.g., F1-score) fall below a certain threshold in production. Which trigger strategy for automated retraining should be implemented?',
  'mcq',
  '["Scheduled retraining","Data drift detection","Performance degradation monitoring","Manual retraining"]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (Performance degradation monitoring) is the correct strategy. Implementing a system to monitor the model''s performance metrics (like accuracy or F1-score) in production and automatically triggering a retraining pipeline when these metrics fall below a predefined threshold ensures the model maintains its effectiveness. Option A (Scheduled retraining) is time-based. Option B (Data drift detection) focuses on input data changes, not directly model output performance. Option D (Manual retraining) is not an automated solution.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain16_3',
  NOW(),
  92,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_16_3',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_16_m_16_3_71',
  'A machine learning team is deploying a new version of a critical model to an Amazon SageMaker endpoint. To mitigate the risk of introducing regressions, they want to test the new model with a small percentage of live traffic before fully committing to the deployment. Which deployment strategy should they use?',
  'mcq',
  '["Blue/green deployment","Direct deployment to 100% traffic","Canary testing","Shadow deployment"]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (Canary testing) is the correct deployment strategy. Canary testing involves gradually routing a small percentage of live traffic to the new model version while the majority of traffic still goes to the old, stable version. This allows the team to monitor the new model''s performance with real-world data and quickly roll back if issues are detected, minimizing the impact of potential regressions (addressing anti-pattern ap-3.3-001). Option A (Blue/green deployment) shifts all traffic at once after validation. Option B (Direct deployment to 100% traffic) is risky and does not mitigate regressions. Option D (Shadow deployment) sends traffic to the new model for testing but does not serve predictions to users.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain16_3',
  NOW(),
  93,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_16_3',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_16_m_16_3_72',
  'A machine learning team is developing a new customer churn prediction model. They want to implement a continuous integration and continuous delivery (CI/CD) pipeline that automatically retrains the model when new data is available, evaluates its performance, and deploys it to a staging environment. The pipeline needs to be triggered by changes in the model''s source code in AWS CodeCommit or by a scheduled event. The entire process should be automated and trackable.',
  'mcq',
  '["Manually run training scripts from a SageMaker Notebook instance and deploy models using the SageMaker SDK.","Use AWS CodePipeline to orchestrate the ML workflow, integrating AWS CodeBuild for training and evaluation, and Amazon SageMaker for deployment.","Set up an Amazon EventBridge rule to trigger an AWS Lambda function that performs all ML pipeline steps.","Create a custom Jenkins server on Amazon EC2 to manage the CI/CD process for ML models."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (correct) is the recommended approach for implementing CI/CD for ML (MLOps). AWS CodePipeline can orchestrate the entire ML workflow, with AWS CodeCommit as the source. AWS CodeBuild can be used to execute training scripts, evaluate models, and prepare artifacts. Finally, Amazon SageMaker can be used for model deployment. This combination provides a fully automated, trackable, and version-controlled pipeline, triggered by code changes or schedules, aligning with the requirements.
Option A (incorrect) is a manual process that lacks automation, version control, and reproducibility, making it unsuitable for a CI/CD pipeline.
Option C (incorrect) could orchestrate simple tasks, but a single AWS Lambda function would become overly complex and difficult to manage for an entire ML pipeline involving training, evaluation, and deployment. CodePipeline and CodeBuild are designed for multi-stage CI/CD workflows.
Option D (incorrect) introduces significant operational overhead for managing a Jenkins server on EC2, including patching, scaling, and maintenance. AWS CodePipeline and CodeBuild are fully managed services that provide similar CI/CD capabilities without the operational burden.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain16_3',
  NOW(),
  94,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_16_3',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_16_m_16_3_73',
  'A manufacturing plant relies on a machine learning model for anomaly detection in real-time sensor data. The model''s performance can degrade over time due to changes in equipment behavior (data drift). The MLOps team needs to set up an automated retraining pipeline that is triggered when significant data drift is detected in the incoming sensor data, ensuring the model remains accurate and effective without constant manual monitoring. The retraining process should be fully automated.',
  'mcq',
  '["Schedule the model retraining to run weekly, regardless of data drift.","Manually monitor data quality metrics and trigger retraining jobs when drift is observed.","Configure Amazon EventBridge to trigger an Amazon SageMaker Pipeline when a data drift detection service publishes an event.","Implement a custom script on an Amazon EC2 instance that continuously analyzes data for drift and initiates retraining."]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (correct) is the most effective and automated solution. By configuring Amazon EventBridge to listen for events published by a data drift detection service (e.g., SageMaker Model Monitor or a custom solution), an Amazon SageMaker Pipeline can be automatically triggered. This pipeline would then orchestrate the data preprocessing, model retraining, and deployment steps, ensuring that the model is retrained only when necessary due to actual data drift, aligning with the requirement for automated, event-driven retraining.
Option A (incorrect) is a scheduled retraining strategy, which is valid, but it might lead to unnecessary retraining if no drift occurs, or delayed retraining if drift occurs before the scheduled time, failing the requirement for ''when significant data drift is detected''.
Option B (incorrect) is a manual approach that is not scalable, error-prone, and contradicts the goal of an automated retraining pipeline.
Option D (incorrect) would introduce significant operational overhead for managing a custom EC2 instance and script, including monitoring, scaling, and maintenance. Amazon EventBridge and SageMaker Pipelines provide a fully managed and integrated solution for event-driven ML workflows.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain16_3',
  NOW(),
  95,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_16_3',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_16_m_16_3_74',
  'A company is deploying a new version of its recommendation model to an Amazon SageMaker real-time endpoint. The new model has undergone extensive offline testing, but the team wants to minimize the risk of negative customer experience in production. They decide to route only 5% of live inference requests to the new model for a few hours, collect metrics, and then gradually increase traffic to 100% if performance is satisfactory. This approach requires minimal downtime and an easy rollback mechanism.',
  'mcq',
  '["Perform an in-place update of the existing SageMaker endpoint with the new model, immediately switching all traffic.","Create a completely new SageMaker endpoint for the new model and manually update application configurations to point to it.","Utilize SageMaker''s built-in traffic shifting capabilities by updating the endpoint configuration to include the new model variant with a weight of 5%.","Deploy the new model to an Amazon SageMaker Serverless Inference endpoint and use AWS Lambda functions to manually split traffic."]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (correct) is the most effective and managed way to perform a canary release or gradual rollout on Amazon SageMaker. SageMaker real-time endpoints support updating the endpoint configuration to include multiple model variants with specified traffic weights. This allows routing a small percentage of live traffic (e.g., 5%) to the new model, monitoring its performance, and then gradually shifting more traffic. This minimizes risk, ensures minimal downtime, and provides an easy rollback by adjusting weights.
Option A (incorrect) represents an anti-pattern (ap-3.3-001). Immediately switching 100% of traffic to a new model without gradual testing carries a high risk of production issues and negative customer experience.
Option B (incorrect) involves creating a new endpoint and manually updating application configurations, which is more complex to manage for gradual traffic shifting and rollback compared to SageMaker''s native capabilities.
Option D (incorrect) would introduce unnecessary complexity and operational overhead. While Serverless Inference supports traffic splitting, using Lambda functions to manually split traffic adds an extra layer of custom logic when SageMaker''s real-time endpoints already provide this functionality natively and in a managed way.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain16_3',
  NOW(),
  96,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_16_3',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_16_m_16_3_75',
  'A large retail company is deploying a new product recommendation model. This model is critical for customer engagement, and its performance must be continuously monitored. The MLOps team wants to implement a strategy to detect performance degradation (e.g., declining accuracy or increasing latency) in the production model and automatically trigger a retraining and redeployment workflow. This should happen without manual intervention.',
  'mcq',
  '["Schedule the model retraining to occur every month, regardless of performance.","Manually monitor model metrics in Amazon CloudWatch and trigger retraining if degradation is observed.","Configure Amazon EventBridge to trigger an Amazon SageMaker Pipeline when Amazon SageMaker Model Monitor detects performance degradation.","Implement a custom cron job on an Amazon EC2 instance to periodically run model evaluation and trigger retraining via the AWS SDK."]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (correct) is the most effective and automated solution for continuous retraining based on performance degradation. Amazon SageMaker Model Monitor can continuously evaluate the performance of a production model and detect issues like data drift or model quality degradation. When such degradation is detected, Model Monitor can publish events to Amazon EventBridge, which can then be configured to trigger an Amazon SageMaker Pipeline. This pipeline would automate the retraining, evaluation, and redeployment of the model, ensuring the model remains accurate and effective without manual intervention.
Option A (incorrect) is a scheduled retraining strategy that might not address performance degradation promptly if it occurs before the scheduled interval, or it might retrain unnecessarily if performance is stable.
Option B (incorrect) is a manual approach that is not scalable, error-prone, and contradicts the goal of an automated MLOps pipeline.
Option D (incorrect) would introduce significant operational overhead for managing a custom EC2 instance and script, including monitoring, scaling, and maintenance. Amazon EventBridge and SageMaker Pipelines, combined with SageMaker Model Monitor, provide a fully managed and integrated solution for event-driven MLOps.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain16_3',
  NOW(),
  97,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_16_3',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_16_m_16_3_76',
  'A data science team requires an AWS-native service to orchestrate their end-to-end machine learning workflows, including data preparation, model training, evaluation, and conditional model registration.',
  'mcq',
  '["Use AWS CodePipeline to manage the ML workflow stages.","Implement the workflow using Amazon SageMaker Pipelines.","Configure Amazon EventBridge rules to trigger sequential Lambda functions.","Manually trigger each step of the ML workflow as needed."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (correct) is the most suitable AWS-native service for orchestrating end-to-end machine learning workflows. Amazon SageMaker Pipelines allows defining, managing, and automating all steps of an ML workflow, including data processing, training, model evaluation, and conditional model registration, with built-in ML-specific features. Option A (incorrect) describes AWS CodePipeline, which is a general-purpose CI/CD service for software releases, not specifically optimized for the unique requirements and steps of ML workflows. Option C (incorrect) involves using Amazon EventBridge and AWS Lambda, which can be used for event-driven automation, but building a complex, stateful ML workflow orchestrator with these services would require significant custom development compared to SageMaker Pipelines. Option D (incorrect) is a manual approach that lacks automation, repeatability, and observability, which is inefficient for continuous ML operations.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain16_3',
  NOW(),
  98,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_16_3',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_16_m_16_3_77',
  'A new version of a critical machine learning model is ready for deployment to a production Amazon SageMaker endpoint. The team needs to minimize the risk of service disruption or performance degradation during the rollout.',
  'mcq',
  '["Deploy the new model directly to 100% of the production traffic.","Implement a canary release strategy by routing a small percentage of traffic to the new model version first.","Perform A/B testing on the new model to compare its performance against the old model.","Manually monitor the endpoint metrics closely after the full deployment of the new model."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (correct) is the most effective strategy to minimize risk during a model rollout. A canary release gradually shifts a small percentage of production traffic to the new model version, allowing the team to monitor its performance and stability with real-world data before fully promoting it. This helps detect issues early and prevent widespread disruption. Option A (incorrect) describes deploying the new model directly to 100% of traffic, which carries a high risk of production outages if the new model has regressions or unexpected behavior, embodying anti-pattern ap-3.3-001. Option C (incorrect) describes A/B testing, which is used for comparing the business impact or performance of different models, not primarily for minimizing deployment risk of a single new version. Option D (incorrect) involves reactive monitoring after a full deployment, which means issues would already be impacting all users, rather than proactively mitigating risk during the rollout.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain16_3',
  NOW(),
  99,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_16_3',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_16_m_16_3_78',
  'A machine learning pipeline needs to be automatically triggered whenever new training data is uploaded to a specific Amazon S3 bucket.',
  'mcq',
  '["Periodically poll the Amazon S3 bucket using an AWS Lambda function to check for new data.","Manually trigger the Amazon SageMaker Pipeline each time new data is uploaded to Amazon S3.","Configure an Amazon EventBridge rule to detect S3 object creation events and trigger the SageMaker Pipeline.","Use AWS CodePipeline to monitor the Amazon S3 bucket for new data and initiate the pipeline."]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (correct) is the most efficient and AWS-native way to achieve event-driven pipeline triggering. Amazon EventBridge can be configured to listen for specific events, such as ''Object Created'' events in an Amazon S3 bucket, and then invoke a target, like an Amazon SageMaker Pipeline, to start its execution. Option A (incorrect) describes a polling mechanism, which is less efficient, can incur higher costs, and introduces latency compared to an event-driven approach. Option B (incorrect) is a manual process, which is not automated and does not meet the requirement for automatic triggering. Option D (incorrect) describes AWS CodePipeline, which is primarily used for continuous integration and continuous delivery of code changes, not typically for triggering ML pipelines based on data uploads to S3.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain16_3',
  NOW(),
  100,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_16_3',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

