INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_17_m_17_2_52',
  'What is the primary purpose of Amazon CloudWatch in monitoring AWS resources?',
  'mcq',
  '["Collect and track metrics, collect and monitor log files, and set alarms","Store large datasets for analytics","Deploy serverless applications","Manage user permissions and access"]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A (correct) accurately describes the primary purpose of Amazon CloudWatch, which is to collect and track metrics, collect and monitor log files, and set alarms for AWS resources and applications. Option B is incorrect; Amazon S3 or Amazon RDS are used for storing large datasets. Option C is incorrect; AWS Lambda or AWS Fargate are used for deploying serverless applications. Option D is incorrect; AWS IAM is used for managing user permissions and access.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain17_3',
  NOW(),
  1,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_17_2',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_17_m_17_2_53',
  'Which AWS service helps optimize the cost of Amazon SageMaker inference endpoints by recommending instance types?',
  'mcq',
  '["AWS Lambda","Amazon SageMaker Inference Recommender","AWS Cost Explorer","Amazon CloudWatch"]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (correct) is Amazon SageMaker Inference Recommender, which helps optimize the cost and performance of inference endpoints by recommending the best instance types and configurations. Option A, AWS Lambda, is a serverless compute service. Option C, AWS Cost Explorer, is for visualizing and managing AWS costs, but doesn''t provide instance recommendations for SageMaker inference. Option D, Amazon CloudWatch, is a monitoring service.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain17_3',
  NOW(),
  2,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_17_2',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_17_m_17_2_54',
  'What type of metrics does Amazon CloudWatch provide for Amazon SageMaker endpoints?',
  'mcq',
  '["Latency, throughput, and error rates","Model accuracy and bias drift","Data quality and feature attribution drift","Training job completion status"]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A (correct) lists the key operational metrics that Amazon CloudWatch provides for Amazon SageMaker endpoints, such as Latency, Throughput (Invocations), and Error rates (InvocationErrors). Options B and C describe metrics related to model quality and data drift, which are typically monitored by Amazon SageMaker Model Monitor, not directly by CloudWatch. Option D is a status, not a continuous metric provided by CloudWatch for endpoints.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain17_3',
  NOW(),
  3,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_17_2',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_17_m_17_2_55',
  'Which Amazon CloudWatch metric provides insight into the number of invocations per unit of time for an Amazon SageMaker endpoint?',
  'mcq',
  '["Invocations","Latency","ModelLatency","CPUUtilization"]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A (Invocations) is the correct Amazon CloudWatch metric that tracks the number of times an Amazon SageMaker endpoint is invoked per unit of time, providing insight into its throughput. Option B (Latency) measures the time taken for requests. Option C (ModelLatency) specifically measures the time spent by the model itself, excluding network and other overhead. Option D (CPUUtilization) is an infrastructure metric, not directly related to the number of invocations.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain17_3',
  NOW(),
  4,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_17_2',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_17_m_17_2_56',
  'What is a primary benefit of right-sizing Amazon SageMaker instances for inference endpoints?',
  'mcq',
  '["Increased model accuracy","Reduced operational costs","Improved data quality","Simplified model deployment"]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (Reduced operational costs) is the primary benefit of right-sizing Amazon SageMaker instances. By selecting instance types and sizes that precisely match the workload requirements, organizations can avoid over-provisioning resources, which directly translates to lower compute expenses. Option A (Increased model accuracy) is determined by the model training process, not instance sizing. Option C (Improved data quality) is related to data preprocessing, not inference instance sizing. Option D (Simplified model deployment) is a general benefit of SageMaker, but right-sizing specifically addresses cost and performance efficiency.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain17_3',
  NOW(),
  5,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_17_2',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_17_m_17_2_57',
  'A data science team is deploying a critical credit risk model on Amazon SageMaker. They need to proactively detect if the statistical properties of the incoming inference data deviate significantly from the data used to train the model, which could lead to degraded model performance. Which specific SageMaker Model Monitor capability should be configured to address this requirement?',
  'mcq',
  '["Data Quality Monitor","Model Quality Monitor","Bias Drift Monitor","Feature Attribution Drift Monitor"]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A (correct) is the Data Quality Monitor, which is specifically designed to detect deviations in the statistical properties of the input data used for inference compared to the baseline training data. This directly addresses the requirement to identify shifts in incoming inference data. Option B (Model Quality Monitor) tracks the actual performance metrics of the model, such as accuracy or F1-score, against a baseline, which is useful for overall model performance but doesn''t specifically target input data characteristics. Option C (Bias Drift Monitor) focuses on detecting changes in model bias over time. Option D (Feature Attribution Drift Monitor) identifies shifts in how features contribute to predictions, rather than the raw data characteristics themselves.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain17_3',
  NOW(),
  6,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_17_2',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_17_m_17_2_58',
  'A company has deployed several real-time inference endpoints using Amazon SageMaker. The operations team needs to track the time taken for the endpoint to process a single request and identify any slowdowns that might impact user experience. Which Amazon CloudWatch metric for SageMaker endpoints should they primarily monitor to achieve this?',
  'mcq',
  '["Invocations","ModelLatency","CPUUtilization","MemoryUtilization"]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (correct) is ModelLatency, which measures the time taken by the model to process a single inference request. This metric directly addresses the need to track slowdowns and impact on user experience. Option A (Invocations) tracks the total number of requests made to the endpoint, which indicates throughput but not individual request processing time. Option C (CPUUtilization) and Option D (MemoryUtilization) are infrastructure metrics that monitor the underlying compute resources, which are important for overall health but do not directly reflect the model''s processing time for a request. An endpoint can have high CPU utilization without necessarily having high model latency if it''s processing many requests efficiently.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain17_3',
  NOW(),
  7,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_17_2',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_17_m_17_2_59',
  'A machine learning model deployed in production is experiencing a gradual decline in accuracy. Analysis reveals that the relationship between the input features and the target variable has fundamentally changed over time, making the existing model''s learned patterns obsolete. What type of model drift is this scenario describing?',
  'mcq',
  '["Input Data Drift","Feature Skew","Concept Drift","Prediction Drift"]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (correct) describes Concept Drift, which occurs when the underlying relationship between the input features and the target variable changes. This means the ''concept'' the model is trying to learn has evolved. Option A (Input Data Drift), often referred to as Data Drift, refers to changes in the statistical properties of the input data itself, but not necessarily the relationship with the target. Option B (Feature Skew) is a specific type of data drift where the distribution of a particular feature changes. Option D (Prediction Drift) refers to changes in the distribution of the model''s output predictions, which can be a symptom of concept or data drift, but not the root cause itself.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain17_3',
  NOW(),
  8,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_17_2',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_17_m_17_2_60',
  'A data science team wants to implement an automated system to detect model quality degradation for their Amazon SageMaker endpoint and immediately notify relevant stakeholders. They need to trigger an alert when the model''s accuracy falls below a predefined threshold. Which combination of AWS services should they use to achieve this with minimal custom development?',
  'mcq',
  '["AWS Lambda and Amazon SNS","Amazon Kinesis and Amazon SQS","Amazon CloudWatch Alarms integrated with Amazon SageMaker Model Monitor","AWS CloudTrail and Amazon EventBridge"]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (correct) is the most direct and integrated solution. Amazon SageMaker Model Monitor can be configured to track model quality metrics (like accuracy) and detect when they fall below a threshold. It can then publish these findings to Amazon CloudWatch, where Amazon CloudWatch Alarms can be set up to trigger notifications (e.g., via Amazon SNS) when the alarm state is reached. This provides automated detection and alerting with minimal custom development. Option A (AWS Lambda and Amazon SNS) could be used for custom alerting, but would require Lambda to poll or process data to detect the degradation, adding complexity. Option B (Amazon Kinesis and Amazon SQS) are streaming and messaging services, not directly designed for automated model quality monitoring and alerting. Option D (AWS CloudTrail and Amazon EventBridge) are primarily for auditing API calls and routing events, not for continuous monitoring of model quality metrics.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain17_3',
  NOW(),
  9,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_17_2',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_17_m_17_2_61',
  'A machine learning team manages a fraud detection model in production. To maintain model performance, they currently retrain the model on a fixed quarterly schedule. However, they''ve observed that fraud patterns can shift rapidly and unpredictably, causing significant drops in model accuracy between scheduled retraining cycles. Which retraining strategy, combined with their existing schedule, would best address this challenge?',
  'mcq',
  '["Triggered retraining on drift detection","Manual ad-hoc retraining","Increasing the scheduled retraining frequency to monthly","Implementing A/B testing for new models"]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A (correct) is triggered retraining on drift detection. This strategy directly addresses the problem of unpredictable shifts by initiating retraining only when a significant change (drift) in data or model performance is detected by monitoring tools like Amazon SageMaker Model Monitor. This complements scheduled retraining by providing a responsive mechanism. Option B (Manual ad-hoc retraining) is reactive and requires human intervention, which is not ideal for rapid, unpredictable shifts. Option C (Increasing the scheduled retraining frequency to monthly) might help but could still miss rapid, unpredictable shifts and increases compute costs unnecessarily if drift hasn''t occurred. Option D (Implementing A/B testing for new models) is a deployment strategy for evaluating new models, not a retraining strategy to address drift in an existing model.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain17_3',
  NOW(),
  10,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_17_2',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_17_m_17_2_62',
  'A data science team is deploying a new image classification model on Amazon SageMaker endpoints. They anticipate varying inference loads throughout the day, with peak times requiring higher capacity. To optimize costs, they want to ensure the endpoint scales up and down automatically based on demand, without over-provisioning. Which cost optimization strategy should they implement?',
  'mcq',
  '["Purchasing Reserved Instances for the endpoint","Implementing auto-scaling for the SageMaker endpoint","Using Amazon EC2 Spot Instances for the endpoint","Manually adjusting instance counts based on historical data"]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (correct) is implementing auto-scaling for the SageMaker endpoint. Amazon SageMaker supports auto-scaling for real-time inference endpoints, allowing the number of instances to automatically adjust based on predefined metrics (e.g., CPU utilization, invocation per instance). This ensures capacity matches demand, optimizing costs by avoiding over-provisioning during low-demand periods. Option A (Purchasing Reserved Instances) is suitable for predictable, sustained workloads but not for highly variable loads where capacity needs to fluctuate. Option C (Using Amazon EC2 Spot Instances) is generally not recommended for real-time inference endpoints due to potential interruptions, which can impact availability and user experience, although SageMaker supports it for asynchronous inference. Option D (Manually adjusting instance counts) is reactive, prone to human error, and does not provide the dynamic responsiveness needed for varying loads.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain17_3',
  NOW(),
  11,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_17_2',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_17_m_17_2_63',
  'A machine learning engineer needs to run a large batch of non-critical data processing jobs for feature engineering on Amazon SageMaker Processing. These jobs are fault-tolerant and can tolerate interruptions, but they are very compute-intensive. What is the MOST cost-effective instance purchasing option for these types of workloads?',
  'mcq',
  '["On-Demand Instances","Spot Instances","Reserved Instances","Dedicated Hosts"]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (correct) is Spot Instances. For fault-tolerant, non-critical workloads like batch processing or feature engineering that can tolerate interruptions, Amazon EC2 Spot Instances offer significant cost savings (up to 90% compared to On-Demand prices). SageMaker Processing jobs can leverage Spot Instances effectively. Option A (On-Demand Instances) are more expensive but provide guaranteed capacity and are suitable for critical, uninterrupted workloads. Option C (Reserved Instances) offer discounts for long-term commitments but are less flexible for intermittent or variable workloads and do not provide the same level of cost savings as Spot for interruptible jobs. Option D (Dedicated Hosts) provide dedicated physical servers and are typically used for specific licensing requirements or compliance, not primarily for cost optimization of flexible ML workloads.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain17_3',
  NOW(),
  12,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_17_2',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_17_m_17_2_64',
  'A retail company uses an Amazon SageMaker endpoint for real-time product recommendations. The data science team wants to ensure the model''s recommendations remain relevant as customer preferences evolve. They need a solution that automatically detects significant changes in customer behavior patterns and triggers a model retraining workflow, while minimizing manual intervention and ensuring the model is always up-to-date. Which approach provides the LEAST operational overhead?',
  'mcq',
  '["Schedule daily retraining jobs regardless of model performance.","Manually review model performance metrics weekly and trigger retraining if needed.","Set up Amazon CloudWatch alarms on infrastructure metrics (CPU, memory) to trigger retraining.","Configure Amazon SageMaker Model Monitor to detect data drift and integrate with Amazon CloudWatch Alarms to trigger an AWS Lambda function for retraining."]'::json,
  '{3}',
  'Option D (correct) provides the least operational overhead and is the most effective. Amazon SageMaker Model Monitor can continuously analyze inference data for data drift, which indicates changes in customer behavior. Integrating Model Monitor with Amazon CloudWatch Alarms allows for automated alerts when drift is detected. These alarms can then trigger an AWS Lambda function to initiate a SageMaker retraining job, creating a fully automated, event-driven retraining pipeline. Option A (Schedule daily retraining jobs) is inefficient and costly, as retraining might not always be necessary (anti-pattern ap-4.2-001). Option B (Manually review model performance) is reactive and high operational overhead. Option C (Set up Amazon CloudWatch alarms on infrastructure metrics) is an anti-pattern (ap-4.1-001) because infrastructure metrics do not indicate model performance or data relevance; an endpoint can be healthy while the model''s recommendations degrade.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain17_3',
  NOW(),
  13,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_17_2',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_17_m_17_2_65',
  'A healthcare provider uses an Amazon SageMaker endpoint for real-time disease diagnosis. The model''s accuracy is critical, and any degradation could have serious consequences. The data science team needs to be immediately alerted if the model starts making less accurate predictions due to changes in patient demographics or disease prevalence, even if the underlying infrastructure is healthy. Which monitoring strategy should they prioritize to detect this specific issue?',
  'mcq',
  '["Implementing Amazon SageMaker Model Monitor for model quality metrics.","Monitoring Amazon CloudWatch metrics for endpoint CPU and memory utilization.","Setting up AWS CloudTrail logs for SageMaker API calls.","Periodically running manual A/B tests with new model versions."]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A (correct) is the most effective strategy. Amazon SageMaker Model Monitor, specifically its Model Quality Monitor, is designed to continuously evaluate the actual performance of a deployed model (e.g., accuracy, F1-score) against a baseline. This directly addresses the need to detect degradation in prediction accuracy due to changes in data or concepts. Option B (Monitoring Amazon CloudWatch metrics for endpoint CPU and memory utilization) is an anti-pattern (ap-4.1-001) because infrastructure metrics only indicate the health of the underlying compute resources, not the quality or accuracy of the model''s predictions. The model can be performing poorly even if the infrastructure is healthy. Option C (Setting up AWS CloudTrail logs) is for auditing API calls, not for monitoring model performance. Option D (Periodically running manual A/B tests) is a deployment strategy for evaluating new models, not a continuous monitoring solution for an already deployed model''s ongoing performance.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain17_3',
  NOW(),
  14,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_17_2',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_17_m_17_2_66',
  'An e-commerce company uses an Amazon SageMaker endpoint for personalized product recommendations. The inference load fluctuates significantly throughout the day, with peak traffic during promotional events. The company needs to ensure high availability and responsiveness during peak times while minimizing infrastructure costs during off-peak hours. Which combination of SageMaker features and AWS services should be used to achieve this MOST cost-effectively?',
  'mcq',
  '["Provisioning a fixed number of large Amazon EC2 instances for the SageMaker endpoint.","Configuring Amazon SageMaker endpoint auto-scaling based on target utilization.","Deploying the model on AWS Lambda for serverless inference.","Using Amazon ECS with Fargate for containerized model deployment."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (correct) is configuring Amazon SageMaker endpoint auto-scaling based on target utilization. SageMaker auto-scaling dynamically adjusts the number of instances for a real-time endpoint based on metrics like CPU utilization or invocation count, ensuring capacity matches demand. This is the most cost-effective way to handle fluctuating loads while maintaining responsiveness. Option A (Provisioning a fixed number of large Amazon EC2 instances) would lead to significant over-provisioning and wasted costs during off-peak hours. Option C (Deploying the model on AWS Lambda) might be suitable for very low-latency, infrequent inferences, but for sustained, potentially high-throughput real-time recommendations, SageMaker endpoints with auto-scaling are generally more optimized and cost-effective. Option D (Using Amazon ECS with Fargate) is a valid container orchestration solution, but SageMaker endpoints offer a more managed and ML-specific solution for model deployment and auto-scaling, typically with less operational overhead for ML workloads.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain17_3',
  NOW(),
  15,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_17_2',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_17_m_17_2_67',
  'A research institution is conducting extensive experimentation with various machine learning models on Amazon SageMaker Training. Many of these training jobs are exploratory, non-urgent, and can be interrupted without significant loss, as checkpoints are saved frequently. The institution has a strict budget and needs to minimize compute costs for these non-critical workloads. Which cost optimization strategy should they prioritize for these training jobs?',
  'mcq',
  '["Using Amazon EC2 Reserved Instances for SageMaker training.","Running all training jobs on On-Demand Amazon EC2 instances.","Configuring SageMaker training jobs to use Spot Instances.","Right-sizing instances by selecting the largest available instance types."]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (correct) is configuring SageMaker training jobs to use Spot Instances. For fault-tolerant, non-urgent training jobs that can tolerate interruptions and leverage checkpointing, Spot Instances offer significant cost savings (up to 90% compared to On-Demand prices). SageMaker allows specifying Spot Instances for training jobs, which is ideal for minimizing compute costs for exploratory workloads. Option A (Using Reserved Instances) is suitable for predictable, long-term, sustained workloads, but not for intermittent or interruptible research jobs. Option B (Running on On-Demand instances) provides guaranteed capacity but is the most expensive option and does not meet the cost minimization goal for non-critical jobs. Option D (Right-sizing instances by selecting the largest available instance types) is generally incorrect; right-sizing means selecting the *appropriate* size, not necessarily the largest, and larger instances are typically more expensive, which contradicts the goal of cost minimization.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain17_3',
  NOW(),
  16,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_17_2',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_17_m_17_2_68',
  'Which Amazon CloudWatch metric is most crucial for monitoring the responsiveness of an Amazon SageMaker real-time inference endpoint?',
  'mcq',
  '["Invocations","ModelLatency","CPUUtilization","MemoryUtilization"]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (ModelLatency) is the most crucial metric for monitoring the responsiveness of an Amazon SageMaker real-time inference endpoint. It measures the time taken by the model to process a request and return a response, directly indicating the endpoint''s speed. Option A (Invocations) tracks the number of requests, which is important for throughput but not responsiveness. Option C (CPUUtilization) and Option D (MemoryUtilization) are infrastructure metrics that indicate resource usage, but a healthy infrastructure does not guarantee good model responsiveness if the model itself is slow.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain17_3',
  NOW(),
  17,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_17_2',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_17_m_17_2_69',
  'What is the primary purpose of using Amazon CloudWatch Alarms in conjunction with Amazon SageMaker endpoint metrics?',
  'mcq',
  '["To automatically retrain the model when performance degrades.","To trigger automated notifications or actions based on metric thresholds.","To visualize historical data trends for the endpoint.","To perform A/B testing on different model versions."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (To trigger automated notifications or actions based on metric thresholds) is the primary purpose of Amazon CloudWatch Alarms. When a SageMaker endpoint metric (like ModelLatency or Errors) crosses a predefined threshold, a CloudWatch Alarm can send notifications (e.g., via Amazon SNS) or trigger automated actions (e.g., an AWS Lambda function). Option A (To automatically retrain the model when performance degrades) is a possible action an alarm could trigger, but not its primary purpose. Option C (To visualize historical data trends) is a function of CloudWatch Dashboards. Option D (To perform A/B testing) is a SageMaker endpoint feature, not directly related to CloudWatch Alarms'' primary function.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain17_3',
  NOW(),
  18,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_17_2',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_17_m_17_2_70',
  'Which AWS service provides detailed metrics and monitoring for Amazon SageMaker endpoints, allowing users to track latency, throughput, and errors?',
  'mcq',
  '["AWS CloudTrail","Amazon CloudWatch","Amazon SageMaker Model Monitor","AWS X-Ray"]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (Amazon CloudWatch) is the AWS service that provides detailed metrics and monitoring for Amazon SageMaker endpoints, including latency, throughput, and errors. CloudWatch collects and processes raw data from SageMaker into readable, near real-time metrics. Option A (AWS CloudTrail) records API calls and events for auditing, not real-time metrics. Option C (Amazon SageMaker Model Monitor) focuses on ML-specific metrics like data quality and model quality drift, not infrastructure metrics. Option D (AWS X-Ray) helps analyze and debug distributed applications, providing insights into requests, but CloudWatch is the primary service for general service metrics.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain17_3',
  NOW(),
  19,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_17_2',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_17_m_17_2_71',
  'A company wants to minimize the cost of running a non-critical Amazon SageMaker batch transform job that can tolerate interruptions. Which pricing model should be chosen?',
  'mcq',
  '["On-Demand Instances","Reserved Instances","Spot Instances","Dedicated Hosts"]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (Spot Instances) is the most cost-effective pricing model for non-critical Amazon SageMaker batch transform jobs that can tolerate interruptions. Spot Instances allow users to bid on unused EC2 capacity, often at significantly lower prices than On-Demand, but can be interrupted with a two-minute warning. Option A (On-Demand Instances) provides flexibility but at a higher cost. Option B (Reserved Instances) are for long-term, predictable workloads and require a commitment, which is not ideal for intermittent batch jobs. Option D (Dedicated Hosts) provide dedicated physical servers and are the most expensive, typically used for specific licensing requirements.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain17_3',
  NOW(),
  20,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_17_2',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_17_m_17_2_72',
  'What is the primary benefit of implementing auto-scaling for Amazon SageMaker real-time inference endpoints?',
  'mcq',
  '["To reduce the latency of model invocations during peak traffic.","To ensure high availability by distributing traffic across multiple Availability Zones.","To optimize costs by matching instance capacity to actual inference traffic.","To automatically retrain the model when its performance degrades."]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (To optimize costs by matching instance capacity to actual inference traffic) is the primary benefit of implementing auto-scaling for Amazon SageMaker real-time inference endpoints. Auto-scaling dynamically adjusts the number of instances based on demand, preventing over-provisioning during low traffic and ensuring sufficient capacity during high traffic, thereby saving costs and maintaining performance. Option A (To reduce latency) is a potential outcome of adequate scaling but not the primary benefit of auto-scaling itself. Option B (To ensure high availability) is achieved by deploying across multiple Availability Zones, which is separate from auto-scaling. Option D (To automatically retrain the model) is a function of model monitoring and maintenance, not auto-scaling.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain17_3',
  NOW(),
  21,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_17_2',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_17_m_17_2_73',
  'An ML team wants to identify underutilized Amazon SageMaker inference endpoints to reduce costs. Which AWS service should they use to analyze resource usage and identify optimization opportunities?',
  'mcq',
  '["AWS CloudTrail","Amazon CloudWatch","AWS Organizations","AWS Budgets"]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (Amazon CloudWatch) is the appropriate service. CloudWatch collects metrics such as CPUUtilization, MemoryUtilization, and Invocations for SageMaker endpoints. By analyzing these metrics, the team can identify endpoints with consistently low utilization, indicating over-provisioning and opportunities for right-sizing to reduce costs. Option A (AWS CloudTrail) is for auditing API calls, not resource utilization analysis. Option C (AWS Organizations) helps manage multiple AWS accounts but doesn''t provide resource utilization insights. Option D (AWS Budgets) allows setting custom cost and usage budgets and receiving alerts, but it doesn''t provide the detailed resource utilization data needed for identifying underutilized endpoints.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain17_3',
  NOW(),
  22,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_17_2',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_17_m_17_2_74',
  'What is the primary purpose of right-sizing Amazon SageMaker instances for ML workloads in production?',
  'mcq',
  '["To ensure the highest possible performance regardless of cost.","To select the most powerful instance type available for the workload.","To optimize costs by choosing the most appropriate instance type and size for the workload''s requirements.","To automatically scale the number of instances based on demand."]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (To optimize costs by choosing the most appropriate instance type and size for the workload''s requirements) is the primary purpose of right-sizing. It involves analyzing workload performance and resource utilization to select an instance type that provides sufficient compute and memory for the task without being over-provisioned, thereby reducing unnecessary costs. Option A (To ensure the highest possible performance regardless of cost) and Option B (To select the most powerful instance type) often lead to over-provisioning and higher costs. Option D (To automatically scale the number of instances) describes auto-scaling, which is complementary to right-sizing but a distinct optimization strategy.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain17_3',
  NOW(),
  23,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_17_2',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_17_m_17_2_75',
  'An ML engineer needs to monitor the number of successful predictions per second for an Amazon SageMaker real-time endpoint. Which Amazon CloudWatch metric should be used?',
  'mcq',
  '["CPUUtilization","MemoryUtilization","Invocations","DiskReadBytes"]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (Invocations) is the correct Amazon CloudWatch metric to monitor the number of successful predictions per second for an Amazon SageMaker real-time endpoint. The ''Invocations'' metric represents the number of times the endpoint was successfully invoked. Options A (CPUUtilization) and B (MemoryUtilization) are infrastructure metrics, not directly related to the number of predictions. Option D (DiskReadBytes) is a disk I/O metric, not relevant for tracking predictions.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain17_3',
  NOW(),
  24,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_17_2',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_17_m_17_2_76',
  'Which Amazon CloudWatch feature allows users to combine multiple metrics into a single view for quick operational insights into Amazon SageMaker endpoints?',
  'mcq',
  '["CloudWatch Logs","CloudWatch Events","CloudWatch Dashboards","CloudWatch Alarms"]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (CloudWatch Dashboards) allows users to combine multiple metrics into a single, customizable view. This provides quick operational insights into the health and performance of Amazon SageMaker endpoints and other AWS resources. Option A (CloudWatch Logs) is for collecting and monitoring log files. Option B (CloudWatch Events) delivers a near real-time stream of system events. Option D (CloudWatch Alarms) triggers actions based on metric thresholds.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain17_3',
  NOW(),
  25,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_17_2',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_17_m_17_2_77',
  'A data scientist wants to be immediately notified if an Amazon SageMaker real-time inference endpoint starts returning a high rate of 5xx errors. Which combination of AWS services should be used to achieve this with minimal operational overhead?',
  'mcq',
  '["AWS CloudTrail to monitor API calls and an AWS Lambda function for notifications.","Amazon CloudWatch to monitor the ''Errors'' metric and an Amazon CloudWatch Alarm.","Amazon SageMaker Model Monitor to detect endpoint errors and send alerts.","Periodically checking SageMaker endpoint logs manually for error messages."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (Amazon CloudWatch to monitor the ''Errors'' metric and an Amazon CloudWatch Alarm) is the most effective and operationally efficient solution. Amazon SageMaker automatically publishes endpoint metrics, including ''Errors'', to Amazon CloudWatch. A CloudWatch Alarm can then be configured on the ''Errors'' metric to trigger notifications (e.g., via Amazon SNS) when the error rate exceeds a specified threshold. Option A (AWS CloudTrail) records API calls for auditing, not real-time operational errors. Option C (Amazon SageMaker Model Monitor) is designed for ML-specific issues like data and model quality drift, not general endpoint errors. Option D (Periodically checking logs manually) is highly inefficient and reactive, leading to significant operational overhead and delayed detection.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain17_3',
  NOW(),
  26,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_17_2',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_17_m_17_2_78',
  'What is the primary benefit of using Amazon SageMaker auto-scaling for inference endpoints?',
  'mcq',
  '["It allows for manual control over instance scaling to optimize costs.","It automatically adjusts the number of instances based on traffic, optimizing cost and performance.","It ensures that models are always deployed on the most powerful instance types.","It provides detailed insights into model quality and data drift."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (It automatically adjusts the number of instances based on traffic, optimizing cost and performance) is the primary benefit of Amazon SageMaker auto-scaling. It dynamically scales the number of instances up during peak demand to maintain performance and scales down during low demand to reduce costs. Option A (It allows for manual control) is incorrect, as auto-scaling is automated. Option C (It ensures models are deployed on the most powerful instance types) is not the goal; auto-scaling aims for appropriate capacity, not necessarily the most powerful. Option D (It provides detailed insights into model quality and data drift) describes Amazon SageMaker Model Monitor, not auto-scaling.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain17_3',
  NOW(),
  27,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_17_2',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_17_m_17_2_79',
  'An ML team is running a large number of Amazon SageMaker training jobs. To optimize costs, they want to use a pricing model that offers significant savings for flexible job start and stop times. Which pricing model is most suitable?',
  'mcq',
  '["On-Demand Instances","Reserved Instances","Spot Instances","Dedicated Instances"]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (Spot Instances) is the most suitable pricing model for Amazon SageMaker training jobs that have flexible start and stop times and can tolerate interruptions. Spot Instances offer significant cost savings by utilizing unused AWS capacity. Option A (On-Demand Instances) are more expensive but offer no interruptions. Option B (Reserved Instances) require a commitment for a fixed period and are generally not ideal for intermittent training jobs. Option D (Dedicated Instances) run on hardware dedicated to a single customer and are typically more expensive, used for specific compliance needs.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain17_3',
  NOW(),
  28,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_17_2',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_17_m_17_2_80',
  'What is the primary goal of cost optimization for ML solutions in production?',
  'mcq',
  '["To always use the cheapest instance types available.","To reduce operational expenses while maintaining required performance and reliability.","To maximize the number of instances running at all times.","To avoid using any managed AWS services for ML workloads."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (To reduce operational expenses while maintaining required performance and reliability) is the primary goal of cost optimization for ML solutions in production. It involves finding the right balance between cost and the necessary performance, availability, and reliability of the ML system. Option A (To always use the cheapest instance types) might compromise performance or reliability. Option C (To maximize the number of instances) would likely increase costs unnecessarily. Option D (To avoid using any managed AWS services) would increase operational overhead and potentially costs due to self-management.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain17_3',
  NOW(),
  29,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_17_2',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_17_m_17_2_81',
  'An ML engineer wants to monitor the overall health and performance of multiple Amazon SageMaker endpoints from a single pane of glass. Which Amazon CloudWatch feature is best suited for this?',
  'mcq',
  '["CloudWatch Logs","CloudWatch Events","CloudWatch Dashboards","CloudWatch Metrics"]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (CloudWatch Dashboards) is best suited for monitoring the overall health and performance of multiple Amazon SageMaker endpoints from a single pane of glass. Dashboards allow users to create custom views of their AWS resources, combining metrics, logs, and alarms. Option A (CloudWatch Logs) is for log management. Option B (CloudWatch Events) is for event-driven automation. Option D (CloudWatch Metrics) provides individual data points, but dashboards aggregate them for a comprehensive view.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain17_3',
  NOW(),
  30,
