INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_17_m_17_1_55',
  'A financial institution uses an Amazon SageMaker endpoint for loan application approval. Regulators require that the model''s predictions are fair and unbiased across different demographic groups. The data science team needs to continuously monitor the model in production to detect any unintended shifts in bias over time. Which specific SageMaker Model Monitor capability should be configured to meet this regulatory requirement?',
  'mcq',
  '["Bias Drift Monitor","Data Quality Monitor","Model Quality Monitor","Feature Attribution Drift Monitor"]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A (correct) is the Bias Drift Monitor. This specific capability within Amazon SageMaker Model Monitor is designed to continuously evaluate a model''s predictions for changes in bias metrics (e.g., DPL, DI, DPPD) across different sensitive attributes (like demographic groups) compared to a baseline. This directly addresses the regulatory requirement to detect shifts in model bias. Option B (Data Quality Monitor) detects changes in the statistical properties of input data. Option C (Model Quality Monitor) tracks overall model performance metrics like accuracy or F1-score. Option D (Feature Attribution Drift Monitor) identifies shifts in how features influence predictions, which is related to explainability but not directly to bias detection.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain17_3',
  NOW(),
  42,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_17_1',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_17_m_17_1_56',
  'A pharmaceutical company is developing a new drug discovery model on Amazon SageMaker. Due to strict regulatory compliance and the need for reproducibility, they must maintain a complete record of every step in the ML workflow, including data transformations, model training runs, and deployment artifacts. Which AWS service is specifically designed to track this information?',
  'mcq',
  '["Amazon CloudWatch","Amazon SageMaker ML Lineage Tracking","AWS CloudTrail","AWS Secrets Manager"]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (correct) is Amazon SageMaker ML Lineage Tracking. This service is specifically designed to record and visualize the lineage of ML workflows, including data sources, processing jobs, training jobs, models, and endpoints. This provides a complete audit trail for reproducibility and compliance. Option A (Amazon CloudWatch) is for monitoring and logging operational metrics. Option C (AWS CloudTrail) records API calls for auditing actions taken in an AWS account, but it does not track the internal lineage of ML artifacts and processes. Option D (AWS Secrets Manager) is for securely storing and managing credentials, not for tracking ML workflow lineage.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain17_3',
  NOW(),
  43,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_17_1',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_17_m_17_1_57',
  'A machine learning model deployed on an Amazon SageMaker endpoint is experiencing degraded performance after a recent update. The operations team needs to quickly revert to a previous, stable version of the model to minimize impact on users. Which SageMaker feature is essential for facilitating this rapid recovery?',
  'mcq',
  '["SageMaker Model Monitor","SageMaker Feature Store","SageMaker Model Versioning and Rollback","SageMaker Pipelines"]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (correct) is SageMaker Model Versioning and Rollback. SageMaker allows you to manage different versions of your models and easily deploy a previous version to an endpoint if a new version causes issues. This is crucial for rapid recovery and minimizing downtime. Option A (SageMaker Model Monitor) is for detecting performance degradation, but not for the rollback action itself. Option B (SageMaker Feature Store) is for storing and serving features for training and inference. Option D (SageMaker Pipelines) is for orchestrating end-to-end ML workflows, which can include deployment, but the specific mechanism for reverting to a previous model is versioning and rollback.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain17_3',
  NOW(),
  44,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_17_1',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_17_m_17_1_58',
  'A data science team has deployed a real-time anomaly detection model on an Amazon SageMaker endpoint. They need to be alerted immediately if the endpoint starts returning HTTP 5xx errors, indicating a server-side issue that impacts the availability of their service. Which Amazon CloudWatch metric for SageMaker endpoints should they monitor to detect this specific problem?',
  'mcq',
  '["Invocations","ModelLatency","Invocation4XXErrors","Invocation5XXErrors"]'::json,
  '{3}',
  'Option D (correct) is Invocation5XXErrors. This Amazon CloudWatch metric specifically counts the number of HTTP 5xx errors returned by the SageMaker endpoint, which indicate server-side issues impacting service availability. Monitoring this metric allows for immediate detection of critical service problems. Option A (Invocations) tracks the total number of requests. Option B (ModelLatency) tracks the time taken for the model to process a request. Option C (Invocation4XXErrors) counts client-side errors (e.g., bad requests), which are different from server-side issues.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain17_3',
  NOW(),
  45,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_17_1',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_17_m_17_1_59',
  'A machine learning model in production is experiencing a decline in predictive accuracy. Investigations reveal that the distribution of the input features provided to the model has changed significantly compared to the training data, but the underlying relationship between features and the target variable remains stable. What type of model drift is primarily occurring in this scenario?',
  'mcq',
  '["Concept Drift","Data Drift","Label Drift","Model Decay"]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (correct) is Data Drift. Data drift, also known as input data drift, occurs when the statistical properties of the input features change over time. The scenario explicitly states that the distribution of input features has changed, while the relationship with the target remains stable. Option A (Concept Drift) would imply that the relationship between features and the target has changed. Option C (Label Drift) refers to changes in the distribution of the target variable itself. Option D (Model Decay) is a general term for model performance degradation over time, which can be caused by various types of drift, but ''Data Drift'' is the specific type described here.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain17_3',
  NOW(),
  46,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_17_1',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_17_m_17_1_60',
  'Which core capability of Amazon SageMaker Model Monitor allows it to assess the statistical characteristics of the input data used for inference against a predefined baseline?',
  'mcq',
  '["Model Quality Monitoring","Data Quality Monitoring","Bias Drift Monitoring","Feature Attribution Monitoring"]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (correct) is Data Quality Monitoring. This specific capability of Amazon SageMaker Model Monitor is designed to continuously analyze the statistical characteristics (e.g., mean, standard deviation, missing values, data types) of the incoming inference data and compare them against a baseline dataset captured during training or a known good period. This helps detect shifts in the input data distribution. Option A (Model Quality Monitoring) focuses on the actual performance metrics of the model (e.g., accuracy, precision). Option C (Bias Drift Monitoring) detects changes in model fairness. Option D (Feature Attribution Monitoring) tracks how feature importance changes over time.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain17_3',
  NOW(),
  47,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_17_1',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_17_m_17_1_61',
  'A data science team wants to establish an automated alert system for their Amazon SageMaker endpoint. They need to be notified whenever the model''s prediction error rate exceeds a certain threshold. Which AWS service is primarily responsible for receiving metrics from SageMaker Model Monitor and then triggering notifications based on defined thresholds?',
  'mcq',
  '["Amazon SQS","AWS Lambda","Amazon CloudWatch Alarms","Amazon EventBridge"]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (correct) is Amazon CloudWatch Alarms. SageMaker Model Monitor publishes its monitoring results and metrics (including prediction error rates) to Amazon CloudWatch. Amazon CloudWatch Alarms can then be configured to monitor these specific metrics and transition to an ''ALARM'' state when a defined threshold is breached, subsequently triggering actions like sending notifications via Amazon SNS. Option A (Amazon SQS) is a message queuing service. Option B (AWS Lambda) is a serverless compute service that could be triggered by an alarm, but the alarm itself is managed by CloudWatch. Option D (Amazon EventBridge) is an event bus service that can route events, but CloudWatch Alarms are the direct mechanism for threshold-based alerting on metrics.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain17_3',
  NOW(),
  48,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_17_1',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_17_m_17_1_62',
  'A machine learning engineering team is developing a new recommendation system and needs to ensure full traceability of all artifacts, including datasets, preprocessing steps, training jobs, and deployed models, to meet internal audit requirements. They also need to easily visualize the flow of data and transformations. Which AWS service is purpose-built to provide this comprehensive traceability?',
  'mcq',
  '["AWS CloudTrail","Amazon CloudWatch Logs","Amazon SageMaker ML Lineage Tracking","AWS Systems Manager"]'::json,
  '{3}',
  'Option C (correct) is Amazon SageMaker ML Lineage Tracking. This service is specifically designed to record and visualize the lineage of ML workflows, including data sources, processing jobs, training jobs, models, and endpoints. It provides a comprehensive, auditable record of how ML artifacts are created and transformed, which is essential for reproducibility and compliance. Option A (AWS CloudTrail) records API calls for auditing actions taken on AWS resources, but it doesn''t track the internal dependencies and transformations within an ML workflow. Option B (Amazon CloudWatch Logs) collects and monitors logs from various AWS services, but it''s a log aggregation service, not a lineage tracker. Option D (AWS Systems Manager) is for operational insights and management of EC2 instances and other resources, not ML lineage.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain17_3',
  NOW(),
  49,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_17_1',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_17_m_17_1_63',
  'A critical fraud detection model on Amazon SageMaker has started exhibiting significantly lower accuracy. The data science team has identified that a recent model deployment is the cause. They need to immediately revert to the previous stable model version to restore performance and minimize business impact. Which SageMaker feature, combined with monitoring, enables this rapid response?',
  'mcq',
  '["SageMaker Model Versioning and Rollback, triggered by Amazon CloudWatch Alarms.","Manually redeploying the previous model from an Amazon S3 bucket.","Using SageMaker Model Monitor to retrain the current model.","Implementing A/B testing to compare the current and previous models."]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A (correct) is SageMaker Model Versioning and Rollback, triggered by Amazon CloudWatch Alarms. SageMaker allows managing multiple model versions, and if a new deployment causes issues, a previous stable version can be quickly rolled back. Integrating this with Amazon CloudWatch Alarms (which can be triggered by SageMaker Model Monitor detecting performance degradation) provides an automated or semi-automated rapid response mechanism. Option B (Manually redeploying) is a manual process, which is slower and more error-prone than an automated rollback. Option C (Using SageMaker Model Monitor to retrain) is for improving the current model over time, not for immediate reversion to a known good state. Option D (Implementing A/B testing) is for evaluating new models in parallel, not for emergency rollback of a faulty deployment.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain17_3',
  NOW(),
  50,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_17_1',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_17_m_17_1_64',
  'A financial services company relies on a machine learning model deployed on Amazon SageMaker for real-time credit risk assessment. They observe that the model''s predictions are becoming less reliable, and investigations show that the model''s accuracy has degraded significantly. However, the infrastructure metrics (CPU, memory) of the SageMaker endpoint remain normal. The team needs to identify the root cause of this accuracy degradation, which could be due to changes in input data distribution, model performance, or bias. Which comprehensive monitoring approach should they implement?',
  'mcq',
  '["Deploying Amazon SageMaker Model Monitor with Data Quality, Model Quality, and Bias Drift monitors.","Setting up Amazon CloudWatch Alarms for high CPU utilization and low memory availability.","Implementing AWS CloudTrail to log all SageMaker API calls for auditing.","Relying solely on Amazon SageMaker endpoint invocation metrics like latency and throughput."]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A (correct) provides the most comprehensive monitoring. Amazon SageMaker Model Monitor, with its Data Quality, Model Quality, and Bias Drift monitors, is specifically designed to detect various forms of model degradation. Data Quality detects shifts in input data distribution, Model Quality tracks actual prediction performance (like accuracy), and Bias Drift monitors for fairness issues. This directly addresses the need to identify the root cause of accuracy degradation. Option B (Setting up Amazon CloudWatch Alarms for CPU/memory) is an anti-pattern (ap-4.1-001) because infrastructure metrics do not reflect model quality; a model can be inaccurate even on healthy infrastructure. Option C (Implementing AWS CloudTrail) is for auditing API calls, not for monitoring model performance. Option D (Relying solely on invocation metrics) provides operational insights but does not directly measure model accuracy or detect underlying drift issues.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain17_3',
  NOW(),
  51,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_17_1',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_17_m_17_1_65',
  'A retail company uses an Amazon SageMaker endpoint for personalized product recommendations. Customer preferences are dynamic, and the model''s effectiveness can decline over time if not updated. The data science team wants to implement an automated process where the model is retrained whenever a significant shift in customer behavior (data drift) is detected. Which approach best automates this model maintenance?',
  'mcq',
  '["Retraining the model on a fixed weekly schedule using SageMaker Pipelines.","Configuring Amazon SageMaker Model Monitor to detect data drift and using Amazon CloudWatch Alarms to trigger a retraining workflow.","Manually analyzing customer feedback and triggering retraining when complaints increase.","Monitoring Amazon CloudWatch for high inference latency and retraining if thresholds are exceeded."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (correct) best automates model maintenance based on detected drift. Amazon SageMaker Model Monitor can continuously analyze inference data for data drift. When drift is detected, it publishes metrics to Amazon CloudWatch, where an Amazon CloudWatch Alarm can be configured to trigger an automated action (e.g., an AWS Lambda function that starts a SageMaker retraining job). This ensures the model is updated responsively to actual changes in customer behavior. Option A (Retraining on a fixed weekly schedule) is an anti-pattern (ap-4.2-001) because it might miss rapid shifts or retrain unnecessarily, leading to suboptimal performance or wasted resources. Option C (Manually analyzing feedback) is reactive and high operational overhead. Option D (Monitoring for high inference latency) indicates an infrastructure or performance issue, not necessarily data drift requiring retraining.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain17_3',
  NOW(),
  52,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_17_1',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_17_m_17_1_66',
  'A media company streams live sports events and uses an Amazon SageMaker endpoint to provide real-time highlights recommendations. They are experiencing occasional buffering and delays for users, and suspect the ML model inference process might be contributing to the latency. The operations team needs to pinpoint if the SageMaker endpoint itself is causing the delay. Which Amazon CloudWatch metric for SageMaker endpoints should they primarily investigate to diagnose this issue?',
  'mcq',
  '["Invocations","Invocation5XXErrors","ModelLatency","CPUUtilization"]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (correct) is ModelLatency. This metric directly measures the time taken by the model to process a single inference request. High ModelLatency indicates that the SageMaker endpoint''s model inference is slow, which could contribute to user-facing delays and buffering. Option A (Invocations) measures the number of requests, not their processing time. Option B (Invocation5XXErrors) indicates server-side errors, which would prevent recommendations entirely, not just delay them. Option D (CPUUtilization) is an infrastructure metric; while high CPU could contribute to latency, ModelLatency directly measures the model''s processing time, providing a more direct diagnosis for this specific problem.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain17_3',
  NOW(),
  53,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_17_1',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_17_m_17_1_67',
  'Which Amazon SageMaker feature is specifically designed to detect data quality issues, model quality degradation, bias drift, and feature attribution drift in production?',
  'mcq',
  '["Amazon CloudWatch","Amazon SageMaker Model Monitor","Amazon SageMaker Ground Truth","Amazon SageMaker Feature Store"]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (Amazon SageMaker Model Monitor) is specifically designed to detect data quality issues, model quality degradation, bias drift, and feature attribution drift in production. It continuously analyzes the data flowing through SageMaker endpoints and the model''s predictions. Option A (Amazon CloudWatch) provides general infrastructure and application metrics. Option C (Amazon SageMaker Ground Truth) is for data labeling. Option D (Amazon SageMaker Feature Store) is for creating, storing, and sharing features for ML models.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain17_3',
  NOW(),
  54,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_17_1',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_17_m_17_1_68',
  'A data scientist observes that the distribution of incoming inference data for a deployed model has significantly changed, leading to degraded model performance. Which type of drift does this scenario describe?',
  'mcq',
  '["Concept drift","Data drift","Bias drift","Model quality drift"]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (Data drift) describes the scenario where the distribution of incoming inference data changes over time, causing the model''s performance to degrade. This means the characteristics of the input features are no longer consistent with the data the model was trained on. Option A (Concept drift) refers to changes in the relationship between input features and the target variable. Option C (Bias drift) refers to changes in model fairness. Option D (Model quality drift) is a general term for degradation in model performance, which can be caused by data or concept drift.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain17_3',
  NOW(),
  55,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_17_1',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_17_m_17_1_69',
  'What is the primary purpose of a baseline in Amazon SageMaker Model Monitor?',
  'mcq',
  '["To define the retraining schedule for the model.","To establish a reference distribution for data and model predictions against which future inference data is compared.","To specify the instance type for the monitoring job.","To configure network isolation for the monitoring endpoint."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (To establish a reference distribution for data and model predictions against which future inference data is compared) is the primary purpose of a baseline in Amazon SageMaker Model Monitor. The baseline is typically generated from the training dataset or a representative dataset, providing a ''normal'' state for data quality, model quality, and bias metrics. Deviations from this baseline indicate drift. Option A (To define the retraining schedule) is a separate maintenance task. Option C (To specify the instance type) and Option D (To configure network isolation) are configuration details for the monitoring job, not the core purpose of the baseline.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain17_3',
  NOW(),
  56,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_17_1',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_17_m_17_1_70',
  'An ML engineer wants to be alerted automatically if the F1-score of a deployed Amazon SageMaker model falls below a specific threshold. Which Amazon SageMaker Model Monitor capability should be configured?',
  'mcq',
  '["Data quality monitoring","Feature attribution drift monitoring","Model quality monitoring","Bias drift monitoring"]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (Model quality monitoring) is the Amazon SageMaker Model Monitor capability that should be configured to detect if the F1-score (a model performance metric) falls below a specific threshold. Model quality monitoring tracks performance metrics of the model''s predictions over time. Option A (Data quality monitoring) focuses on the input data''s integrity. Option B (Feature attribution drift monitoring) tracks changes in feature importance. Option D (Bias drift monitoring) tracks changes in model fairness.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain17_3',
  NOW(),
  57,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_17_1',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_17_m_17_1_71',
  'Which Amazon SageMaker Model Monitor capability helps detect if the model''s predictions are becoming unfairly skewed towards certain groups over time?',
  'mcq',
  '["Data quality monitoring","Model quality monitoring","Bias drift monitoring","Feature attribution drift monitoring"]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (Bias drift monitoring) is the Amazon SageMaker Model Monitor capability that helps detect if the model''s predictions are becoming unfairly skewed towards certain groups over time. It monitors various bias metrics to ensure fairness in model behavior. Option A (Data quality monitoring) focuses on input data integrity. Option B (Model quality monitoring) tracks overall performance metrics. Option D (Feature attribution drift monitoring) tracks changes in how features influence predictions.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain17_3',
  NOW(),
