INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_17_m_17_3_53',
  'A data science team is configuring an Amazon SageMaker training job. They need to grant SageMaker the necessary permissions to access training data stored in Amazon S3 and to write model artifacts back to S3 upon completion. Which AWS IAM entity is specifically used to grant these permissions to SageMaker services?',
  'mcq',
  '["IAM User","IAM Role","IAM Group","IAM Policy"]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (correct) is an IAM Role. An IAM role is an AWS identity with permission policies that determine what the identity can and cannot do in AWS. SageMaker training jobs assume an IAM role (often called an execution role) to perform actions on your behalf, such as reading data from S3, writing model artifacts, and interacting with other AWS services. Option A (IAM User) is for human users or applications that need long-term credentials. Option C (IAM Group) is a collection of IAM users, not an identity that a service assumes. Option D (IAM Policy) is a document that defines permissions, but it''s attached to an IAM user, group, or role, it''s not the entity that SageMaker assumes.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain17_3',
  NOW(),
  84,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_17_3',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_17_m_17_3_54',
  'A highly regulated financial institution is deploying an Amazon SageMaker training job that processes sensitive customer data. To meet compliance requirements, the training job must not have any direct access to the public internet and must only communicate with AWS services through private endpoints. Which networking configuration should be applied to the SageMaker training job?',
  'mcq',
  '["Configure the training job to run in a public subnet with an Internet Gateway.","Configure the training job to run in a VPC with no direct internet access and use VPC endpoints for AWS service access.","Attach a public IP address to the training instance and restrict outbound traffic with security groups.","Use AWS Direct Connect to establish a private connection to the public internet."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (correct) is to configure the training job to run in a VPC with no direct internet access and use VPC endpoints for AWS service access. This ensures that the training job is isolated within a private network, preventing any data exfiltration or unauthorized access from the public internet, and all necessary communication with AWS services (like Amazon S3 for data) occurs privately via VPC endpoints. Option A (Public subnet with Internet Gateway) provides public internet access, which violates the requirement for no direct public internet access. Option C (Attach a public IP address) also grants public internet access and is an anti-pattern (ap-4.3-001) for regulated environments. Option D (AWS Direct Connect) provides a private connection to AWS, but it doesn''t inherently restrict public internet access for SageMaker jobs running within a VPC; VPC configuration is still needed.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain17_3',
  NOW(),
  85,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_17_3',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_17_m_17_3_55',
  'A company is training a machine learning model on Amazon SageMaker using sensitive customer data stored in Amazon S3. To meet data protection regulations, all data must be encrypted at rest and in transit. Which AWS service should be used to manage the encryption keys for the data stored in S3 and processed by SageMaker?',
  'mcq',
  '["AWS Secrets Manager","AWS Certificate Manager","AWS Key Management Service (AWS KMS)","AWS IAM"]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (correct) is AWS Key Management Service (AWS KMS). AWS KMS is a managed service that makes it easy to create and control encryption keys used to encrypt your data. It integrates with Amazon S3 for encryption at rest and can be used by SageMaker for encrypting data volumes and model artifacts. Option A (AWS Secrets Manager) is for managing secrets like database credentials, not encryption keys for data. Option B (AWS Certificate Manager) is for managing SSL/TLS certificates. Option D (AWS IAM) is for managing access permissions, not encryption keys themselves.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain17_3',
  NOW(),
  86,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_17_3',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_17_m_17_3_56',
  'A large enterprise has multiple data science teams collaborating on various projects within Amazon SageMaker Studio. Each team member requires isolated development environments and access to specific project resources, while ensuring their work does not interfere with others. Which SageMaker Studio feature is designed to provide this multi-user isolation and access control?',
  'mcq',
  '["SageMaker Notebook Instances","SageMaker Studio User Profiles","SageMaker Projects","SageMaker JumpStart"]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (correct) is SageMaker Studio User Profiles. SageMaker Studio supports multi-user environments by allowing administrators to create individual user profiles for each data scientist. Each user profile provides an isolated environment with its own Amazon EFS volume for storage and can be associated with specific IAM execution roles, ensuring granular access control and preventing interference between users. Option A (SageMaker Notebook Instances) are single-user environments and do not inherently provide multi-user isolation within a shared Studio environment. Option C (SageMaker Projects) are for MLOps templates and collaboration on specific ML projects, but user isolation is handled by Studio User Profiles. Option D (SageMaker JumpStart) provides pre-built solutions and models, not user isolation.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain17_3',
  NOW(),
  87,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_17_3',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_17_m_17_3_57',
  'A machine learning engineer is developing a SageMaker training script that needs to connect to an external database to fetch additional training data. The database credentials (username and password) must be stored and accessed securely, without hardcoding them in the script. Which AWS service is the most appropriate for managing these sensitive credentials?',
  'mcq',
  '["Amazon S3","AWS Systems Manager Parameter Store","AWS Secrets Manager","Amazon DynamoDB"]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (correct) is AWS Secrets Manager. AWS Secrets Manager is a dedicated service for securely storing, retrieving, and rotating database credentials, API keys, and other secrets throughout their lifecycle. It integrates with IAM for access control and provides automatic rotation capabilities, making it the most appropriate service for managing sensitive credentials like database passwords for ML workloads. Option A (Amazon S3) is object storage and not designed for secure secret management with rotation. Option B (AWS Systems Manager Parameter Store) can store parameters and secrets, but Secrets Manager offers more advanced features specifically for secrets, such as automatic rotation and integration with databases. Option D (Amazon DynamoDB) is a NoSQL database and not a dedicated secrets management service.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain17_3',
  NOW(),
  88,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_17_3',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_17_m_17_3_58',
  'A security team needs to audit all actions performed by data scientists and automated processes within Amazon SageMaker, including starting training jobs, deploying endpoints, and accessing SageMaker Studio. They require a comprehensive log of all API calls made to SageMaker services for compliance and security analysis. Which AWS service should be enabled to capture this information?',
  'mcq',
  '["Amazon CloudWatch Logs","AWS CloudTrail","Amazon GuardDuty","AWS Config"]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (correct) is AWS CloudTrail. AWS CloudTrail provides a record of actions taken by a user, role, or an AWS service in SageMaker. It logs all API calls made to SageMaker (and other AWS services) as events, including the identity of the caller, the time of the call, the source IP address, and the request parameters. This is essential for security analysis, auditing, and compliance. Option A (Amazon CloudWatch Logs) is a logging service that can store logs from various sources, but CloudTrail is the specific service for API call logging. Option C (Amazon GuardDuty) is a threat detection service, not an API logging service. Option D (AWS Config) is for assessing, auditing, and evaluating the configurations of your AWS resources, not for logging API calls.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain17_3',
  NOW(),
  89,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_17_3',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_17_m_17_3_59',
  'A data science team is setting up an Amazon SageMaker endpoint for real-time inference. They want to control which specific AWS accounts or IAM users can invoke this endpoint, rather than relying solely on the execution role attached to the endpoint itself. Which type of IAM policy is most suitable for defining these granular permissions directly on the SageMaker endpoint resource?',
  'mcq',
  '["Identity-based policy","Resource-based policy","Service control policy (SCP)","Permissions boundary"]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option B (correct) is a Resource-based policy. Resource-based policies are attached directly to an AWS resource (in this case, the SageMaker endpoint) and specify who has access to that resource and what actions they can perform. This allows the data science team to define granular permissions for specific AWS accounts or IAM users to invoke the endpoint. Option A (Identity-based policy) is attached to an IAM user, group, or role, defining what that identity can do. While an identity-based policy on the caller is also needed, a resource-based policy on the endpoint provides an additional layer of control. Option C (Service control policy (SCP)) is used in AWS Organizations to set maximum permissions for accounts, not for granular resource access. Option D (Permissions boundary) is an advanced feature to set the maximum permissions an identity-based policy can grant.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain17_3',
  NOW(),
  90,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_17_3',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_17_m_17_3_60',
  'A highly regulated government agency is training a machine learning model on Amazon SageMaker using classified data. The security policy mandates that all training jobs must operate in a completely isolated network environment, without any direct access to the public internet, to prevent data exfiltration. All communication with AWS services, such as Amazon S3 for data storage, must occur privately. Which architecture best satisfies these stringent security requirements?',
  'mcq',
  '["Configure the SageMaker training job to run in a public subnet with an Internet Gateway and restrictive security groups.","Run the SageMaker training job in a VPC configured with private subnets, and use VPC Endpoints for Amazon S3 and other AWS services.","Allow the SageMaker training job to use default public internet access but implement a Web Application Firewall (WAF) for egress filtering.","Deploy the training job on Amazon EC2 instances in a private subnet and manually configure proxy servers for AWS service access."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (correct) best satisfies the security requirements. Running the SageMaker training job in private subnets within a VPC ensures network isolation. By configuring VPC Endpoints for Amazon S3 and other necessary AWS services, all data transfer and communication remain entirely within the AWS private network, never traversing the public internet. This prevents data exfiltration and meets the ''no public internet access'' mandate. Option A (Public subnet with Internet Gateway) grants public internet access, violating the core requirement. Option C (Default public internet access with WAF) is an anti-pattern (ap-4.3-001) as it still involves public internet access, which is explicitly forbidden. A WAF is primarily for inbound web traffic, not egress filtering for a training job. Option D (Manual proxy servers on EC2) introduces significant operational overhead and complexity compared to the managed VPC Endpoints provided by AWS for SageMaker.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain17_3',
  NOW(),
  91,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_17_3',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_17_m_17_3_61',
  'A large research institution provides Amazon SageMaker Studio environments to hundreds of data scientists. Each data scientist works on different projects, requiring unique access permissions to various Amazon S3 buckets, AWS Glue Data Catalogs, and other resources. The institution needs to ensure that each data scientist''s environment and permissions are isolated and managed centrally. Which SageMaker and IAM features should be utilized to achieve this secure, multi-user isolation?',
  'mcq',
  '["Assigning a single, broad IAM role to all SageMaker Studio users.","Creating individual SageMaker Studio User Profiles, each associated with a specific IAM execution role.","Configuring network ACLs and security groups to restrict access for each user.","Relying on SageMaker Projects to define resource access for individual users."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (correct) is creating individual SageMaker Studio User Profiles, each associated with a specific IAM execution role. SageMaker Studio is designed for multi-user environments, where each data scientist gets a unique User Profile. This profile can be configured with a distinct IAM execution role, allowing for granular, least-privilege access to specific S3 buckets, Glue Data Catalogs, and other AWS resources. This ensures secure isolation and central management of permissions. Option A (Assigning a single, broad IAM role) violates the principle of least privilege and would grant all users access to all resources, compromising security. Option C (Configuring network ACLs and security groups) provides network-level isolation but does not manage granular resource-level permissions for individual users. Option D (Relying on SageMaker Projects) helps organize ML workflows but does not directly manage user-level access permissions; that''s the role of IAM and Studio User Profiles.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain17_3',
  NOW(),
  92,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_17_3',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_17_m_17_3_62',
  'A company requires strict access control for its Amazon SageMaker Studio users, ensuring they can only access specific projects and resources. Which AWS service is primarily used to manage these permissions?',
  'mcq',
  '["AWS KMS","AWS Secrets Manager","AWS IAM","Amazon VPC"]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (AWS IAM) is primarily used to manage access permissions for Amazon SageMaker Studio users and other AWS resources. IAM allows you to define granular permissions through roles and policies, ensuring users only have access to the specific projects and resources they need (principle of least privilege). Option A (AWS KMS) is for managing encryption keys. Option B (AWS Secrets Manager) is for managing secrets like API keys. Option D (Amazon VPC) is for network isolation.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain17_3',
  NOW(),
  93,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_17_3',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_17_m_17_3_63',
  'A highly regulated company needs to ensure that all Amazon SageMaker training jobs are isolated from the public internet to prevent data exfiltration. Which networking configuration is required?',
  'mcq',
  '["Configuring the training job to use a public subnet with an Internet Gateway.","Running the training job in a VPC with no direct internet access and using VPC endpoints for AWS service access.","Attaching a security group that allows all outbound traffic to the training job.","Relying on AWS KMS encryption to secure data during training."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (Running the training job in a VPC with no direct internet access and using VPC endpoints for AWS service access) is the required networking configuration. This ensures that the training job cannot access the public internet, preventing data exfiltration, and uses VPC endpoints to securely access necessary AWS services like Amazon S3 within the private network. Option A (Public subnet with Internet Gateway) provides direct internet access, which is the anti-pattern to avoid. Option C (Security group allowing all outbound traffic) would still allow internet access. Option D (AWS KMS encryption) secures data at rest and in transit but does not prevent network access to the public internet.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain17_3',
  NOW(),
  94,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_17_3',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_17_m_17_3_64',
  'A company needs to store sensitive API keys and database credentials used by its Amazon SageMaker processing jobs. Which AWS service is designed for securely managing and rotating these credentials?',
  'mcq',
  '["Amazon S3","AWS KMS","AWS Secrets Manager","AWS IAM"]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (AWS Secrets Manager) is the AWS service designed for securely managing, storing, and rotating sensitive credentials like API keys and database credentials. It helps protect access to applications, services, and IT resources. Option A (Amazon S3) is object storage. Option B (AWS KMS) is for managing encryption keys. Option D (AWS IAM) is for managing user and service permissions.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain17_3',
  NOW(),
  95,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_17_3',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_17_m_17_3_65',
  'A company requires that all data at rest used by their Amazon SageMaker training jobs and inference endpoints must be encrypted. Which AWS service is primarily responsible for managing the encryption keys used for this purpose?',
  'mcq',
  '["AWS IAM","Amazon VPC","AWS KMS","AWS Secrets Manager"]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (correct) is AWS Key Management Service (AWS KMS), which is used to create and manage cryptographic keys and control their use across a wide range of AWS services. For SageMaker, KMS can encrypt data stored in Amazon S3, EBS volumes attached to instances, and other integrated services. Option A (AWS IAM) manages access permissions but not the encryption keys themselves. Option B (Amazon VPC) provides network isolation, not encryption key management. Option D (AWS Secrets Manager) is for securely storing application secrets like API keys or database credentials, not for managing encryption keys for data at rest.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain17_3',
  NOW(),
  96,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_17_3',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_17_m_17_3_66',
  'A data scientist needs to securely store sensitive credentials, such as API keys for external services or database passwords, that their Amazon SageMaker training jobs require. Which AWS service is best suited for this purpose?',
  'mcq',
  '["Amazon S3","AWS KMS","AWS Secrets Manager","Amazon CloudWatch"]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (correct) is AWS Secrets Manager, which helps you protect access to your applications, services, and IT resources by enabling you to easily rotate, manage, and retrieve database credentials, API keys, and other secrets throughout their lifecycle. Option A (Amazon S3) is object storage and while data can be encrypted, it''s not designed for secret rotation and management. Option B (AWS KMS) manages encryption keys, not the secrets themselves. Option D (Amazon CloudWatch) is a monitoring service and not suitable for storing credentials.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain17_3',
  NOW(),
  97,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_17_3',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_17_m_17_3_67',
  'Which AWS service is used to define and manage permissions for users, groups, and roles to access Amazon SageMaker resources and perform specific actions?',
  'mcq',
  '["AWS KMS","AWS Secrets Manager","AWS IAM","Amazon VPC"]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (correct) is AWS Identity and Access Management (IAM), which enables you to securely control access to AWS services and resources. You use IAM to create and manage AWS users and groups, and use permissions to allow and deny their access to AWS resources, including Amazon SageMaker. Option A (AWS KMS) manages encryption keys. Option B (AWS Secrets Manager) stores and manages secrets like passwords and API keys. Option D (Amazon VPC) provides network isolation for AWS resources.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain17_3',
  NOW(),
  98,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_17_3',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_17_m_17_3_68',
  'An organization needs to ensure that all API calls made to Amazon SageMaker services are logged for security analysis, auditing, and compliance purposes. Which AWS service should be enabled to capture these events?',
  'mcq',
  '["Amazon CloudWatch","AWS CloudTrail","Amazon SageMaker Model Monitor","AWS IAM"]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (correct) is AWS CloudTrail. CloudTrail provides a record of actions taken by a user, role, or an AWS service in SageMaker. It captures API calls and related events, which is essential for security analysis, auditing, and compliance. Option A (Amazon CloudWatch) collects metrics and logs for operational monitoring, but CloudTrail specifically focuses on API activity logging. Option C (Amazon SageMaker Model Monitor) is for detecting model and data drift, not for auditing API calls. Option D (AWS IAM) manages access permissions but does not log the actions performed.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain17_3',
  NOW(),
  99,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_17_3',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

