  '["File mode","Pipe mode","FastFile mode","Batch mode","Direct mode"]'::json,
  '{1,2}',
  'Option B (Pipe mode) and Option C (FastFile mode) are optimized Amazon SageMaker input modes designed to stream data directly from Amazon S3 to the training instances. This approach avoids the need to download the entire dataset before training begins, significantly reducing startup latency for large datasets and improving training efficiency. Option A (File mode) downloads the entire dataset to the training instance''s EBS volume before training starts, which can cause significant startup latency for large datasets (anti-pattern ap-1.1-001). Option D (Batch mode) and Option E (Direct mode) are not standard SageMaker input modes for training data.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain14_3',
  NOW(),
  13,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_14_1',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_14_m_14_1_130',
  'Which AWS services are suitable for ingesting large volumes of streaming data into a data lake built on Amazon S3 for subsequent machine learning processing? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Amazon Kinesis Data Streams","Amazon SQS","Amazon Kinesis Data Firehose","AWS Step Functions","Amazon DynamoDB"]'::json,
  '{0,2}',
  'Option A (Amazon Kinesis Data Streams) is a highly scalable and durable real-time data streaming service that can capture, process, and store data streams. It can be used as a source for other services to deliver data to Amazon S3. Option C (Amazon Kinesis Data Firehose) is a fully managed service that automatically loads streaming data into Amazon S3, Amazon Redshift, Amazon OpenSearch Service, and Splunk. It handles buffering, encryption, and compression, making it ideal for direct ingestion into a data lake. Option B (Amazon SQS) is a message queuing service, not primarily for streaming large volumes of data to S3. Option D (AWS Step Functions) is a serverless workflow service, not a data ingestion service. Option E (Amazon DynamoDB) is a NoSQL database, not designed for streaming data into a data lake.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain14_3',
  NOW(),
  14,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_14_1',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_14_m_14_1_131',
  'A machine learning team needs to store various types of data, including raw logs, processed features, and model artifacts, in a scalable and cost-effective manner. Which AWS storage options are commonly used as foundational components for a data lake architecture for ML? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Amazon RDS","Amazon S3","Amazon EFS","AWS Glue Data Catalog","Amazon Redshift"]'::json,
  '{1,3}',
  'Option B (Amazon S3) is the most common and cost-effective storage service for building a data lake, offering virtually unlimited scalability and high durability for various data types, including raw, processed, and refined data. Option D (AWS Glue Data Catalog) is a metadata repository that stores schema information for data stored in Amazon S3, enabling services like Amazon Athena and Amazon Redshift Spectrum to query the data effectively. Option A (Amazon RDS) is a relational database service, not typically used for raw data lake storage. Option C (Amazon EFS) is a scalable file system for EC2 instances, suitable for shared file storage but not the primary component of a data lake. Option E (Amazon Redshift) is a data warehouse, often used for analytics on structured data after it''s processed in the data lake, but not the raw storage layer itself.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain14_3',
  NOW(),
  15,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_14_1',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_14_m_14_1_132',
  'Which of the following are common data ingestion patterns for machine learning workloads on AWS? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Batch processing","Real-time streaming","Direct database access","API Gateway integration","Serverless function execution"]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'Option A (Batch processing) involves collecting data over a period and then processing it in large chunks. This is a common pattern for historical data, periodic updates, or when immediate processing is not required. Option B (Real-time streaming) involves processing data as it arrives, suitable for scenarios requiring immediate insights or reactions, such as IoT sensor data or clickstreams. Option C (Direct database access) is a method of accessing data but not a general ingestion pattern for ML workloads that often aggregate data from various sources into a data lake. Option D (API Gateway integration) is for building and managing APIs, not a data ingestion pattern itself. Option E (Serverless function execution) describes a compute model, not a data ingestion pattern.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain14_3',
  NOW(),
  16,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_14_1',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_14_m_14_1_133',
  'When building a data lake for machine learning, which AWS services are typically used together to form the core architecture for data storage and metadata management? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Amazon Redshift","Amazon S3","AWS Glue Data Catalog","Amazon DynamoDB","Amazon EFS"]'::json,
  '{1,2}',
  'Option B (Amazon S3) serves as the scalable, durable, and cost-effective object storage layer for the data lake, holding raw, processed, and refined data. Option C (AWS Glue Data Catalog) acts as the central metadata repository for the data lake, storing schemas, table definitions, and locations of data stored in S3, making it discoverable and queryable by other services like Amazon Athena. Option A (Amazon Redshift) is a data warehouse, often used for analytics on structured data, but not the core storage for a data lake. Option D (Amazon DynamoDB) is a NoSQL database, suitable for specific application data but not a general data lake storage. Option E (Amazon EFS) is a network file system, not typically the primary storage for a large-scale data lake.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain14_3',
  NOW(),
  17,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_14_1',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_14_m_14_1_134',
  'A company is ingesting sensor data from thousands of devices for real-time anomaly detection using machine learning. Which AWS services are best suited for collecting and delivering this high-volume streaming data to a processing layer? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Amazon SQS","Amazon Kinesis Data Streams","Amazon Kinesis Data Firehose","AWS Batch","Amazon RDS"]'::json,
  '{1,2}',
  'Option B (Amazon Kinesis Data Streams) is designed for real-time processing of large streams of data. It allows for custom applications to process data as it arrives, making it suitable for real-time anomaly detection. Option C (Amazon Kinesis Data Firehose) is a fully managed service for delivering real-time streaming data to destinations like Amazon S3, Amazon Redshift, or Amazon OpenSearch Service. While it can also deliver to custom destinations via AWS Lambda, it''s often used for direct ingestion into storage for later processing. Option A (Amazon SQS) is a message queuing service, not optimized for high-throughput real-time streaming data ingestion. Option D (AWS Batch) is for running batch computing workloads. Option E (Amazon RDS) is a relational database service, not for streaming data ingestion.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain14_3',
  NOW(),
  18,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_14_1',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_14_m_14_1_135',
  'Which of the following are valid storage options for machine learning datasets on AWS, offering different characteristics regarding access patterns and scalability? (Select THREE.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Amazon S3","Amazon Redshift","Amazon EFS","Amazon CloudWatch","AWS KMS"]'::json,
  '{0,1,2}',
  'Option A (Amazon S3) is highly scalable object storage, ideal for data lakes, raw data, and model artifacts. Option B (Amazon Redshift) is a petabyte-scale data warehouse, suitable for structured, analytical datasets. Option C (Amazon EFS) provides scalable file storage for EC2 instances, useful for shared access to datasets across multiple compute instances. Option D (Amazon CloudWatch) is a monitoring service, not a data storage service. Option E (AWS KMS) is a key management service, used for encryption, not for storing datasets.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain14_3',
  NOW(),
  19,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_14_1',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_14_m_14_1_136',
  'Which of the following are key considerations for data formats when storing machine learning data in Amazon S3 for efficient querying and processing? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Row-based vs. columnar storage","Data encryption at rest","Data compression","IAM role permissions","VPC endpoint configuration"]'::json,
  '{0,2}',
  'Option A (Row-based vs. columnar storage) is a critical consideration. Columnar formats (like Parquet, ORC) are generally more efficient for analytical queries and machine learning workloads than row-based formats (like CSV, JSON) because they allow for reading only necessary columns, reducing I/O and improving performance (anti-pattern ap-1.1-002). Option C (Data compression) significantly reduces storage costs and improves query performance by minimizing the amount of data that needs to be read from disk. Option B (Data encryption at rest) is a security best practice, but not a data format consideration itself. Option D (IAM role permissions) and Option E (VPC endpoint configuration) are related to access control and networking, respectively, not data formats.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain14_3',
  NOW(),
  20,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_14_1',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_14_m_14_1_137',
  'A data scientist is preparing a dataset for a new machine learning model. Which of the following are valid data ingestion patterns for bringing data into AWS for ML workloads? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Batch ingestion","Streaming ingestion","Direct database replication","Manual file upload via console","API calls from client applications"]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'Option A (Batch ingestion) involves collecting data over a period and then processing it in large batches. This is common for historical data or periodic updates. Option B (Streaming ingestion) involves processing data continuously as it arrives, suitable for real-time analytics or immediate reactions. These are the two primary patterns for data ingestion in ML. Option C (Direct database replication) is a method of data transfer but not a general ingestion pattern for ML data lakes. Option D (Manual file upload via console) is a way to get data into S3, but not a scalable ''pattern'' for ongoing ingestion. Option E (API calls from client applications) describes how data might be generated, but not the ingestion pattern into the ML pipeline.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain14_3',
  NOW(),
  21,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_14_1',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_14_m_14_1_138',
  'Which AWS services can be used to store and manage large-scale datasets for machine learning, providing different access patterns and performance characteristics? (Select THREE.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Amazon S3","Amazon Redshift","Amazon DynamoDB","AWS Lambda","Amazon CloudWatch"]'::json,
  '{0,1,2}',
  'Option A (Amazon S3) is object storage, ideal for data lakes, raw data, and model artifacts, offering high scalability and cost-effectiveness. Option B (Amazon Redshift) is a data warehouse, optimized for analytical queries on structured data. Option C (Amazon DynamoDB) is a NoSQL database, suitable for key-value and document data with low-latency access. These three are common storage options for ML datasets. Option D (AWS Lambda) is a serverless compute service. Option E (Amazon CloudWatch) is a monitoring service. Neither Lambda nor CloudWatch are primary data storage services.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain14_3',
  NOW(),
  22,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_14_1',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_14_m_14_1_139',
  'A large e-commerce company processes millions of customer clickstream events daily, generating several terabytes of raw data. This data needs to be ingested, stored, and prepared for machine learning models that predict user behavior and personalize recommendations. The ML team requires the ability to perform complex analytical queries on historical data and efficiently feed data into Amazon SageMaker training jobs. The solution must be highly scalable, cost-effective for long-term storage, and optimize data retrieval for analytical workloads. Which two approaches should the ML Solutions Architect recommend for ingesting and storing this data to meet these requirements? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Ingest real-time clickstream data using Amazon Kinesis Data Firehose to deliver directly to Amazon S3.","Store all raw and processed data in Amazon Redshift for direct querying and SageMaker integration.","Utilize Amazon S3 as a data lake for raw and processed data, leveraging its scalability and cost-effectiveness.","Store all data in Amazon DynamoDB tables for quick access, then export to S3 for SageMaker training.","Transform data into row-based CSV format before storing in S3 to simplify data loading into SageMaker."]'::json,
  '{0,2}',
  'Option A (correct) involves using Amazon Kinesis Data Firehose for ingesting high-volume, real-time streaming data. Firehose can automatically deliver data to Amazon S3, which is a common pattern for building data lakes, and it handles scaling and buffering, minimizing operational overhead. Option C (correct) recommends using Amazon S3 as the core data lake storage. S3 offers unmatched scalability, durability, and cost-effectiveness for storing vast amounts of raw and processed ML data. It integrates seamlessly with other AWS services for processing and is the preferred storage for SageMaker training datasets. Option B (incorrect) suggests storing all data in Amazon Redshift. While Redshift is excellent for analytical queries, storing all raw and processed data, especially terabytes of historical clickstream data, directly in Redshift would be significantly more expensive than S3 for long-term storage and less flexible for a data lake architecture. Option D (incorrect) proposes Amazon DynamoDB. DynamoDB is a NoSQL database optimized for high-performance, low-latency key-value access, not for large-scale analytical queries on historical data or cost-effective long-term storage of raw ML datasets. Exporting to S3 adds an unnecessary step and cost. Option E (incorrect) suggests transforming data into row-based CSV format. For analytical ML workloads, especially with large datasets, columnar formats like Parquet or ORC are significantly more efficient. They offer better compression and enable predicate pushdown, reducing query costs and improving performance in services like Amazon Athena or Redshift Spectrum (anti-pattern: ap-1.1-002).',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain14_3',
  NOW(),
  23,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_14_1',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_14_m_14_1_140',
  'A global media company collects vast amounts of user engagement data from its streaming platforms, totaling hundreds of terabytes. This data is used for personalized content recommendations and targeted advertising. The ML engineering team needs to store this data cost-effectively, ensure high availability, and optimize it for analytical queries using Amazon Athena and subsequent training with Amazon SageMaker. They are concerned about the cost of data retrieval and query performance. Which two storage and data format strategies should be implemented to achieve these goals? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Store all raw and processed data in Amazon S3, leveraging its tiered storage classes for cost optimization.","Utilize Amazon Redshift for primary storage of all historical user engagement data to enable fast SQL queries.","Convert all processed data into columnar formats like Apache Parquet or ORC before storing in Amazon S3.","Maintain data exclusively in JSON format within Amazon S3 to simplify schema evolution and data parsing.","Use Amazon EFS to store the processed datasets, ensuring high-performance file system access for SageMaker."]'::json,
  '{0,2}',
  'Option A (correct) recommends storing all raw and processed data in Amazon S3. S3 is the foundational storage for data lakes due to its scalability, durability, and cost-effectiveness. Leveraging S3''s tiered storage classes (e.g., S3 Standard-IA, S3 Glacier) allows for further cost optimization based on access patterns. Option C (correct) advises converting processed data into columnar formats like Apache Parquet or ORC. This is crucial for optimizing analytical queries with Amazon Athena and reducing data retrieval costs. Columnar formats offer superior compression and allow query engines to read only the necessary columns, significantly improving performance and cost efficiency compared to row-based formats (anti-pattern: ap-1.1-002). Option B (incorrect) suggests using Amazon Redshift for primary storage of all historical data. While Redshift is a powerful data warehouse, storing hundreds of terabytes of raw and processed data primarily in Redshift would be significantly more expensive than S3, especially for data that is not constantly queried. S3 as a data lake with Redshift Spectrum or Athena for querying is more cost-effective. Option D (incorrect) proposes maintaining data exclusively in JSON format. While JSON is flexible, it is a row-based format and is less efficient for analytical queries on large datasets compared to columnar formats like Parquet or ORC, leading to higher query costs and slower performance. Option E (incorrect) suggests using Amazon EFS. Amazon EFS is a scalable file storage for EC2 instances, suitable for shared file system access, but it is generally more expensive than S3 for large-scale, long-term data lake storage and is not typically used as the primary storage for analytical ML datasets.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain14_3',
  NOW(),
  24,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_14_1',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_14_m_14_1_141',
  'A research institution is collecting genomic sequencing data, which is extremely large (petabytes) and requires long-term, highly durable, and cost-effective storage. The data is accessed infrequently for new research projects but needs to be readily available for Amazon SageMaker Processing jobs when required. The institution also needs to ensure that the data is organized and easily discoverable for various research teams. Which two AWS services or approaches should the ML Solutions Architect recommend for storing and cataloging this data? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Store all genomic data in Amazon S3, utilizing S3 Glacier Deep Archive for cost-effective long-term storage.","Use Amazon Redshift as the primary storage for all genomic data to enable complex SQL queries.","Implement an AWS Glue Data Catalog to manage metadata and schema for the data stored in S3.","Store the data in Amazon RDS for PostgreSQL to leverage its relational database capabilities for complex queries.","Utilize Amazon EFS for storing the genomic data to provide shared file system access to compute instances."]'::json,
  '{0,2}',
  'Option A (correct) recommends storing all genomic data in Amazon S3, specifically utilizing S3 Glacier Deep Archive for cost-effective long-term storage. S3 is ideal for petabyte-scale data due to its scalability, durability, and various storage classes that cater to different access patterns, making it highly cost-effective for infrequently accessed archival data. Option C (correct) suggests implementing an AWS Glue Data Catalog. A Data Catalog is essential for managing metadata and schema for data stored in S3, making the genomic data easily discoverable and queryable by various research teams and services like Amazon Athena or SageMaker Processing. Option B (incorrect) proposes using Amazon Redshift as primary storage. Redshift is a data warehouse optimized for structured analytical queries, not for storing petabytes of raw, infrequently accessed genomic data, which would be prohibitively expensive compared to S3. Option D (incorrect) suggests Amazon RDS for PostgreSQL. RDS is a relational database service, not suitable for storing petabytes of unstructured or semi-structured genomic data, nor is it cost-effective for such scale compared to object storage. Option E (incorrect) recommends Amazon EFS. EFS provides scalable file storage, but it is generally more expensive than S3 for petabyte-scale, long-term archival storage and is typically used for shared file systems for compute instances rather than a primary data lake storage for ML.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain14_3',
  NOW(),
  25,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_14_1',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_14_m_14_1_142',
  'A research team is analyzing satellite imagery for environmental monitoring, generating petabytes of image data daily. This data needs to be ingested efficiently, stored in a highly available and durable manner, and made accessible for various processing and analysis tasks, including Amazon SageMaker training. The team requires the ability to quickly query metadata associated with images (e.g., capture date, location) and perform large-scale batch processing. Which two AWS services are best suited for ingesting and storing this high-volume image data while supporting metadata querying and batch processing? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Amazon S3 for scalable, durable, and cost-effective object storage of image data.","Amazon Kinesis Data Firehose for ingesting high-volume streaming image data into S3.","Amazon Redshift for storing all raw image data and performing metadata queries.","Amazon DynamoDB for storing image metadata and enabling fast lookups.","Amazon EFS for providing a shared file system for all image data to SageMaker."]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'Option A (correct) is Amazon S3. S3 is the most suitable service for storing petabytes of image data due to its extreme scalability, high durability, and cost-effectiveness. It''s the foundation of a data lake and integrates seamlessly with other AWS services for processing and ML training. Option B (correct) is Amazon Kinesis Data Firehose. For ingesting petabytes of image data daily, Firehose can efficiently stream this high-volume data directly into Amazon S3. It handles automatic scaling, buffering, and compression, simplifying the ingestion pipeline. Option C (incorrect) suggests Amazon Redshift for storing all raw image data. Redshift is a data warehouse for structured data and analytical queries, not designed for storing raw petabytes of image objects, which would be prohibitively expensive and inefficient. While it could store *some* metadata, it''s not the primary storage for the images themselves. Option D (incorrect) suggests Amazon DynamoDB for storing image metadata. While DynamoDB offers fast lookups, for petabytes of data, a data lake approach with S3 for objects and AWS Glue Data Catalog or Amazon Athena for metadata querying (potentially with a smaller, specialized database for very high-frequency metadata lookups if needed) is generally more scalable and cost-effective. Storing all metadata in DynamoDB might become complex and costly at this scale. Option E (incorrect) suggests Amazon EFS. EFS is a network file system primarily for shared file access across EC2 instances. It is not designed for petabyte-scale, cost-effective object storage and is generally much more expensive than S3 for data lake use cases.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain14_3',
  NOW(),
  26,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_14_1',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_14_m_14_1_143',
  'An autonomous vehicle company collects vast amounts of sensor data (LiDAR, camera, radar) from its fleet, generating hundreds of terabytes daily. This data is critical for training perception models. The ML team needs to ingest this high-volume, high-velocity data, store it cost-effectively for long-term archival, and ensure it''s available for large-scale batch processing and Amazon SageMaker training jobs. They also need to efficiently manage metadata for data discovery and lineage. Which two services are most suitable for ingesting and storing this data while supporting these requirements? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Amazon Kinesis Data Firehose to ingest streaming sensor data and deliver it to Amazon S3.","Amazon S3 for scalable, durable, and cost-effective long-term storage of raw and processed sensor data.","Amazon Redshift to store all raw sensor data and manage its metadata for discovery.","Amazon DynamoDB for high-throughput storage of raw sensor data and associated metadata.","Amazon EFS for providing a high-performance, shared file system for all sensor data to SageMaker."]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'Option A (correct) is Amazon Kinesis Data Firehose. For high-volume, high-velocity streaming sensor data, Firehose is an excellent choice for ingestion. It automatically scales to handle throughput, buffers data, and delivers it to Amazon S3, simplifying the creation of a data lake for autonomous vehicle data. Option B (correct) is Amazon S3. S3 is the most appropriate service for storing hundreds of terabytes of raw and processed sensor data due to its unparalleled scalability, durability, and cost-effectiveness for long-term archival. It''s the standard for data lake storage and directly integrates with SageMaker for training. Option C (incorrect) suggests Amazon Redshift for storing all raw sensor data. Redshift is a data warehouse optimized for structured data and analytical queries, not for storing raw, unstructured, or semi-structured sensor data at petabyte scale. It would be prohibitively expensive and inefficient for this use case. Option D (incorrect) suggests Amazon DynamoDB for high-throughput storage of raw sensor data. DynamoDB is a NoSQL database for key-value and document data, optimized for low-latency access to individual items. It is not designed for storing massive volumes of raw sensor objects or for cost-effective long-term archival of large datasets. Option E (incorrect) suggests Amazon EFS. EFS provides a scalable network file system, but it is generally more expensive than S3 for large-scale, long-term data lake storage and is not typically used as the primary storage for raw, high-volume sensor data from autonomous vehicles.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain14_3',
  NOW(),
  27,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_14_1',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_14_m_14_1_144',
  'A large logistics company uses machine learning to optimize delivery routes. They collect GPS data, traffic information, and delivery statuses in real-time. This data needs to be ingested, stored, and prepared for models that predict optimal routes and delivery times. The ML team requires a solution that can handle high-velocity streaming data, store it cost-effectively for historical analysis, and ensure that the data is in an optimized format for analytical queries using Amazon Athena. Which two AWS services or approaches should the ML Solutions Architect recommend for ingesting and storing this data? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Use Amazon Kinesis Data Firehose to ingest real-time GPS and traffic data and deliver it to Amazon S3.","Store all raw and processed data in Amazon S3, leveraging its scalability and cost-effectiveness for a data lake.","Store all data in Amazon Redshift for direct querying and route optimization model training.","Transform all processed data into row-based CSV format before storing in S3 to simplify data loading into SageMaker.","Utilize Amazon EFS to store the processed datasets, ensuring high-performance file system access for SageMaker."]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'Option A (correct) recommends using Amazon Kinesis Data Firehose. For high-velocity streaming data like GPS and traffic information, Firehose is ideal for ingesting and automatically delivering it to Amazon S3. It simplifies the setup of a streaming data pipeline without managing servers. Option B (correct) suggests storing all raw and processed data in Amazon S3. S3 is the cornerstone of a cost-effective and scalable data lake, providing durable storage for vast amounts of data. It integrates well with other AWS services for processing and is the primary storage for SageMaker training datasets. Option C (incorrect) suggests storing all data in Amazon Redshift. While Redshift is excellent for analytical queries, storing all raw and processed high-volume historical data directly in Redshift would be significantly more expensive than S3 for long-term storage and less flexible for a data lake architecture. Option D (incorrect) proposes transforming all processed data into row-based CSV format. For analytical queries with Amazon Athena, columnar formats like Parquet or ORC are significantly more efficient and cost-effective than row-based formats like CSV (anti-pattern: ap-1.1-002). Columnar formats reduce data scanned and improve query performance. Option E (incorrect) suggests utilizing Amazon EFS. Amazon EFS is a scalable network file system, but it is generally more expensive than S3 for large-scale, long-term data lake storage and is not typically used as the primary storage for analytical ML datasets.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain14_3',
  NOW(),
  28,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_14_1',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_14_m_14_1_145',
  'A large telecommunications company is building an ML model to predict network congestion. They collect vast amounts of network performance metrics (e.g., latency, throughput, packet loss) from various devices, generating high-velocity streaming data. The ML team needs to ingest this data, store it cost-effectively for long-term analysis, and ensure it''s in a format optimized for querying with Amazon Athena and subsequent training with Amazon SageMaker. Which two AWS services or approaches should the ML Solutions Architect recommend for ingesting and storing this data? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Use Amazon Kinesis Data Firehose to ingest real-time network metrics and deliver them to Amazon S3.","Store all raw and processed network metrics in Amazon S3, leveraging its scalability and cost-effectiveness.","Store all network metrics in Amazon Redshift for direct querying and model training.","Transform all processed data into row-based JSON format before storing in S3 to simplify data parsing.","Utilize Amazon EFS to store the processed datasets, ensuring high-performance file system access for SageMaker."]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'Option A (correct) recommends using Amazon Kinesis Data Firehose. For high-velocity streaming network performance metrics, Firehose is an ideal service for ingesting and automatically delivering data to Amazon S3. It handles automatic scaling, buffering, and compression, simplifying the ingestion pipeline. Option B (correct) suggests storing all raw and processed network metrics in Amazon S3. S3 is the most suitable service for storing vast amounts of data due to its unparalleled scalability, high durability, and cost-effectiveness for long-term storage. It''s the foundation of a data lake and directly integrates with SageMaker for training. Option C (incorrect) suggests storing all network metrics in Amazon Redshift. Redshift is a data warehouse optimized for structured data and analytical queries, not for storing raw, high-volume streaming data at petabyte scale. It would be significantly more expensive and less flexible than S3 for a data lake approach. Option D (incorrect) proposes transforming all processed data into row-based JSON format. While JSON is flexible, it is a row-based format and is less efficient for analytical queries on large datasets compared to columnar formats like Parquet or ORC (anti-pattern: ap-1.1-002). Columnar formats offer better compression and allow query engines to read only necessary columns, reducing query costs and improving performance. Option E (incorrect) suggests utilizing Amazon EFS. Amazon EFS is a scalable network file system, but it is generally more expensive than S3 for large-scale, long-term data lake storage and is not typically used as the primary storage for analytical ML datasets.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain14_3',
  NOW(),
  29,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_14_1',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_14_m_14_1_146',
  'For analytical machine learning workloads that involve large datasets frequently queried by Amazon Athena, which two data formats are most cost-effective and performant when stored in Amazon S3? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Apache Parquet","JSON","Apache ORC","CSV","TFRecord"]'::json,
  '{0,2}',
  'For analytical workloads with large datasets queried by Amazon Athena, columnar data formats significantly reduce query costs and improve performance due to predicate pushdown and better compression. Option A (Apache Parquet) and Option C (Apache ORC) are both columnar formats optimized for analytical queries, making them the most cost-effective and performant choices. Option B (JSON) and Option D (CSV) are row-based formats (representing anti-pattern ap-1.1-002) which are less efficient for analytical queries as Athena must scan entire rows. Option E (TFRecord) is a binary format optimized for TensorFlow training pipelines, not general analytical querying with Athena.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain14_3',
  NOW(),
  30,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_14_1',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

