INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_14_m_14_2_104',
  'Which AWS services can be used to perform large-scale ETL (Extract, Transform, Load) operations and feature engineering on datasets before machine learning model training? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Amazon Kinesis Data Streams","AWS Glue","Amazon SageMaker Data Wrangler","Amazon Redshift","Amazon SQS"]'::json,
  '{1,2}',
  'Option B (AWS Glue) is a serverless data integration service that makes it easy to discover, prepare, and combine data for analytics and machine learning. It provides ETL capabilities, including a visual ETL editor and Apache Spark-based jobs. Option C (Amazon SageMaker Data Wrangler) is a feature within Amazon SageMaker that simplifies the process of aggregating and preparing data for ML. It offers a visual interface to perform data selection, cleansing, exploration, and feature engineering. Option A (Amazon Kinesis Data Streams) is for real-time data streaming, not primarily for batch ETL or feature engineering. Option D (Amazon Redshift) is a data warehouse service, while it can perform transformations, it''s not its primary role for general ML feature engineering. Option E (Amazon SQS) is a message queuing service, not an ETL or feature engineering tool.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain14_3',
  NOW(),
  43,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_14_2',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_14_m_14_2_105',
  'A machine learning team needs to ensure consistency in feature computation between training and inference to prevent training-serving skew. Which AWS services or approaches help achieve this consistency? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Manually applying transformations at inference time","Amazon SageMaker Feature Store","Amazon SageMaker Pipelines","AWS Lambda for inference only","Amazon S3 for feature storage"]'::json,
  '{1,2}',
  'Option B (Amazon SageMaker Feature Store) is a purpose-built service to store, update, retrieve, and share machine learning features for training and inference. It ensures that the same feature definitions and transformations are used consistently across both phases, preventing training-serving skew. Option C (Amazon SageMaker Pipelines) allows you to define and orchestrate ML workflows, including data preprocessing and feature engineering steps. By defining these steps within a pipeline, you ensure that the same logic is applied during both training and subsequent inference, maintaining consistency (anti-pattern ap-1.2-002). Option A (Manually applying transformations at inference time) is an anti-pattern (ap-1.2-002) as it introduces a high risk of inconsistencies. Option D (AWS Lambda for inference only) might be used to host inference, but doesn''t inherently guarantee feature consistency without a shared feature engineering pipeline or store. Option E (Amazon S3 for feature storage) stores features but doesn''t enforce consistent computation logic.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain14_3',
  NOW(),
  44,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_14_2',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_14_m_14_2_106',
  'Which of the following techniques are commonly used to handle missing data values in a dataset during the data preparation phase for machine learning? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Normalization","Imputation","Dropping rows/columns","One-hot encoding","Standardization"]'::json,
  '{1,2}',
  'Option B (Imputation) involves filling in missing values with estimated values, such as the mean, median, mode, or more advanced statistical methods. Option C (Dropping rows/columns) is a straightforward strategy where rows (samples) or columns (features) with missing data are removed from the dataset. This is suitable when missing data is minimal or a column has too many missing values to be useful. Option A (Normalization) and Option E (Standardization) are feature scaling techniques, not methods for handling missing data. Option D (One-hot encoding) is a technique for converting categorical variables into a numerical format, not for addressing missing values.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain14_3',
  NOW(),
  45,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_14_2',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_14_m_14_2_107',
  'A data scientist needs to prepare a dataset where several features have different scales and distributions. Which feature engineering techniques are used to transform numerical features to a common scale or distribution? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Binning","Normalization","Log transforms","One-hot encoding","Correlation analysis"]'::json,
  '{1,2}',
  'Option B (Normalization) scales numerical features to a specific range, typically between 0 and 1, which helps prevent features with larger values from dominating the learning process. Option C (Log transforms) are used to reduce the skewness of a distribution and stabilize variance, often applied to highly skewed data to make it more Gaussian-like. Option A (Binning) converts numerical features into categorical bins, not primarily for scaling. Option D (One-hot encoding) is for converting categorical variables to numerical, not for scaling numerical features. Option E (Correlation analysis) is for understanding relationships between features, not a transformation technique.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain14_3',
  NOW(),
  46,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_14_2',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_14_m_14_2_108',
  'A data engineering team needs to perform complex data transformations and aggregations on petabytes of data stored in Amazon S3 as part of their machine learning pipeline. Which AWS services are best suited for these large-scale ETL and data preparation tasks? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Amazon Kinesis Data Streams","AWS Glue","Amazon Athena","Amazon SageMaker Processing","AWS Lambda"]'::json,
  '{1,3}',
  'Option B (AWS Glue) is a serverless data integration service that supports large-scale ETL jobs using Apache Spark. It''s ideal for transforming and preparing data from various sources, including Amazon S3, for machine learning. Option D (Amazon SageMaker Processing) provides a managed infrastructure for running data processing workloads, including feature engineering, data validation, and model evaluation. It supports various frameworks like Spark, Scikit-learn, and custom containers, making it suitable for complex transformations on large datasets. Option A (Amazon Kinesis Data Streams) is for real-time streaming, not batch ETL. Option C (Amazon Athena) is an interactive query service, not an ETL tool for complex transformations. Option E (AWS Lambda) is for serverless functions, suitable for smaller, event-driven tasks, but not typically for petabyte-scale batch ETL.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain14_3',
  NOW(),
  47,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_14_2',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_14_m_14_2_109',
  'To improve model performance, a data scientist needs to apply feature scaling techniques to numerical data and encode categorical variables. Which of the following are common feature engineering techniques for these purposes? (Select THREE.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Normalization","Standardization","Handling missing data","One-hot encoding","Feature selection"]'::json,
  '{0,1,3}',
  'Option A (Normalization) scales numerical features to a specific range (e.g., 0 to 1), often using Min-Max scaling. Option B (Standardization) scales numerical features to have a mean of 0 and a standard deviation of 1, often using Z-score scaling. Both are crucial for algorithms sensitive to feature scales. Option D (One-hot encoding) is a common technique to convert categorical variables into a numerical format that machine learning algorithms can understand, by creating new binary features for each category. Option C (Handling missing data) and Option E (Feature selection) are important data preparation steps, but they are distinct from feature scaling and encoding categorical variables.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain14_3',
  NOW(),
  48,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_14_2',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_14_m_14_2_110',
  'A machine learning team is building a new model and wants to ensure that the features used for training are consistently available and reusable across different models and teams. Which AWS service is specifically designed for storing, sharing, and reusing ML features? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Amazon S3","Amazon SageMaker Feature Store","AWS Glue Data Catalog","Amazon DynamoDB","Amazon SageMaker Data Wrangler"]'::json,
  '{1,2}',
  'Option B (Amazon SageMaker Feature Store) is a purpose-built service that makes it easy to create, store, share, and manage features for machine learning. It provides both online and offline stores for low-latency inference and batch training, ensuring feature consistency and reusability. Option C (AWS Glue Data Catalog) can be used to store metadata about features, making them discoverable and providing a centralized schema repository for features stored in Amazon S3 or other data stores. While Amazon S3 (Option A) can store feature data, it doesn''t provide the managed feature serving, versioning, and consistency guarantees of a feature store. Option D (Amazon DynamoDB) is a NoSQL database, not specifically optimized for ML features. Option E (Amazon SageMaker Data Wrangler) is for feature engineering, not for storing and serving features.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain14_3',
  NOW(),
  49,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_14_2',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_14_m_14_2_111',
  'Which of the following are effective strategies for handling class imbalance in a dataset, where one class significantly outnumbers others, to prevent models from being biased towards the majority class? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Normalization","Oversampling","Undersampling","Log transforms","Dimensionality reduction"]'::json,
  '{1,2}',
  'Option B (Oversampling) involves increasing the number of instances in the minority class, for example, by duplicating existing samples or generating synthetic samples (like SMOTE). Option C (Undersampling) involves reducing the number of instances in the majority class to balance the class distribution. Both techniques aim to create a more balanced dataset for training. Option A (Normalization) and Option D (Log transforms) are feature scaling or transformation techniques, not directly for handling class imbalance. Option E (Dimensionality reduction) aims to reduce the number of features, which is unrelated to class imbalance.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain14_3',
  NOW(),
  50,
  NULL,
