) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_14_m_14_2_112',
  'A data scientist is preparing a dataset for a binary classification task where one class is significantly underrepresented. Which techniques can be applied to address this class imbalance? (Select THREE.)',
  'multiple',
  '["SMOTE (Synthetic Minority Over-sampling Technique)","Random oversampling","Feature scaling","Undersampling the majority class","One-hot encoding"]'::json,
  '{0,1,3}',
  'Option A (SMOTE) is a popular oversampling technique that generates synthetic samples for the minority class. Option B (Random oversampling) involves randomly duplicating instances from the minority class. Option D (Undersampling the majority class) involves randomly removing instances from the majority class. These three methods directly address class imbalance by modifying the dataset''s class distribution. Option C (Feature scaling) is used to normalize or standardize numerical features. Option E (One-hot encoding) is used to convert categorical features into a numerical format. These are general feature engineering techniques, not specific to class imbalance.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain14_3',
  NOW(),
  51,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_14_2',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_14_m_14_2_113',
  'Which AWS services can be used to perform feature engineering and data transformation tasks for machine learning, offering both visual interfaces and programmatic options? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Amazon S3","AWS Glue DataBrew","Amazon SageMaker Data Wrangler","Amazon Redshift","Amazon Kinesis Data Streams"]'::json,
  '{1,2}',
  'Option B (AWS Glue DataBrew) is a visual data preparation tool that allows data analysts and data scientists to clean and normalize data without writing code. It offers over 250 pre-built transformations. Option C (Amazon SageMaker Data Wrangler) provides a visual interface to aggregate and prepare data for ML, supporting various transformations and feature engineering steps. It also allows exporting the processing steps as code for programmatic execution. Option A (Amazon S3) is a storage service. Option D (Amazon Redshift) is a data warehouse. Option E (Amazon Kinesis Data Streams) is for streaming data. None of these are primary tools for visual or programmatic feature engineering.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain14_3',
  NOW(),
  52,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_14_2',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_14_m_14_2_114',
  'Which of the following AWS services can be used to perform data cleaning, transformation, and feature engineering tasks, providing a serverless and scalable environment? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Amazon S3","AWS Glue","Amazon SageMaker Data Wrangler","Amazon Athena","Amazon Redshift"]'::json,
  '{1,2}',
  'Option B (AWS Glue) is a serverless data integration service that provides ETL capabilities using Apache Spark, making it highly scalable for data cleaning and transformation. Option C (Amazon SageMaker Data Wrangler) is a feature within SageMaker that offers a visual interface and integrated tools for data preparation and feature engineering, which can be executed in a scalable manner. Option A (Amazon S3) is a storage service. Option D (Amazon Athena) is a query service. Option E (Amazon Redshift) is a data warehouse. While these can be part of a data pipeline, they are not primarily designed for serverless data cleaning and feature engineering.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain14_3',
  NOW(),
  53,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_14_2',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_14_m_14_2_115',
  'A machine learning workflow requires consistent feature generation across training and inference to avoid discrepancies. Which AWS services or practices help maintain this consistency? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Storing features in Amazon S3 without schema","Using SageMaker Feature Store","Implementing SageMaker Pipelines for feature engineering","Manually applying transformations at inference time","Using different libraries for training and inference"]'::json,
  '{1,2}',
  'Option B (Using SageMaker Feature Store) provides a centralized repository for features, ensuring that the same feature definitions and transformations are used for both training and inference, thereby preventing training-serving skew. Option C (Implementing SageMaker Pipelines for feature engineering) allows you to define and orchestrate the entire ML workflow, including feature engineering steps. This ensures that the exact same transformation logic is applied consistently during both training and inference. Option A (Storing features in Amazon S3 without schema) does not guarantee consistency. Option D (Manually applying transformations at inference time) is an anti-pattern (ap-1.2-002) as it is prone to errors and inconsistencies. Option E (Using different libraries for training and inference) would likely lead to inconsistencies and training-serving skew.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain14_3',
  NOW(),
  54,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_14_2',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_14_m_14_2_116',
  'Which of the following are common feature engineering techniques used to prepare numerical data for machine learning models? (Select THREE.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Normalization","One-hot encoding","Binning","Log transforms","Dropping missing values"]'::json,
  '{0,2,3}',
  'Option A (Normalization) scales numerical features to a specific range, typically 0-1. Option C (Binning) converts continuous numerical features into discrete categories or bins. Option D (Log transforms) are used to reduce skewness in numerical data, making distributions more symmetrical. These three are direct feature engineering techniques for numerical data. Option B (One-hot encoding) is for categorical data. Option E (Dropping missing values) is a strategy for handling missing data, not a feature engineering technique.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain14_3',
  NOW(),
  55,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_14_2',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_14_m_14_2_117',
  'A data scientist is preparing a dataset for a classification model and discovers that the dataset has a severe class imbalance. Which strategies can be employed to mitigate the effects of this imbalance on model training? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Applying PCA for dimensionality reduction","Using class weights during model training","Implementing SMOTE (Synthetic Minority Over-sampling Technique)","Standardizing numerical features","Performing correlation analysis"]'::json,
  '{1,2}',
  'Option B (Using class weights during model training) assigns different weights to classes in the loss function, giving more importance to the minority class. Option C (Implementing SMOTE) is a specific oversampling technique that generates synthetic samples for the minority class, effectively balancing the dataset. Option A (Applying PCA for dimensionality reduction) is for reducing the number of features. Option D (Standardizing numerical features) is a scaling technique. Option E (Performing correlation analysis) is for feature selection or understanding relationships. These are not direct strategies for handling class imbalance.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain14_3',
  NOW(),
  56,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_14_2',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_14_m_14_2_118',
  'A data scientist needs to prepare a dataset for a machine learning model, which includes handling outliers, imputing missing values, and deduplicating records. Which AWS services are designed to facilitate these data cleaning tasks? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Amazon Kinesis Data Streams","AWS Glue","Amazon SageMaker Data Wrangler","Amazon Redshift","Amazon S3"]'::json,
  '{1,2}',
  'Option B (AWS Glue) provides robust ETL capabilities, including data cleaning, transformation, and deduplication using Apache Spark. It can be used to programmatically handle missing values and outliers. Option C (Amazon SageMaker Data Wrangler) offers a visual interface with built-in transformations for data cleaning, such as handling missing values, identifying outliers, and deduplicating records, making it user-friendly for data scientists. Option A (Amazon Kinesis Data Streams) is for real-time streaming. Option D (Amazon Redshift) is a data warehouse. Option E (Amazon S3) is a storage service. These are not primary services for data cleaning.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain14_3',
  NOW(),
  57,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_14_2',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_14_m_14_2_119',
  'Which of the following are common feature engineering techniques used to transform categorical variables into a numerical format suitable for machine learning models? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Normalization","One-hot encoding","Label encoding","Standardization","Log transforms"]'::json,
  '{1,2}',
  'Option B (One-hot encoding) converts categorical variables into a binary vector representation, creating new columns for each category. Option C (Label encoding) assigns a unique integer to each category. Both are common methods for converting categorical data into a numerical format that ML algorithms can process. Option A (Normalization), Option D (Standardization), and Option E (Log transforms) are techniques used for transforming numerical features, not categorical ones.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain14_3',
  NOW(),
  58,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_14_2',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_14_m_14_2_120',
  'A machine learning team needs to ensure that the feature engineering logic applied during model training is identical to the logic used during inference to prevent model performance degradation. Which AWS services or practices help enforce this consistency? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Using different data preprocessing scripts for training and inference","Leveraging Amazon SageMaker Feature Store for feature serving","Defining feature transformations within Amazon SageMaker Pipelines","Storing raw data in Amazon S3 for both training and inference","Manually synchronizing feature logic across environments"]'::json,
  '{1,2}',
  'Option B (Leveraging Amazon SageMaker Feature Store for feature serving) provides a centralized, managed repository for features, ensuring that the same features, generated with the same logic, are available for both training and real-time inference. Option C (Defining feature transformations within Amazon SageMaker Pipelines) allows you to codify and orchestrate your ML workflow, including feature engineering steps. This ensures that the exact same transformation logic is executed consistently during both training and deployment for inference. Option A (Using different data preprocessing scripts for training and inference) is an anti-pattern (ap-1.2-002) that directly leads to training-serving skew. Option D (Storing raw data in Amazon S3) is for storage but doesn''t enforce consistent transformation logic. Option E (Manually synchronizing feature logic) is prone to human error and inconsistencies.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain14_3',
  NOW(),
  59,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_14_2',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_14_m_14_2_121',
  'A financial institution is developing a fraud detection system that requires processing historical transaction data and real-time streaming data. The historical data, spanning several years, is stored in Amazon S3 in various formats. The real-time data comes from new transactions. The ML team needs to combine these datasets, perform complex feature engineering, and ensure that the processed features are consistent between training and inference. The solution must handle large volumes of data efficiently and support repeatable feature generation. Which two AWS services are best suited for transforming this data and ensuring feature consistency for both batch and real-time ML workflows? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Amazon SageMaker Data Wrangler for interactive data preparation and feature engineering.","AWS Glue for large-scale ETL jobs and data cataloging.","Amazon Kinesis Data Streams for storing processed features.","Amazon Redshift for performing feature engineering directly on raw data.","Amazon SageMaker Ground Truth for labeling the engineered features."]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'Option A (correct) is Amazon SageMaker Data Wrangler, which provides a visual interface and built-in transformations for interactive data preparation and feature engineering. It can connect to various data sources, including S3, and can export processing jobs to SageMaker Processing or AWS Glue, making it suitable for both batch and real-time feature engineering pipelines. Option B (correct) is AWS Glue, a fully managed ETL service that is ideal for large-scale data integration and transformation tasks. It can process data from S3, perform complex transformations using Spark, and store the results back into S3. AWS Glue Data Catalog is also crucial for managing metadata across various data sources, enabling consistent access for ML workflows. Option C (incorrect) is Amazon Kinesis Data Streams, which is designed for real-time data ingestion and processing, not for storing processed features or performing complex batch feature engineering. While it can be part of a real-time feature pipeline, it''s not the primary service for feature engineering itself. Option D (incorrect) suggests Amazon Redshift for direct feature engineering on raw data. While Redshift can perform SQL-based transformations, it''s primarily a data warehouse for analytical queries, and performing extensive, complex feature engineering on raw, diverse datasets might be more efficiently handled by services like AWS Glue or SageMaker Data Wrangler, especially when dealing with non-SQL transformations. Option E (incorrect) is Amazon SageMaker Ground Truth, which is used for human labeling of datasets, not for automated data transformation or feature engineering.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain14_3',
  NOW(),
  60,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_14_2',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_14_m_14_2_122',
  'A data science team is developing a new model to classify customer support tickets. They have collected millions of text-based tickets, which require extensive preprocessing, including tokenization, stemming, and vectorization. The team needs a scalable and managed solution to perform these transformations, handle missing values, and encode categorical metadata associated with each ticket. The solution must support iterative development of feature engineering pipelines and integrate well with Amazon SageMaker for model training. Which two AWS services are most appropriate for building and managing this data transformation pipeline? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Amazon SageMaker Data Wrangler for visual data preparation and feature engineering.","AWS Glue DataBrew for data profiling, cleaning, and normalization without writing code.","Amazon Kinesis Data Firehose for batch processing and feature encoding.","Amazon Redshift for complex text processing and feature extraction.","Amazon SageMaker Ground Truth for automated text preprocessing."]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'Option A (correct) is Amazon SageMaker Data Wrangler. It provides a visual interface for data preparation and feature engineering, supporting a wide range of transformations, including those for text data (e.g., tokenization). It can connect to various data sources, handle missing values, and export feature engineering pipelines to SageMaker Processing or AWS Glue, making it suitable for iterative development and integration with SageMaker training. Option B (correct) is AWS Glue DataBrew. It is a visual data preparation tool that allows data scientists and analysts to clean and normalize data without writing code. It offers over 250 built-in transformations, including those for handling missing values and encoding categorical variables, making it highly effective for preparing diverse datasets for ML. Option C (incorrect) is Amazon Kinesis Data Firehose. Firehose is a service for delivering real-time streaming data to destinations like S3, Redshift, or Splunk. It is not designed for complex batch text processing, tokenization, or feature engineering. Option D (incorrect) suggests Amazon Redshift for complex text processing. While Redshift can perform some text operations with SQL, it is primarily a columnar data warehouse optimized for structured analytical queries. Complex, iterative text preprocessing and feature extraction are generally more efficiently handled by specialized tools or Spark-based services like AWS Glue. Option E (incorrect) is Amazon SageMaker Ground Truth. Ground Truth is a service for building high-quality training datasets by human labeling, not for automated text preprocessing or feature engineering.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain14_3',
  NOW(),
  61,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_14_2',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_14_m_14_2_123',
  'A marketing analytics team needs to build a model to predict customer churn. They have collected customer demographic data, interaction logs, and purchase history from various sources. Before training the model, the data needs to be cleaned, transformed, and new features engineered (e.g., customer lifetime value, frequency of purchases). The team wants to ensure that the feature engineering process is robust, repeatable, and that the same features are used consistently during both model training and real-time inference. Which two AWS services or approaches should the ML Solutions Architect recommend for this feature engineering process? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Use AWS Glue for large-scale ETL and feature engineering, leveraging its Spark-based processing capabilities.","Implement Amazon SageMaker Data Wrangler for interactive data preparation and feature engineering.","Develop separate, optimized Python scripts for feature engineering in training and inference environments.","Utilize Amazon Redshift to store raw data and perform all feature engineering using SQL queries.","Apply feature scaling and normalization to the entire dataset before splitting into training and test sets."]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'Option A (correct) recommends using AWS Glue. AWS Glue is a fully managed ETL service that can run Spark jobs, making it highly suitable for large-scale data cleaning, transformation, and complex feature engineering on diverse datasets. It can process data from S3 and other sources, and the generated scripts can be version-controlled and reused for consistent feature generation. Option B (correct) suggests implementing Amazon SageMaker Data Wrangler. Data Wrangler provides a visual interface for data preparation and feature engineering, allowing data scientists to quickly build and iterate on transformation flows. It can export these flows as SageMaker Processing jobs or AWS Glue jobs, ensuring that the same logic is applied consistently for both training and inference (preventing training-serving skew). Option C (incorrect) proposes developing separate Python scripts for training and inference. This is an anti-pattern (ap-1.2-002) as it introduces a high risk of training-serving skew, where subtle differences in feature computation logic between environments can lead to degraded model performance in production. Consistency is key. Option D (incorrect) suggests utilizing Amazon Redshift for all feature engineering using SQL queries. While Redshift is powerful for SQL-based transformations, not all feature engineering tasks (e.g., complex string manipulation, custom aggregations beyond standard SQL functions) are optimally performed in a data warehouse, and it might be less flexible or cost-effective than Spark-based solutions like AWS Glue for extensive transformations on raw data. Option E (incorrect) suggests applying feature scaling and normalization to the entire dataset before splitting. This is an anti-pattern (ap-1.2-001) because it causes data leakage; the statistics of the test set would influence the transformation applied to the training set. Scalers should always be fitted *only* on the training data and then applied to both training and test sets.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain14_3',
  NOW(),
  62,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_14_2',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_14_m_14_2_124',
  'A large retail chain is building a recommendation engine. They collect customer purchase history, browsing behavior, and demographic data. The data science team needs to prepare this data for modeling, which involves handling missing values, encoding categorical variables, and normalizing numerical features. They are particularly concerned about ensuring that the feature transformations applied during training are identical to those applied during real-time inference to avoid model degradation. Which two AWS services or approaches should the ML Solutions Architect recommend to achieve consistent feature transformations and manage feature engineering workflows? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Use Amazon SageMaker Feature Store to store and retrieve consistently computed features for training and inference.","Leverage AWS Glue DataBrew for visual data preparation, exporting transformation recipes for consistent application.","Manually implement feature engineering logic in separate AWS Lambda functions for real-time inference and SageMaker Processing jobs for batch training.","Store all raw customer data in Amazon DynamoDB and perform feature engineering directly within the database.","Apply feature scaling to the entire dataset, including both training and test data, before performing the train-test split."]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'Option A (correct) recommends using Amazon SageMaker Feature Store. This service is specifically designed to centralize, store, and serve features consistently for both training and inference workflows. By using Feature Store, the same feature definitions and computed values are available, directly addressing the concern of training-serving skew (anti-pattern: ap-1.2-002). Option B (correct) suggests leveraging AWS Glue DataBrew. DataBrew provides a visual interface for data preparation and feature engineering. Users can define transformation recipes, which can then be exported and applied consistently across different datasets or environments (e.g., batch processing via AWS Glue jobs or SageMaker Processing jobs). This ensures the same logic is used for both training and inference. Option C (incorrect) proposes manually implementing feature engineering logic in separate AWS Lambda functions and SageMaker Processing jobs. This approach is an anti-pattern (ap-1.2-002) because it significantly increases the risk of inconsistencies (training-serving skew) due to potential differences in logic, versions, or configurations between the two separate implementations. Option D (incorrect) suggests storing raw customer data in Amazon DynamoDB and performing feature engineering directly within the database. DynamoDB is a NoSQL key-value store optimized for high-performance, low-latency access, not for complex, large-scale analytical feature engineering. Such operations are better suited for services like AWS Glue or SageMaker Data Wrangler on data stored in S3. Option E (incorrect) suggests applying feature scaling to the entire dataset before splitting. This is an anti-pattern (ap-1.2-001) as it introduces data leakage. The statistics (mean, standard deviation) used for scaling would be derived from the entire dataset, including the test set, making the model evaluation unrealistically optimistic. Scalers should be fitted only on the training data.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain14_3',
  NOW(),
  63,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_14_2',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_14_m_14_2_125',
  'A media company wants to build a recommendation system for its vast catalog of movies and TV shows. They have millions of user interaction logs, movie metadata, and user demographic information. The ML team needs to combine these disparate datasets, perform complex feature engineering (e.g., creating embeddings from movie descriptions, calculating user watch time per genre), and ensure these features are consistently generated and available for both batch training and real-time inference. Which two AWS services are best suited for creating and managing these features efficiently? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Amazon SageMaker Feature Store to centralize, store, and serve features for consistent use across ML workflows.","AWS Glue for large-scale ETL and feature engineering, capable of processing diverse data types.","Amazon Kinesis Data Streams for storing processed features to ensure real-time availability.","Amazon Redshift for performing all feature engineering directly on raw data using complex SQL queries.","Amazon SageMaker Ground Truth for labeling movie genres and user preferences to generate features."]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'Option A (correct) is Amazon SageMaker Feature Store. This service is specifically designed to store, share, and serve ML features consistently across training and inference. It helps prevent training-serving skew (anti-pattern: ap-1.2-002) by ensuring that the same feature definitions and values are used in both environments, which is crucial for a recommendation system. Option B (correct) is AWS Glue. AWS Glue is a fully managed ETL service that can process large volumes of diverse data (structured, semi-structured, unstructured) using Apache Spark. It''s ideal for complex feature engineering tasks like creating embeddings, calculating aggregations, and combining disparate datasets before storing features in a Feature Store or S3. Option C (incorrect) is Amazon Kinesis Data Streams. Kinesis Data Streams is for real-time data ingestion and processing, not for storing processed features for long-term use or for batch feature engineering. While it can be part of a real-time feature pipeline, it''s not the primary storage or processing engine for complex features. Option D (incorrect) suggests Amazon Redshift for performing all feature engineering directly on raw data. While Redshift can handle SQL-based transformations, complex feature engineering involving embeddings from text or custom aggregations across diverse data sources might be more efficiently and flexibly handled by Spark-based services like AWS Glue or SageMaker Processing. Option E (incorrect) is Amazon SageMaker Ground Truth. Ground Truth is a human labeling service used to create high-quality training datasets, not for automated feature engineering or generating features from existing data.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain14_3',
  NOW(),
  64,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_14_2',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_14_m_14_2_126',
  'A media streaming service is developing a new content recommendation model. The data science team has collected extensive user interaction data, including watch history, ratings, and genre preferences. They need to perform feature engineering, such as calculating user-specific genre affinities and content popularity scores. The team is particularly concerned about ensuring that the feature engineering logic is applied consistently during both batch model retraining and real-time inference to avoid performance degradation. Which two AWS services or approaches should the ML Solutions Architect recommend to achieve this consistency? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Use Amazon SageMaker Feature Store to centralize and serve the engineered features for both training and inference.","Implement AWS Glue for batch feature engineering, ensuring the same Spark scripts are used for both training data generation and real-time feature computation.","Develop separate, optimized Lambda functions for real-time feature computation and SageMaker Processing jobs for batch feature generation.","Store all raw user interaction data in Amazon DynamoDB and perform feature engineering directly within the database.","Apply feature scaling and normalization to the entire dataset, including test data, before the train-test split."]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'Option A (correct) recommends using Amazon SageMaker Feature Store. This service is purpose-built to store, manage, and serve ML features consistently for both training and inference. By using Feature Store, the same feature definitions and values are guaranteed across environments, directly preventing training-serving skew (anti-pattern: ap-1.2-002). Option B (correct) suggests implementing AWS Glue for batch feature engineering. AWS Glue allows for the creation of Spark-based ETL jobs. By defining the feature engineering logic once in an AWS Glue job, the same script can be used to generate features for batch training data and, potentially, adapted for real-time feature computation (e.g., via a streaming Glue job or by porting the core logic to a real-time service), ensuring consistency. Option C (incorrect) proposes developing separate Lambda functions and SageMaker Processing jobs. This approach is an anti-pattern (ap-1.2-002) as it significantly increases the risk of training-serving skew due to potential differences in logic, versions, or configurations between the two separate implementations. Consistency is paramount. Option D (incorrect) suggests storing all raw user interaction data in Amazon DynamoDB and performing feature engineering directly within the database. DynamoDB is a NoSQL key-value store optimized for low-latency access to individual items, not for complex, large-scale analytical feature engineering tasks that involve aggregations and transformations across diverse datasets. These are better suited for services like AWS Glue or SageMaker Processing. Option E (incorrect) suggests applying feature scaling and normalization to the entire dataset, including test data, before the train-test split. This is an anti-pattern (ap-1.2-001) because it introduces data leakage. The statistics (mean, standard deviation) used for scaling would be derived from the entire dataset, including the test set, making the model evaluation unrealistically optimistic. Scalers should be fitted only on the training data.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain14_3',
  NOW(),
  65,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_14_2',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_14_m_14_2_127',
  'A biotechnology company is analyzing large-scale genetic sequencing data to identify disease markers. The raw data is stored in Amazon S3, and the data science team needs to perform extensive feature engineering, including dimensionality reduction and normalization, to prepare the data for various ML models. They want to ensure that the feature engineering process is robust, scalable, and that the same transformations are applied consistently during both training and inference. Which two AWS services or approaches should the ML Solutions Architect recommend for building and managing this feature engineering pipeline? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Use AWS Glue for large-scale ETL and feature engineering, leveraging its Spark-based processing capabilities for complex transformations.","Implement Amazon SageMaker Data Wrangler for interactive data preparation and feature engineering, exporting pipelines for operationalization.","Develop custom Python scripts on Amazon EC2 instances for both training and inference feature engineering to maximize flexibility.","Store all raw genetic data in Amazon RDS for PostgreSQL and perform feature engineering using SQL functions.","Apply normalization techniques to the entire dataset before the train-test split to ensure uniform feature scaling."]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'Option A (correct) recommends using AWS Glue. AWS Glue is a fully managed ETL service that can run Spark jobs, making it highly suitable for large-scale data cleaning, transformation, and complex feature engineering, including dimensionality reduction and normalization, on diverse datasets like genetic sequencing data. It ensures scalability and repeatability. Option B (correct) suggests implementing Amazon SageMaker Data Wrangler. Data Wrangler provides a visual interface for data preparation and feature engineering, allowing data scientists to quickly build and iterate on transformation flows. It can connect to S3, perform various transformations, and export these flows as SageMaker Processing jobs or AWS Glue jobs, ensuring that the same logic is applied consistently for both training and inference (preventing training-serving skew). Option C (incorrect) proposes developing custom Python scripts on Amazon EC2 instances for both training and inference feature engineering. While flexible, this approach increases operational overhead and significantly raises the risk of training-serving skew (anti-pattern: ap-1.2-002) due to potential inconsistencies between two separate implementations. Managed services like AWS Glue or SageMaker Data Wrangler are preferred for consistency and scalability. Option D (incorrect) suggests storing all raw genetic data in Amazon RDS for PostgreSQL and performing feature engineering using SQL functions. RDS is a relational database and is not designed for storing or processing large-scale, often semi-structured or unstructured, genetic sequencing data, nor is it optimal for complex feature engineering like dimensionality reduction. Option E (incorrect) suggests applying normalization techniques to the entire dataset before the train-test split. This is an anti-pattern (ap-1.2-001) because it introduces data leakage. The statistics (e.g., mean, standard deviation) used for normalization would be derived from the entire dataset, including the test set, leading to an overoptimistic evaluation of the model''s performance. Scalers should be fitted only on the training data.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain14_3',
  NOW(),
  66,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_14_2',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_14_m_14_2_128',
  'A large retail company is building a personalized product recommendation system. They collect customer purchase history, browsing behavior, and demographic data from various sources. The ML team needs to combine these disparate datasets, perform complex feature engineering (e.g., calculating customer lifetime value, frequency of purchases, product embeddings), and ensure these features are consistently generated and available for both batch training and real-time inference. Which two AWS services or approaches should the ML Solutions Architect recommend for creating and managing these features efficiently? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Amazon SageMaker Feature Store to centralize, store, and serve features for consistent use across ML workflows.","AWS Glue for large-scale ETL and feature engineering, capable of processing diverse data types.","Amazon Kinesis Data Firehose for ingesting raw data and performing real-time feature engineering.","Amazon Redshift for performing all feature engineering directly on raw data using complex SQL queries.","Amazon SageMaker Ground Truth for labeling product categories and customer preferences to generate features."]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'Option A (correct) is Amazon SageMaker Feature Store. This service is purpose-built to store, manage, and serve ML features consistently for both training and inference workflows. By using Feature Store, the same feature definitions and values are guaranteed across environments, directly preventing training-serving skew (anti-pattern: ap-1.2-002). Option B (correct) is AWS Glue. AWS Glue is a fully managed ETL service that can process large volumes of diverse data (structured, semi-structured, unstructured) using Apache Spark. It''s ideal for complex feature engineering tasks like creating embeddings, calculating aggregations, and combining disparate datasets before storing features in a Feature Store or S3. Option C (incorrect) is Amazon Kinesis Data Firehose. Kinesis Data Firehose is primarily for real-time data ingestion and delivery to destinations like S3, Redshift, or Splunk. While it can perform basic transformations, it''s not designed for complex, large-scale feature engineering. Option D (incorrect) suggests Amazon Redshift for performing all feature engineering directly on raw data. While Redshift can handle SQL-based transformations, complex feature engineering involving embeddings from text or custom aggregations across diverse data sources might be more efficiently and flexibly handled by Spark-based services like AWS Glue or SageMaker Processing. Option E (incorrect) is Amazon SageMaker Ground Truth. Ground Truth is a human labeling service used to create high-quality training datasets, not for automated feature engineering or generating features from existing data.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain14_3',
  NOW(),
  67,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_14_2',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

