  '["Completeness","Consistency","Accuracy","Cost-effectiveness","Scalability"]'::json,
  '{0,1,2}',
  'Option A (Completeness) refers to the absence of missing values in the dataset. Option B (Consistency) ensures that data values are uniform and adhere to defined rules (e.g., data types, formats). Option C (Accuracy) verifies that the data correctly represents the real-world information it is intended to describe. These three are fundamental aspects of data quality. Option D (Cost-effectiveness) and Option E (Scalability) are architectural considerations for the ML pipeline or infrastructure, not direct measures of data quality itself.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain14_3',
  NOW(),
  84,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_14_3',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_14_m_14_3_134',
  'When preparing a dataset for machine learning, it is crucial to split the data appropriately for training, validation, and testing. Which data splitting strategies are generally suitable for datasets where the order of observations does not matter or where class distribution needs to be preserved? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Random split","Time-series split","Stratified split","Chronological split","K-fold cross-validation"]'::json,
  '{0,2}',
  'Option A (Random split) is a common strategy where data points are randomly assigned to training, validation, and test sets. It''s suitable when observations are independent and identically distributed. Option C (Stratified split) ensures that the proportion of each class in the target variable is maintained across the training, validation, and test sets, which is crucial for imbalanced datasets. Option B (Time-series split) and Option D (Chronological split) are essentially the same and are specifically for time-series data where the order matters (anti-pattern ap-1.3-001 for non-time-series data). Option E (K-fold cross-validation) is a resampling procedure used to evaluate ML models on a limited data sample, not a primary splitting strategy for train/test sets.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain14_3',
  NOW(),
  85,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_14_3',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_14_m_14_3_135',
  'A machine learning team is preparing a dataset for model training and needs to ensure that the data is free from bias, especially concerning sensitive attributes. Which AWS service can be used to detect and quantify bias in the training data? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
