) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_14_m_14_3_136',
  'To ensure the robustness and fairness of machine learning models, it is critical to detect and measure bias in training datasets. Which of the following are common types of bias that SageMaker Clarify can help detect? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Class imbalance","Feature correlation with sensitive attributes","Network latency bias","Storage cost bias","Compute resource bias"]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'Option A (Class imbalance) refers to an unequal distribution of classes in the target variable, which can lead to models favoring the majority class. SageMaker Clarify can detect and report on this. Option B (Feature correlation with sensitive attributes) identifies if features are unfairly correlated with sensitive attributes (e.g., gender, race), which can lead to discriminatory model outcomes. SageMaker Clarify is designed to measure such correlations. Option C (Network latency bias), Option D (Storage cost bias), and Option E (Compute resource bias) are not types of data or model bias that SageMaker Clarify is designed to detect; they relate to infrastructure performance or cost.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain14_3',
  NOW(),
  87,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_14_3',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_14_m_14_3_137',
  'A data scientist is preparing a dataset for a fraud detection model where fraudulent transactions are rare events. Which data splitting strategies should be considered to ensure that both training and test sets contain a representative number of fraudulent cases? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Random split","Stratified split","Time-series split","Simple random sampling","K-fold cross-validation"]'::json,
  '{1,4}',
  'Option B (Stratified split) is crucial for imbalanced datasets like fraud detection, as it ensures that the proportion of each class (fraudulent vs. non-fraudulent) is maintained in both the training and test sets. This prevents scenarios where a split might accidentally leave out minority class samples from the training set. Option E (K-fold cross-validation), especially stratified k-fold, is a robust evaluation technique that repeatedly splits the data into K folds, ensuring each fold has a similar class distribution, and trains the model K times. Option A (Random split) might result in splits where one set has too few or no minority class samples, especially with rare events. Option C (Time-series split) is for time-dependent data, not general imbalanced classification. Option D (Simple random sampling) is similar to a random split and carries the same risks for imbalanced data.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain14_3',
  NOW(),
  88,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_14_3',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_14_m_14_3_138',
  'Which of the following are key aspects of data quality that should be addressed during the data preparation phase for machine learning? (Select THREE.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Uniqueness","Consistency","Accuracy","Data volume","Network bandwidth"]'::json,
  '{0,1,2}',
  'Option A (Uniqueness) ensures that there are no duplicate records or entries in the dataset that should be unique. Option B (Consistency) refers to the uniformity of data values and adherence to defined rules and formats across the dataset. Option C (Accuracy) means that the data correctly reflects the real-world information it is supposed to represent. These three are fundamental dimensions of data quality. Option D (Data volume) and Option E (Network bandwidth) are characteristics of the data or infrastructure, not direct measures of data quality.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain14_3',
  NOW(),
  89,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_14_3',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_14_m_14_3_139',
  'Which of the following are essential data quality checks that should be performed on a dataset before it is used for machine learning model training? (Select THREE.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Completeness","Consistency","Accuracy","Data storage cost","Compute instance type"]'::json,
  '{0,1,2}',
  'Option A (Completeness) checks for missing values in the dataset. Option B (Consistency) verifies that data values are uniform and adhere to predefined rules and formats. Option C (Accuracy) ensures that the data correctly reflects the real-world information it represents. These three are fundamental data quality dimensions. Option D (Data storage cost) and Option E (Compute instance type) are infrastructure or cost-related concerns, not direct data quality checks.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain14_3',
  NOW(),
  90,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_14_3',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_14_m_14_3_140',
  'Which AWS services can be used to track the lineage of machine learning artifacts, including data sources, processing jobs, and model versions, for enhanced governance and reproducibility? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Amazon S3","Amazon SageMaker ML Lineage Tracking","AWS CloudTrail","AWS Config","Amazon DynamoDB"]'::json,
  '{1,2}',
  'Option B (Amazon SageMaker ML Lineage Tracking) is specifically designed to automatically track the lineage of ML workflow steps, including data sources, processing jobs, training jobs, and model artifacts, providing a comprehensive audit trail. Option C (AWS CloudTrail) records API calls and related events made in your AWS account, which can provide an audit trail of actions taken on ML resources and data, contributing to governance. Option A (Amazon S3) stores artifacts but doesn''t track their lineage. Option D (AWS Config) monitors resource configurations, not ML lineage. Option E (Amazon DynamoDB) is a database, not a lineage tracking service.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain14_3',
  NOW(),
  91,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_14_3',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_14_m_14_3_141',
  'A data science team is preparing a large dataset for an image classification model. They need to annotate images with bounding boxes and labels. Which AWS services can facilitate this data labeling process? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Amazon SageMaker Ground Truth","Amazon SageMaker Data Wrangler","AWS Glue DataBrew","Amazon S3","Amazon Rekognition"]'::json,
  '{0,3}',
  'Option A (Amazon SageMaker Ground Truth) is a fully managed data labeling service that helps build high-quality training datasets for machine learning. It supports various tasks, including image classification and object detection with bounding boxes, using human annotators. Option D (Amazon S3) is essential as it stores the raw images and the labeled output from Ground Truth. Option B (Amazon SageMaker Data Wrangler) and Option C (AWS Glue DataBrew) are for data preparation and feature engineering, not for human-in-the-loop labeling. Option E (Amazon Rekognition) is a pre-trained computer vision service for analyzing images, not for creating custom labeled datasets.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain14_3',
  NOW(),
  92,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_14_3',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_14_m_14_3_142',
  'A healthcare provider is building a machine learning model to predict patient readmission rates. The dataset contains sensitive patient information, including demographics, medical history, and treatment outcomes. Before training the model, the data science team needs to ensure data integrity, detect potential biases in the training data, and prepare the dataset for modeling. They are particularly concerned about fairness and ethical AI. Which two actions should the ML Solutions Architect recommend to address data integrity, detect bias, and prepare the data for modeling in a compliant manner? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Use Amazon SageMaker Clarify to analyze the training dataset for bias before model training.","Implement random data splitting for time-series patient data to ensure diverse training examples.","Utilize Amazon SageMaker Ground Truth to automatically identify and correct data inconsistencies.","Apply data quality checks for completeness, consistency, and accuracy using AWS Glue Data Quality.","Store all sensitive patient data in an unencrypted Amazon S3 bucket for easier access by SageMaker."]'::json,
  '{0,3}',
  'Option A (correct) recommends using Amazon SageMaker Clarify, which is specifically designed to detect bias in ML datasets and models. It can analyze the training data for imbalances and correlations with sensitive attributes (e.g., gender, ethnicity) before model training, which is crucial for ethical AI in healthcare. Option D (correct) suggests applying data quality checks for completeness, consistency, and accuracy using AWS Glue Data Quality. Ensuring high data integrity is fundamental for reliable ML models, and AWS Glue Data Quality allows defining rules and monitoring data quality throughout the ETL pipeline. Option B (incorrect) proposes implementing random data splitting for time-series patient data. This is an anti-pattern (ap-1.3-001) for time-series data, as it can lead to data leakage where future data influences the training set, resulting in an unrealistically optimistic model evaluation. Time-based splits are essential for such data. Option C (incorrect) suggests Amazon SageMaker Ground Truth. Ground Truth is used for human data labeling, not for automatically identifying or correcting data inconsistencies. While human review can help improve data quality, it''s not an automated data integrity solution. Option E (incorrect) recommends storing sensitive patient data in an unencrypted Amazon S3 bucket. This is a severe security and compliance violation, especially for healthcare data (PHI/PII). Data must always be encrypted at rest and in transit, and access strictly controlled.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain14_3',
  NOW(),
  93,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_14_3',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_14_m_14_3_143',
  'A global manufacturing company is developing a predictive maintenance model for its industrial equipment. Sensor data from thousands of machines is collected continuously, generating high-velocity time-series data. The ML team needs to prepare this data for modeling, ensuring that the training, validation, and test datasets are representative and avoid data leakage. They also need to detect any potential bias in the sensor data related to specific equipment types or manufacturing lines. Which two strategies should the ML Solutions Architect recommend for data splitting and bias detection? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Use a time-based splitting strategy for the sensor data to maintain chronological order.","Employ random splitting across the entire dataset to maximize the diversity of training examples.","Utilize Amazon SageMaker Clarify to measure potential bias in the sensor data related to equipment attributes.","Implement oversampling techniques to balance the dataset before splitting for training and testing.","Apply SageMaker Feature Store to automatically split the data and detect anomalies."]'::json,
  '{0,2}',
  'Option A (correct) recommends using a time-based splitting strategy for time-series sensor data. This is crucial to prevent data leakage, where future information could inadvertently influence the training set. Training data should always chronologically precede validation and test data (anti-pattern: ap-1.3-001). Option C (correct) suggests utilizing Amazon SageMaker Clarify. SageMaker Clarify is designed to detect and measure bias in training datasets, which is essential for ensuring fairness in predictive maintenance models, especially if certain equipment types or manufacturing lines might be underrepresented or have different data characteristics. Option B (incorrect) proposes employing random splitting across the entire dataset. For time-series data, random splits are an anti-pattern (ap-1.3-001) because they allow future data to leak into the training set, leading to an overoptimistic evaluation of the model''s performance. Option D (incorrect) suggests implementing oversampling techniques before splitting. While oversampling can help handle class imbalance, it should generally be applied *after* the train-test split on the training data only, to avoid data leakage from the test set into the training set. Applying it before splitting can lead to inflated performance metrics. Option E (incorrect) suggests applying SageMaker Feature Store to automatically split data and detect anomalies. SageMaker Feature Store is for storing, sharing, and serving ML features consistently across training and inference, not for data splitting strategies or automated anomaly detection in raw data. While it ensures feature consistency, it doesn''t handle the splitting methodology itself.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain14_3',
  NOW(),
  94,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_14_3',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_14_m_14_3_144',
  'A large social media company is developing a content moderation model. The model needs to be trained on a massive dataset of user-generated content, which includes text, images, and videos. The data science team has identified that the dataset contains a significant class imbalance, with very few examples of ''harmful'' content compared to ''safe'' content. Additionally, they need to ensure that the model is not biased against certain demographic groups. Which two strategies should the ML Solutions Architect recommend to address class imbalance and detect bias in the training data? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Use Amazon SageMaker Clarify to analyze the training dataset for class imbalance and potential bias related to sensitive attributes.","Apply oversampling techniques (e.g., SMOTE) or undersampling techniques to balance the classes in the training dataset.","Implement random data splitting for the content dataset to ensure an even distribution of harmful content.","Utilize Amazon SageMaker Ground Truth to automatically correct class imbalance by generating synthetic data.","Store all raw content data in Amazon Redshift for efficient bias detection queries."]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'Option A (correct) recommends using Amazon SageMaker Clarify. Clarify is specifically designed to detect and measure bias in ML datasets, including class imbalance and feature correlation with sensitive attributes, which is critical for ensuring fairness in content moderation models. Option B (correct) suggests applying oversampling techniques (e.g., SMOTE) or undersampling techniques. These are standard methods for handling class imbalance in datasets, ensuring that the model receives sufficient examples of minority classes during training to learn effectively. Option C (incorrect) proposes implementing random data splitting. While random splitting is common, it does not inherently address class imbalance and might not ensure an even distribution of minority classes, especially with severe imbalance. Stratified splitting would be more appropriate for maintaining class proportions, but even then, techniques like over/undersampling are needed for true balance. Option D (incorrect) suggests Amazon SageMaker Ground Truth for automatically correcting class imbalance. Ground Truth is a human labeling service; it does not automatically generate synthetic data or correct class imbalance. Its purpose is to create high-quality labels for training. Option E (incorrect) suggests storing all raw content data in Amazon Redshift for efficient bias detection queries. While Redshift can perform queries, it is not the primary service for bias detection in ML datasets. SageMaker Clarify is specifically built for this purpose and can integrate with data stored in S3, which is more cost-effective for large, diverse content datasets.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain14_3',
  NOW(),
  95,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_14_3',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_14_m_14_3_145',
  'A pharmaceutical company is developing a machine learning model to predict drug efficacy based on patient clinical trial data. The dataset contains sensitive patient health information (PHI) and includes both structured and unstructured data. Before model training, the data science team needs to ensure data quality, detect potential biases in the dataset (e.g., related to patient demographics or treatment groups), and prepare the data for modeling while adhering to strict compliance requirements. Which two strategies should the ML Solutions Architect recommend to ensure data integrity and detect bias effectively? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Utilize Amazon SageMaker Clarify to analyze the clinical trial dataset for bias in sensitive attributes and class imbalance.","Implement AWS Glue Data Quality to define and monitor data quality rules for completeness, consistency, and accuracy.","Perform random data splitting on the time-series clinical trial data to ensure a diverse representation of patient outcomes.","Use Amazon SageMaker Ground Truth for automated data cleaning and imputation of missing values in the dataset.","Store all clinical trial data in Amazon RDS for PostgreSQL and rely on its built-in features for bias detection."]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'Option A (correct) recommends utilizing Amazon SageMaker Clarify. Clarify is essential for detecting and measuring bias in sensitive attributes (e.g., demographics, treatment groups) within the clinical trial dataset, which is critical for ensuring fairness and ethical considerations in healthcare ML models. It also helps identify class imbalance. Option B (correct) suggests implementing AWS Glue Data Quality. Data quality is paramount for clinical trial data. AWS Glue Data Quality allows the definition and monitoring of data quality rules for completeness, consistency, and accuracy, ensuring that the data used for model training is reliable and trustworthy. Option C (incorrect) proposes performing random data splitting on time-series clinical trial data. This is an anti-pattern (ap-1.3-001) for time-series data. Random splits can lead to data leakage, where future information is inadvertently included in the training set, resulting in an overly optimistic model evaluation. Time-based splits are required. Option D (incorrect) suggests using Amazon SageMaker Ground Truth for automated data cleaning and imputation. Ground Truth is a human labeling service, not an automated data cleaning or imputation tool. While human review can improve data quality, it''s not its primary function for these specific tasks. Option E (incorrect) suggests storing all clinical trial data in Amazon RDS for PostgreSQL and relying on its built-in features for bias detection. RDS is a relational database and does not have built-in capabilities for detecting ML-specific biases in datasets. Specialized services like SageMaker Clarify are needed for this purpose.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain14_3',
  NOW(),
  96,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_14_3',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_14_m_14_3_146',
  'A financial services company is developing a credit risk assessment model using historical loan application data. The dataset contains sensitive customer information, including income, credit scores, and loan default status. The ML team needs to ensure the data is prepared correctly, potential biases are identified, and data quality is maintained before model training. They are particularly concerned about ensuring fairness in lending decisions and avoiding data leakage during model evaluation. Which two strategies should the ML Solutions Architect recommend for data preparation, bias detection, and integrity? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Utilize Amazon SageMaker Clarify to detect and measure bias in the training dataset related to sensitive attributes like income or demographics.","Implement data quality checks for completeness, consistency, and accuracy using AWS Glue Data Quality.","Apply random data splitting for time-series loan application data to ensure a diverse representation of applicants.","Use Amazon SageMaker Ground Truth for automated imputation of missing credit score values.","Store all raw loan application data in Amazon Redshift and rely on its built-in features for data integrity."]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'Option A (correct) recommends utilizing Amazon SageMaker Clarify. For a credit risk model, it''s crucial to detect and measure bias related to sensitive attributes (e.g., income, demographics) in the training dataset to ensure fairness in lending decisions (anti-pattern: ap-1.3-002). Clarify provides the tools for this analysis. Option B (correct) suggests implementing AWS Glue Data Quality. Maintaining high data quality (completeness, consistency, accuracy) is fundamental for any ML model, especially in financial services. AWS Glue Data Quality allows defining and monitoring rules to ensure the reliability of the loan application data. Option C (incorrect) proposes applying random data splitting for time-series loan application data. This is an anti-pattern (ap-1.3-001) for time-series data, as it can lead to data leakage where future data influences the training set, resulting in an unrealistically optimistic model evaluation. Time-based splits are essential. Option D (incorrect) suggests using Amazon SageMaker Ground Truth for automated imputation. Ground Truth is a human labeling service, not an automated tool for data cleaning or imputation of missing values. Option E (incorrect) suggests storing all raw loan application data in Amazon Redshift and relying on its built-in features for data integrity. While Redshift is a data warehouse, it doesn''t provide specific built-in features for comprehensive data integrity checks or bias detection for ML. AWS Glue Data Quality and SageMaker Clarify are the appropriate services for these tasks.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain14_3',
  NOW(),
  97,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_14_3',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_14_m_14_3_147',
  'A global energy company is developing a machine learning model to predict equipment failures in remote oil rigs. They collect sensor data, maintenance logs, and environmental conditions from thousands of devices. The data is time-series in nature, and the ML team needs to ensure that the data is prepared correctly, potential biases are identified, and data integrity is maintained before model training. They are particularly concerned about ensuring the model''s fairness across different rig locations and avoiding data leakage during evaluation. Which two strategies should the ML Solutions Architect recommend for data splitting and bias detection? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Use a time-based splitting strategy for the time-series sensor data to preserve chronological order and prevent data leakage.","Utilize Amazon SageMaker Clarify to analyze the training dataset for bias related to rig location or equipment type.","Implement random data splitting across the entire dataset to maximize the diversity of training examples.","Apply oversampling techniques to balance the dataset before splitting for training and testing.","Use Amazon SageMaker Ground Truth to automatically identify and correct data inconsistencies in sensor readings."]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'Option A (correct) recommends using a time-based splitting strategy for the time-series sensor data. This is crucial to prevent data leakage, where future information could inadvertently influence the training set. Training data should always chronologically precede validation and test data (anti-pattern: ap-1.3-001). Option B (correct) suggests utilizing Amazon SageMaker Clarify. Clarify is designed to detect and measure bias in ML datasets, which is essential for ensuring fairness in predictive maintenance models, especially if certain rig locations or equipment types might be underrepresented or have different data characteristics (anti-pattern: ap-1.3-002). Option C (incorrect) proposes implementing random data splitting across the entire dataset. For time-series data, random splits are an anti-pattern (ap-1.3-001) because they allow future data to leak into the training set, leading to an overoptimistic evaluation of the model''s performance. Option D (incorrect) suggests applying oversampling techniques before splitting. While oversampling can help handle class imbalance, it should generally be applied *after* the train-test split on the training data only, to avoid data leakage from the test set into the training set. Applying it before splitting can lead to inflated performance metrics. Option E (incorrect) suggests Amazon SageMaker Ground Truth to automatically identify and correct data inconsistencies. Ground Truth is a human labeling service; it does not automatically identify or correct data inconsistencies in sensor readings. While human review can help improve data quality, it''s not an automated data integrity solution.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain14_3',
  NOW(),
  98,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_14_3',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_14_m_14_3_148',
  'Which two capabilities does Amazon SageMaker Clarify provide for detecting bias in machine learning datasets? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Analyze Amazon S3 access patterns for the dataset to infer data quality issues.","Measure class imbalance in the target variable.","Automatically generate synthetic data to balance skewed datasets.","Analyze feature correlation with sensitive attributes.","Visually inspect data distributions for outliers."]'::json,
  '{1,3}',
  'Amazon SageMaker Clarify is a purpose-built service for detecting bias in machine learning datasets and models. Option B (correct) and Option D (correct) are core capabilities of SageMaker Clarify, allowing data scientists to measure class imbalance and analyze feature correlation with sensitive attributes before model training. Option A is incorrect because S3 access patterns are for security and auditing, not data bias detection. Option C is incorrect because SageMaker Clarify focuses on detection and measurement, not automatic generation of synthetic data for mitigation. Option E is incorrect because while data distribution inspection is part of data exploration, SageMaker Clarify provides structured metrics for bias detection rather than just visual inspection for general outliers.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain14_3',
  NOW(),
  99,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_14_3',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

