) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_15_m_15_1_118',
  'A development team needs to build a machine learning model using a popular open-source framework for deep learning, with the flexibility to customize the training script. They also want to leverage a pre-built solution for a common ML task without starting from scratch. Which Amazon SageMaker capabilities or services would best support these requirements? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["SageMaker Training Jobs with custom TensorFlow scripts","SageMaker Ground Truth for data labeling","SageMaker JumpStart for pre-built models and solutions","SageMaker Clarify for bias detection","SageMaker Feature Store for feature management"]'::json,
  '{0,2}',
  'Option A (SageMaker Training Jobs with custom TensorFlow scripts) allows users to train models using popular open-source frameworks like TensorFlow, PyTorch, or scikit-learn by providing their own custom training scripts, offering flexibility and customization. Option C (SageMaker JumpStart for pre-built models and solutions) provides a hub of pre-trained models, notebooks, and solutions for common ML tasks, enabling users to quickly deploy and fine-tune models without extensive development. Option B (SageMaker Ground Truth) is for data labeling. Option D (SageMaker Clarify) is for bias detection and explainability. Option E (SageMaker Feature Store) is for creating, storing, and sharing ML features.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain15_3',
  NOW(),
  34,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_15_1',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_15_m_15_1_119',
  'A startup needs to quickly deploy a machine learning model for sentiment analysis on customer reviews. They have limited ML expertise and want to avoid building a model from scratch. Which Amazon SageMaker features or services can help them achieve this with minimal effort? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Amazon SageMaker JumpStart","AWS Lambda for custom model inference","Amazon SageMaker Ground Truth","Transfer learning with a pre-trained model","Amazon SageMaker Automatic Model Tuning (HPO)"]'::json,
  '{0,3}',
  'Option A (Amazon SageMaker JumpStart) provides a collection of pre-trained models, notebooks, and solutions for common ML tasks, allowing users to quickly deploy models like sentiment analysis without extensive custom development. Option D (Transfer learning with a pre-trained model) allows leveraging knowledge from a model trained on a large dataset for a similar task, which is highly effective for accelerating development and improving performance with limited data or expertise. Option B (AWS Lambda for custom model inference) could be used for inference but doesn''t directly help with model selection or training from scratch. Option C (Amazon SageMaker Ground Truth) is for data labeling. Option E (Amazon SageMaker Automatic Model Tuning (HPO)) is for optimizing hyperparameters of a model, assuming a model is already chosen and being trained.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain15_3',
  NOW(),
  35,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_15_1',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_15_m_15_1_120',
  'A data scientist is working on an unsupervised learning problem to identify natural groupings within a customer dataset for market segmentation. The goal is to discover hidden patterns without any labeled data. Which Amazon SageMaker built-in algorithms are suitable for this type of task? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Linear Learner","k-means","XGBoost","PCA (Principal Component Analysis)","DeepAR"]'::json,
  '{1,3}',
  'Option B (k-means) is a popular unsupervised clustering algorithm used to partition N observations into K clusters, where each observation belongs to the cluster with the nearest mean. This is ideal for identifying natural groupings. Option D (PCA (Principal Component Analysis)) is an unsupervised dimensionality reduction technique that can also be used for exploratory data analysis to identify underlying patterns and reduce noise, which can aid in understanding data structure before or during clustering. Option A (Linear Learner) and Option C (XGBoost) are supervised learning algorithms. Option E (DeepAR) is a supervised algorithm for time series forecasting.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain15_3',
  NOW(),
  36,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_15_1',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_15_m_15_1_121',
  'A company wants to develop a model to predict customer churn based on various customer attributes. They have a dataset with both numerical and categorical features and need a robust, high-performance algorithm. Which Amazon SageMaker built-in algorithms are well-suited for this binary classification problem? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["k-means","XGBoost","Linear Learner","PCA","DeepAR"]'::json,
  '{1,2}',
  'Option B (XGBoost) is a highly efficient and powerful gradient boosting framework that excels in structured data classification and regression tasks, making it an excellent choice for predicting customer churn. Option C (Linear Learner) is a supervised learning algorithm that can perform both classification and regression, capable of handling large datasets and providing good performance for binary classification. Option A (k-means) and Option D (PCA) are unsupervised learning algorithms used for clustering and dimensionality reduction, respectively. Option E (DeepAR) is a time series forecasting algorithm.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain15_3',
  NOW(),
  37,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_15_1',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_15_m_15_1_122',
  'A data science team is starting a new project that requires building a custom deep learning model. They need to choose a machine learning framework that offers extensive libraries for neural networks and is widely supported by the ML community. Which frameworks are officially supported by Amazon SageMaker for custom training scripts? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Apache Spark MLlib","TensorFlow","Scikit-learn","Microsoft Cognitive Toolkit (CNTK)","PyTorch"]'::json,
  '{1,4}',
  'Option B (TensorFlow) is a widely used open-source machine learning framework, particularly popular for deep learning, and is fully supported by Amazon SageMaker for custom training jobs. Option E (PyTorch) is another leading open-source deep learning framework known for its flexibility and ease of use, also fully supported by Amazon SageMaker. Option A (Apache Spark MLlib) is a machine learning library for Spark, not a standalone deep learning framework supported in the same way for custom scripts. Option C (Scikit-learn) is supported for traditional machine learning tasks but not typically for deep learning. Option D (Microsoft Cognitive Toolkit (CNTK)) is a deep learning framework but is not one of the primary, widely supported frameworks for custom scripts on SageMaker.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain15_3',
  NOW(),
  38,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_15_1',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_15_m_15_1_123',
  'A machine learning engineer needs to quickly set up and deploy a pre-trained image classification model without writing extensive code. The goal is to leverage existing, high-quality models and solutions. Which Amazon SageMaker service or feature is specifically designed for this purpose? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Amazon SageMaker JumpStart","Amazon SageMaker Automatic Model Tuning (HPO)","Amazon SageMaker Ground Truth","Transfer learning with pre-trained models","Amazon SageMaker Experiments"]'::json,
  '{0,3}',
  'Option A (Amazon SageMaker JumpStart) provides a collection of pre-trained models, notebooks, and solutions for common ML tasks, enabling users to quickly deploy and fine-tune models without extensive custom development. Option D (Transfer learning with pre-trained models) is a technique where a model trained on a large dataset for a general task is adapted for a specific, related task, significantly reducing the need for large datasets and extensive training from scratch. This is a core concept leveraged by JumpStart. Option B (Amazon SageMaker Automatic Model Tuning (HPO)) is for optimizing hyperparameters, not for initial model selection or quick deployment of pre-trained models. Option C (Amazon SageMaker Ground Truth) is for data labeling. Option E (Amazon SageMaker Experiments) is for tracking ML experiments.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain15_3',
  NOW(),
  39,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_15_1',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_15_m_15_1_124',
  'A team is building a recommendation system and needs to identify similar items based on their features. They have a large dataset of item embeddings and want to find the nearest neighbors for each item efficiently. Which Amazon SageMaker built-in algorithms are suitable for finding similarities or nearest neighbors? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["k-NN (k-Nearest Neighbors)","Linear Learner","XGBoost","k-means","Object Detection"]'::json,
  '{0,3}',
  'Option A (k-NN (k-Nearest Neighbors)) is a non-parametric, instance-based learning algorithm that can be used for both classification and regression, but its core principle is finding the ''k'' closest data points in the feature space, making it directly suitable for finding similar items or nearest neighbors. Option D (k-means) is an unsupervised clustering algorithm that groups similar data points together. While not directly a ''nearest neighbor'' search, the clusters it forms represent groups of similar items, and finding the cluster an item belongs to can imply similarity. Option B (Linear Learner) and Option C (XGBoost) are supervised learning algorithms primarily for classification and regression. Option E (Object Detection) is a computer vision algorithm.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain15_3',
  NOW(),
  40,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_15_1',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_15_m_15_1_125',
  'A large manufacturing company is implementing a predictive maintenance solution using machine learning. They have collected sensor data from various machines, which is a time-series dataset. The goal is to predict equipment failures proactively. The data science team wants to leverage a built-in Amazon SageMaker algorithm that is well-suited for time-series forecasting and can scale efficiently. They also need to consider using a pre-built solution to accelerate development. Which of the following approaches should the team consider? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Utilizing the Amazon SageMaker DeepAR algorithm for time-series forecasting.","Leveraging Amazon SageMaker JumpStart for a pre-built forecasting solution.","Developing a custom recurrent neural network (RNN) from scratch using a generic EC2 instance.","Applying the Amazon SageMaker XGBoost algorithm for its strong performance in classification tasks.","Using the Amazon SageMaker k-means algorithm for clustering similar failure patterns."]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'Option A (correct) is utilizing the Amazon SageMaker DeepAR algorithm. DeepAR is specifically designed for forecasting time series data using recurrent neural networks, making it highly suitable for predictive maintenance with sensor data. Option B (correct) is leveraging Amazon SageMaker JumpStart. JumpStart provides pre-built solutions and models, including forecasting solutions, which can significantly accelerate development and provide a strong baseline for the team''s needs. Option C (incorrect) suggests developing a custom RNN from scratch on a generic EC2 instance. This approach aligns with anti-pattern ap-2.1-001, as SageMaker built-in algorithms like DeepAR are optimized for distributed training and scale on AWS infrastructure, offering higher efficiency and lower operational overhead than a custom implementation. Option D (incorrect) is applying Amazon SageMaker XGBoost. While powerful, XGBoost is primarily for tabular data classification and regression, not specifically optimized for time-series forecasting. Option E (incorrect) is using Amazon SageMaker k-means. K-means is a clustering algorithm, useful for identifying groups, but not directly for forecasting future events like equipment failures.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain15_3',
  NOW(),
  41,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_15_1',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_15_m_15_1_126',
  'A media streaming service is developing a content recommendation system. They have a massive dataset of user viewing history and content metadata. The team wants to use a machine learning model that can handle large-scale training and inference, and they are exploring different frameworks. They also need to consider options for leveraging existing state-of-the-art models to reduce development time. Which of the following framework and model reuse strategies are appropriate for this scenario within Amazon SageMaker? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Using TensorFlow or PyTorch frameworks for custom model development and distributed training.","Leveraging pre-trained models and transfer learning via SageMaker JumpStart.","Implementing a custom algorithm from scratch on a non-SageMaker EC2 instance.","Relying solely on Amazon SageMaker Linear Learner for complex recommendation tasks.","Developing a model using scikit-learn for extremely large-scale, distributed training."]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'Option A (correct) suggests using TensorFlow or PyTorch frameworks. These are leading deep learning frameworks fully supported by Amazon SageMaker, enabling custom model development, distributed training, and handling large-scale data and complex architectures required for recommendation systems. Option B (correct) is leveraging pre-trained models and transfer learning via SageMaker JumpStart. JumpStart provides access to a wide range of pre-trained models from popular model hubs, allowing the team to quickly deploy and fine-tune state-of-the-art models, significantly reducing development time. Option C (incorrect) proposes implementing a custom algorithm from scratch on a non-SageMaker EC2 instance. This is an anti-pattern (ap-2.1-001) as SageMaker provides optimized environments and built-in algorithms for distributed training, making custom implementations on generic EC2 instances less efficient and more operationally intensive. Option D (incorrect) suggests relying solely on Amazon SageMaker Linear Learner. While useful for certain linear problems, it is generally not suitable for the complexity and scale of modern content recommendation systems that often require deep learning. Option E (incorrect) states developing a model using scikit-learn for extremely large-scale, distributed training. Scikit-learn is primarily designed for single-machine, in-memory computation and is not inherently built for distributed training on the scale required by a massive media streaming service.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain15_3',
  NOW(),
  42,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_15_1',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_15_m_15_1_127',
  'A large manufacturing company is developing a quality control system using machine learning to detect defects in product images. They have a massive dataset of annotated images and need to train a robust image classification model. The data science team wants to leverage a built-in Amazon SageMaker algorithm that is highly optimized for image classification tasks and can handle large datasets efficiently. They also want to ensure that they are not unnecessarily building custom algorithms when a suitable SageMaker option exists. Which built-in SageMaker algorithm and approach should they prioritize? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Amazon SageMaker Image Classification algorithm for supervised learning on image data.","Leveraging transfer learning with pre-trained models from SageMaker JumpStart.","Developing a custom Convolutional Neural Network (CNN) from scratch outside of SageMaker.","Using Amazon SageMaker Linear Learner for its simplicity in image classification.","Applying Amazon SageMaker k-means for unsupervised image clustering."]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'Option A (correct) is the Amazon SageMaker Image Classification algorithm. This built-in algorithm is specifically designed for supervised learning on image data, highly optimized for performance and scalability on AWS infrastructure, making it ideal for detecting defects in product images. Option B (correct) is leveraging transfer learning with pre-trained models from SageMaker JumpStart. JumpStart provides access to pre-trained image classification models (e.g., ResNet, VGG) that can be fine-tuned on the company''s specific defect dataset, significantly reducing training time and often achieving higher accuracy than training from scratch. Option C (incorrect) suggests developing a custom CNN from scratch outside of SageMaker. This aligns with anti-pattern ap-2.1-001, as building custom algorithms when SageMaker built-in algorithms or JumpStart solutions satisfy requirements introduces unnecessary development, testing, and maintenance overhead without necessarily improving performance. Option D (incorrect) is using Amazon SageMaker Linear Learner. Linear Learner is suitable for linear regression and classification on tabular data, but it is generally not appropriate for complex image classification tasks that require deep learning architectures. Option E (incorrect) is applying Amazon SageMaker k-means. K-means is an unsupervised clustering algorithm, which groups similar data points. While it could be used for exploratory analysis, it is not a supervised classification algorithm for detecting specific defects.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain15_3',
  NOW(),
  43,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_15_1',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_15_m_15_1_128',
  'A telecommunications company is analyzing customer call records to predict customer satisfaction scores. The dataset contains a mix of numerical and categorical features. The data science team needs to select an appropriate built-in Amazon SageMaker algorithm that is highly effective for tabular data, capable of handling both regression and classification tasks, and known for its performance. They also want to ensure the chosen algorithm can scale for large datasets. Which two SageMaker built-in algorithms are strong candidates for this problem? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Amazon SageMaker XGBoost for its gradient boosting capabilities.","Amazon SageMaker Linear Learner for its efficiency with linear models.","Amazon SageMaker DeepAR for time-series forecasting.","Amazon SageMaker BlazingText for text classification.","Amazon SageMaker k-NN for non-parametric classification and regression."]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'Option A (correct) is Amazon SageMaker XGBoost. XGBoost is a highly efficient and popular gradient boosting algorithm known for its strong performance on tabular data, capable of handling both classification and regression problems, and designed to scale efficiently on SageMaker. It is an excellent choice for predicting customer satisfaction scores. Option B (correct) is Amazon SageMaker Linear Learner. This algorithm is effective for large-scale linear regression and classification problems on tabular data. While simpler than XGBoost, it is highly efficient and can serve as a strong baseline or even a production model for problems where linearity is sufficient, making it a viable candidate. Option C (incorrect) is Amazon SageMaker DeepAR. DeepAR is specifically designed for time-series forecasting, which is not the primary requirement for predicting customer satisfaction from call records. Option D (incorrect) is Amazon SageMaker BlazingText. BlazingText is optimized for text classification and word embeddings, not for general tabular data analysis. Option E (incorrect) is Amazon SageMaker k-NN. While k-NN can be used for classification and regression, it is a non-parametric algorithm that can be computationally expensive for large datasets during inference and may not be the most performant or scalable choice compared to XGBoost or Linear Learner for this type of problem.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain15_3',
  NOW(),
  44,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_15_1',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_15_m_15_1_129',
  'A retail chain wants to optimize its product placement strategy by understanding customer purchasing patterns. They have a large dataset of anonymized transaction records, including items purchased together. The data science team aims to identify natural groupings of products that are frequently bought together without any prior labels. They need to use an Amazon SageMaker built-in algorithm that is suitable for unsupervised learning on this type of transactional data to discover underlying structures. Which two SageMaker built-in algorithms are appropriate for this task? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Amazon SageMaker k-means for clustering similar products or transactions.","Amazon SageMaker PCA (Principal Component Analysis) for dimensionality reduction.","Amazon SageMaker XGBoost for supervised classification of purchasing behavior.","Amazon SageMaker Linear Learner for predicting individual product sales.","Amazon SageMaker Object Detection for identifying products in images."]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'Option A (correct) is Amazon SageMaker k-means. K-means is a popular unsupervised clustering algorithm that can identify natural groupings within data. In this scenario, it can be used to cluster products that are frequently bought together, helping to discover purchasing patterns for product placement. Option B (correct) is Amazon SageMaker PCA (Principal Component Analysis). PCA is an unsupervised dimensionality reduction technique. While not directly for clustering, it can be used as a preprocessing step to reduce the complexity of high-dimensional transactional data, making subsequent clustering (e.g., with k-means) more efficient and effective by focusing on the most significant variations in the data. Option C (incorrect) is Amazon SageMaker XGBoost. XGBoost is a supervised learning algorithm primarily used for classification and regression when target labels are available, which is not the case for discovering natural groupings without prior labels. Option D (incorrect) is Amazon SageMaker Linear Learner. Linear Learner is also a supervised algorithm for linear regression and classification, not suitable for unsupervised pattern discovery. Option E (incorrect) is Amazon SageMaker Object Detection. This algorithm is designed for identifying and localizing objects within images, which is irrelevant to analyzing transactional data for purchasing patterns.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain15_3',
  NOW(),
  45,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_15_1',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_15_m_15_1_130',
  'A media company is developing a content moderation system using machine learning to filter inappropriate images and videos. They have a large dataset of multimedia content and need to train models that can accurately classify content. The data science team wants to leverage pre-built, state-of-the-art models to accelerate their development and ensure high performance without building everything from scratch. Which two Amazon SageMaker capabilities are most suitable for this approach? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Amazon SageMaker JumpStart for pre-trained models and solutions.","Transfer learning with pre-trained models from popular model hubs.","Developing custom image and video classification algorithms on generic EC2 instances.","Using Amazon SageMaker Linear Learner for complex image and video classification.","Amazon SageMaker Automatic Model Tuning (HPO) for selecting the best pre-trained model."]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'Option A (correct) is Amazon SageMaker JumpStart. JumpStart provides a wide range of pre-trained models and end-to-end solutions for various use cases, including image and video classification. This allows the media company to quickly deploy and fine-tune state-of-the-art models, significantly accelerating development. Option B (correct) is transfer learning with pre-trained models from popular model hubs. SageMaker supports using models from various frameworks (TensorFlow, PyTorch, Hugging Face) and model hubs, enabling data scientists to apply transfer learning by fine-tuning pre-trained models on their specific content moderation dataset, which is a highly effective strategy for complex tasks like image and video classification. Option C (incorrect) suggests developing custom algorithms on generic EC2 instances. This aligns with anti-pattern ap-2.1-001, as SageMaker provides optimized environments and built-in algorithms or JumpStart solutions that are more efficient and require less operational overhead than building and managing custom solutions on generic EC2 instances. Option D (incorrect) is using Amazon SageMaker Linear Learner. Linear Learner is suitable for linear classification and regression on tabular data, not for complex image and video classification tasks that typically require deep neural networks. Option E (incorrect) is Amazon SageMaker Automatic Model Tuning (HPO). HPO is for optimizing hyperparameters of a given model, not for selecting or deploying pre-trained models themselves, although it could be used to tune a fine-tuned pre-trained model.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain15_3',
  NOW(),
  46,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_15_1',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_15_m_15_1_131',
  'A social media company is developing a content moderation system that needs to classify user-generated text into various categories (e.g., spam, offensive, news). They have a large corpus of text data and need to train a model that can handle the nuances of natural language. The data science team wants to leverage a built-in Amazon SageMaker algorithm that is highly efficient for text classification and can generate word embeddings. They also want to consider using a pre-built solution to kickstart their development. Which two SageMaker services or algorithms are most appropriate for this task? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Amazon SageMaker BlazingText for fast and scalable text classification and word embeddings.","Amazon SageMaker JumpStart for pre-built NLP models and solutions.","Developing a custom transformer model from scratch on a generic EC2 instance.","Amazon SageMaker XGBoost for tabular text feature classification.","Amazon SageMaker DeepAR for time-series analysis of text frequency."]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'Option A (correct) is Amazon SageMaker BlazingText. BlazingText is a highly optimized built-in algorithm for fast and scalable text classification and learning word embeddings, making it ideal for classifying user-generated text into categories. Option B (correct) is Amazon SageMaker JumpStart. JumpStart provides access to a wide array of pre-trained NLP models (e.g., from Hugging Face) and solutions that can be fine-tuned for content moderation, significantly accelerating development and leveraging state-of-the-art models. Option C (incorrect) suggests developing a custom transformer model from scratch on a generic EC2 instance. This aligns with anti-pattern ap-2.1-001, as SageMaker offers optimized built-in algorithms like BlazingText and pre-trained models via JumpStart, which provide a more efficient and less operationally intensive approach than building custom solutions from scratch on generic infrastructure. Option D (incorrect) is Amazon SageMaker XGBoost. While powerful for tabular data, XGBoost is not specifically designed for the intricacies of natural language processing and would require extensive feature engineering to handle text data effectively. Option E (incorrect) is Amazon SageMaker DeepAR. DeepAR is a time-series forecasting algorithm and is entirely unrelated to text classification or content moderation.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain15_3',
  NOW(),
  47,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_15_1',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_15_m_15_1_132',
  'A large energy company is developing a predictive model for energy consumption based on historical usage patterns, weather data, and time-of-day information. This involves a complex time-series forecasting problem. The data science team wants to leverage a built-in Amazon SageMaker algorithm that is specifically designed for time-series forecasting and can handle multivariate inputs. They also need to ensure that the chosen approach can scale to large datasets. Which two SageMaker built-in algorithms or approaches are most suitable for this scenario? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Amazon SageMaker DeepAR for its deep learning approach to time-series forecasting.","Amazon SageMaker XGBoost for its robust performance on tabular data, including time-series features.","Developing a custom ARIMA model on a generic EC2 instance for precise control.","Amazon SageMaker Linear Learner for its simplicity in forecasting.","Amazon SageMaker BlazingText for analyzing textual descriptions of energy events."]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'Option A (correct) is Amazon SageMaker DeepAR. DeepAR is a built-in SageMaker algorithm specifically designed for forecasting time series data using recurrent neural networks. It can handle multiple related time series and incorporate various exogenous features (like weather and time-of-day), making it highly suitable for complex energy consumption forecasting. Option B (correct) is Amazon SageMaker XGBoost. While not a native time-series model, XGBoost is highly effective for tabular data and can be successfully applied to time-series forecasting by engineering appropriate features (e.g., lag features, rolling averages, Fourier transforms) from the time-series data. Its robust performance and scalability make it a strong candidate for this problem. Option C (incorrect) suggests developing a custom ARIMA model on a generic EC2 instance. This aligns with anti-pattern ap-2.1-001, as SageMaker provides optimized built-in algorithms (like DeepAR) that are more efficient and scalable for distributed training, reducing the need for custom implementations and operational overhead. Option D (incorrect) is Amazon SageMaker Linear Learner. While simple, Linear Learner is generally not powerful enough to capture the complex, non-linear patterns often present in energy consumption time series data. Option E (incorrect) is Amazon SageMaker BlazingText. BlazingText is designed for text classification and word embeddings, which is entirely unrelated to energy consumption forecasting.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain15_3',
  NOW(),
  48,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_15_1',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_15_m_15_1_133',
  'Which of the following Amazon SageMaker built-in algorithms are suitable for common unsupervised learning tasks? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["XGBoost","k-means","Linear Learner","PCA","DeepAR"]'::json,
  '{1,3}',
  'Option B (k-means) is a clustering algorithm used for grouping similar data points, which is a common unsupervised learning task. Option D (PCA - Principal Component Analysis) is a dimensionality reduction technique used to reduce the number of features in a dataset while retaining most of the variance, also an unsupervised task. Option A (XGBoost) and Option C (Linear Learner) are supervised learning algorithms used for classification and regression. Option E (DeepAR) is a supervised algorithm specifically designed for forecasting time series data.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain15_3',
  NOW(),
  49,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_15_1',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

