INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_15_m_15_2_91',
  'When configuring an Amazon SageMaker Training Job, which of the following are key considerations? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Model deployment endpoint","Instance selection","Hyperparameter ranges","Spot Training","Data preprocessing logic"]'::json,
  '{1,3}',
  'Option B (Instance selection) and Option D (Spot Training) are critical configurations for an Amazon SageMaker Training Job. Instance selection determines the compute resources for training, while Spot Training can significantly reduce costs for fault-tolerant workloads. Option A (Model deployment endpoint) is configured after training, for inference. Option C (Hyperparameter ranges) are set for SageMaker Automatic Model Tuning, not a single training job directly. Option E (Data preprocessing logic) is typically part of the training script or a separate data preparation step, not a direct configuration parameter of the training job itself.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain15_3',
  NOW(),
  50,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_15_2',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_15_m_15_2_92',
  'Which of the following are benefits of using Amazon SageMaker Automatic Model Tuning (HPO)? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Provides real-time inference endpoints","Automates the search for optimal hyperparameters","Manages data labeling workflows","Supports various search strategies like Bayesian optimization","Tracks model lineage and versions"]'::json,
  '{1,3}',
  'Option B (Automates the search for optimal hyperparameters) and Option D (Supports various search strategies like Bayesian optimization) are core benefits of Amazon SageMaker Automatic Model Tuning (HPO), which efficiently finds the best hyperparameter combinations. Option A (Provides real-time inference endpoints) is handled by SageMaker Endpoints. Option C (Manages data labeling workflows) is a function of SageMaker Ground Truth. Option E (Tracks model lineage and versions) is managed by SageMaker Experiments and Model Registry.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain15_3',
  NOW(),
  51,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_15_2',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_15_m_15_2_93',
  'A data science team has a long-running training job that can tolerate interruptions and needs to minimize costs. Which two SageMaker training features should they leverage? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["SageMaker Automatic Model Tuning","SageMaker Managed Spot Training","SageMaker Distributed Training","Checkpointing","SageMaker Experiments"]'::json,
  '{1,3}',
  'Option B (SageMaker Managed Spot Training) allows using EC2 Spot Instances, which can significantly reduce training costs. Option D (Checkpointing) enables the training job to save its state periodically, allowing it to resume from the last saved checkpoint if interrupted, which is crucial when using Spot Instances. Option A (SageMaker Automatic Model Tuning) is for hyperparameter optimization, not direct cost minimization for a single job. Option C (SageMaker Distributed Training) is for improving training speed and scale, not primarily cost reduction. Option E (SageMaker Experiments) is for tracking experiments.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain15_3',
  NOW(),
  52,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_15_2',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_15_m_15_2_94',
  'Which of the following are common strategies for distributed training of large machine learning models? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Hyperparameter optimization","Data parallelism","Feature engineering","Model parallelism","Model evaluation"]'::json,
  '{1,3}',
  'Option B (Data parallelism) and Option D (Model parallelism) are the two primary strategies for distributing the training workload of large machine learning models across multiple instances or devices. Data parallelism distributes data, while model parallelism distributes the model itself. Option A (Hyperparameter optimization), Option C (Feature engineering), and Option E (Model evaluation) are distinct phases or concepts in the ML workflow, not strategies for distributed training.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain15_3',
  NOW(),
  53,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_15_2',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_15_m_15_2_95',
  'Which capabilities does Amazon SageMaker Experiments provide for machine learning workflows? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Automate model deployment","Organize and track ML experiments","Manage data storage for training","Compare model performance across different runs","Provide pre-built algorithms"]'::json,
  '{1,3}',
  'Option B (Organize and track ML experiments) and Option D (Compare model performance across different runs) are core functionalities of Amazon SageMaker Experiments, allowing data scientists to manage, compare, and reproduce their machine learning experiments effectively. Option A (Automate model deployment) is handled by SageMaker Endpoints or MLOps pipelines. Option C (Manage data storage for training) is typically done using Amazon S3. Option E (Provide pre-built algorithms) is a feature of SageMaker built-in algorithms and SageMaker JumpStart.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain15_3',
  NOW(),
  54,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_15_2',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_15_m_15_2_96',
  'A data scientist is setting up a SageMaker Automatic Model Tuning job. To ensure efficient convergence and avoid wasting resources, which two practices should they follow? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Use a very wide search space","Use Bayesian optimization","Set a very low number of trials","Narrow hyperparameter ranges based on domain knowledge","Only use random search"]'::json,
  '{1,3}',
  'Option B (Use Bayesian optimization) is an efficient search strategy that intelligently explores the hyperparameter space. Option D (Narrow hyperparameter ranges based on domain knowledge) helps to focus the search on more promising regions, leading to faster convergence and reduced resource consumption. Option A (Use a very wide search space) and Option C (Set a very low number of trials) would lead to sparse coverage and miss optimal configurations, wasting resources. Option E (Only use random search) is a valid strategy but Bayesian optimization is often more efficient for complex search spaces.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain15_3',
  NOW(),
  55,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_15_2',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_15_m_15_2_97',
  'A machine learning team is developing a new model and needs to manage and compare different training runs, including their hyperparameters, metrics, and artifacts. They want to ensure reproducibility and track the lineage of their models. Which Amazon SageMaker services or features are best suited for experiment tracking and management? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Amazon SageMaker Experiments","Amazon SageMaker Model Registry","Amazon SageMaker Debugger","Amazon SageMaker Clarify","Amazon SageMaker Feature Store"]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'Option A (Amazon SageMaker Experiments) is specifically designed to organize, track, compare, and evaluate machine learning experiments, including hyperparameters, metrics, and artifacts, ensuring reproducibility and providing a clear lineage of model development. Option B (Amazon SageMaker Model Registry) allows for cataloging models, managing their versions, and tracking their lineage, which is crucial for managing different training runs and their associated models. Option C (Amazon SageMaker Debugger) is for debugging training jobs in real-time. Option D (Amazon SageMaker Clarify) is for bias detection and explainability. Option E (Amazon SageMaker Feature Store) is for managing and serving machine learning features.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain15_3',
  NOW(),
  56,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_15_2',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_15_m_15_2_98',
  'A data scientist is training a deep learning model on a large dataset using Amazon SageMaker. To optimize the training process, they want to automatically find the best combination of hyperparameters and also reduce the cost of long-running training jobs. Which SageMaker capabilities should they leverage? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["SageMaker Automatic Model Tuning (HPO)","SageMaker Managed Spot Training","SageMaker Debugger","SageMaker Clarify","SageMaker Model Monitor"]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'Option A (SageMaker Automatic Model Tuning (HPO)) automates the process of finding the best version of a model by running many training jobs on a dataset using different hyperparameter combinations, optimizing for a specified objective metric. Option B (SageMaker Managed Spot Training) allows using Amazon EC2 Spot Instances for training jobs, which can significantly reduce training costs by up to 90% compared to On-Demand instances, with SageMaker managing interruptions and resuming jobs from checkpoints. Option C (SageMaker Debugger) is for debugging training jobs. Option D (SageMaker Clarify) is for bias detection and explainability. Option E (SageMaker Model Monitor) is for monitoring deployed models.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain15_3',
  NOW(),
  57,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_15_2',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_15_m_15_2_99',
  'A machine learning engineer is training a large language model on Amazon SageMaker and needs to distribute the training workload across multiple GPU instances to accelerate the process. They are considering strategies to efficiently handle the model''s size and the vast amount of training data. Which distributed training strategies are commonly used in SageMaker for this purpose? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Data parallelism","Model parallelism","Feature engineering parallelism","Hyperparameter parallelism","Inference parallelism"]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'Option A (Data parallelism) is a distributed training strategy where the training data is split across multiple GPUs, and each GPU trains a replica of the model on its subset of data. Gradients are then aggregated to update the model. Option B (Model parallelism) is a distributed training strategy where the model itself is split across multiple GPUs, allowing training of models that are too large to fit on a single GPU. Option C (Feature engineering parallelism) and Option D (Hyperparameter parallelism) are not standard distributed training strategies for model training itself; hyperparameter parallelism is related to HPO. Option E (Inference parallelism) is for distributing inference, not training.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain15_3',
  NOW(),
  58,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_15_2',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_15_m_15_2_100',
  'A data science team is training a new image classification model and needs to prevent overfitting, where the model performs well on training data but poorly on unseen data. Which regularization techniques are commonly employed to address this issue? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["L1 regularization","Batch Normalization","Dropout","Early stopping","Gradient Boosting"]'::json,
  '{0,2}',
  'Option A (L1 regularization) adds a penalty to the loss function proportional to the absolute value of the weights, which encourages sparsity and can lead to feature selection, helping to prevent overfitting. Option C (Dropout) is a regularization technique primarily used in neural networks where randomly selected neurons are ignored during training. This prevents complex co-adaptations on the training data and forces the network to learn more robust features. Option B (Batch Normalization) helps stabilize and accelerate training but is not primarily a regularization technique to prevent overfitting. Option D (Early stopping) is a technique to stop training when validation performance degrades, which is a form of regularization but not a direct ''regularization technique'' in the same category as L1/L2 or dropout. Option E (Gradient Boosting) is an ensemble method for building models, not a regularization technique.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain15_3',
  NOW(),
  59,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_15_2',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_15_m_15_2_101',
  'A data science team is training a new model on Amazon SageMaker and needs to systematically evaluate its performance on different subsets of the data to ensure robust generalization. They want to avoid a single train-test split that might lead to an overly optimistic or pessimistic evaluation. Which cross-validation strategies are commonly used for this purpose? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["K-fold cross-validation","Leave-one-out cross-validation","Bootstrap aggregation (Bagging)","Stratified sampling","Random undersampling"]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'Option A (K-fold cross-validation) is a widely used strategy where the dataset is divided into K folds, and the model is trained K times, each time using K-1 folds for training and one fold for validation. The results are then averaged, providing a more robust estimate of model performance. Option B (Leave-one-out cross-validation) is a special case of K-fold cross-validation where K is equal to the number of data points, meaning each data point is used once as a validation set. This is computationally intensive but provides a very precise estimate. Option C (Bootstrap aggregation (Bagging)) is an ensemble method, not a cross-validation strategy. Option D (Stratified sampling) is a sampling technique used to ensure that each fold in cross-validation has the same proportion of target class labels as the full dataset, but it''s a sampling method, not a cross-validation strategy itself. Option E (Random undersampling) is a technique for handling imbalanced datasets, not a cross-validation strategy.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain15_3',
  NOW(),
  60,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_15_2',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_15_m_15_2_102',
  'A machine learning engineer is fine-tuning a pre-trained deep learning model on Amazon SageMaker for a specific downstream task. They want to ensure the fine-tuning process is efficient and that the model''s performance is optimized for the new dataset. Which strategies are effective for refining pre-trained models? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Freezing early layers and training only later layers","Training all layers from scratch with random initialization","Using a small learning rate for fine-tuning","Applying aggressive data augmentation only to the pre-training dataset","Increasing the complexity of the pre-trained model"]'::json,
  '{0,2}',
  'Option A (Freezing early layers and training only later layers) is a common and effective strategy in transfer learning. Early layers of a pre-trained model often capture generic features (e.g., edges, textures in images) that are useful across many tasks, so freezing them preserves this knowledge. Later layers, which capture more task-specific features, are then fine-tuned on the new dataset. Option C (Using a small learning rate for fine-tuning) is crucial because the pre-trained model has already learned meaningful representations. A small learning rate prevents large updates that could disrupt these learned features, allowing for gradual adaptation to the new task. Option B (Training all layers from scratch with random initialization) defeats the purpose of transfer learning and is equivalent to training a new model, which is inefficient and often performs worse with limited data. Option D (Applying aggressive data augmentation only to the pre-training dataset) is incorrect; data augmentation should be applied to the fine-tuning dataset to increase its diversity. Option E (Increasing the complexity of the pre-trained model) would likely lead to overfitting, especially with a smaller fine-tuning dataset.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain15_3',
  NOW(),
  61,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_15_2',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_15_m_15_2_103',
  'A data scientist needs to train a machine learning model using a custom PyTorch script on Amazon SageMaker. They want to ensure the training job is configured to use appropriate compute resources and can scale efficiently. Which aspects of SageMaker Training Jobs are relevant for setting up this custom training? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Instance selection (e.g., GPU instances)","SageMaker Clarify for bias detection","Distributed training configuration","SageMaker Ground Truth for data labeling","SageMaker Feature Store for feature management"]'::json,
  '{0,2}',
  'Option A (Instance selection (e.g., GPU instances)) is crucial for SageMaker Training Jobs, as it determines the type and number of compute instances (e.g., p3, p4, g5 for GPU-accelerated training) that will run the custom PyTorch script, directly impacting performance and cost. Option C (Distributed training configuration) is essential for scaling training jobs efficiently across multiple instances, especially for large models or datasets, by configuring data parallelism or model parallelism. Option B (SageMaker Clarify for bias detection) is for post-training analysis, not training job setup. Option D (SageMaker Ground Truth for data labeling) is for preparing training data, not configuring the training job itself. Option E (SageMaker Feature Store for feature management) is for managing and serving features, not for setting up a training job.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain15_3',
  NOW(),
  62,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_15_2',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_15_m_15_2_104',
  'A machine learning team is developing a new model and needs to track the performance of different model versions over time in production. They also want to ensure that models can be easily rolled back if performance degrades. Which Amazon SageMaker services or features are essential for managing model versions and monitoring their performance post-deployment? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Amazon SageMaker Model Registry","Amazon SageMaker Model Monitor","Amazon SageMaker Experiments","Amazon SageMaker Automatic Model Tuning (HPO)","Amazon SageMaker Ground Truth"]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'Option A (Amazon SageMaker Model Registry) allows you to catalog models, manage their versions, and track their lineage, making it easy to deploy specific versions and roll back if needed. Option B (Amazon SageMaker Model Monitor) continuously monitors the quality of machine learning models in production, detecting data drift, model drift, and other issues that can degrade performance over time. Option C (Amazon SageMaker Experiments) is for tracking and comparing ML experiments during development, not for managing deployed models. Option D (Amazon SageMaker Automatic Model Tuning (HPO)) is for hyperparameter optimization during training. Option E (Amazon SageMaker Ground Truth) is for data labeling.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain15_3',
  NOW(),
  63,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_15_2',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_15_m_15_2_105',
  'A data scientist is performing hyperparameter tuning for a deep learning model on Amazon SageMaker. They have defined a wide search space for several hyperparameters but are concerned about the computational cost and time required for an exhaustive search. Which strategies can help optimize the hyperparameter tuning process to converge faster to optimal configurations? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Using Bayesian optimization in SageMaker Automatic Model Tuning (HPO)","Narrowing hyperparameter ranges based on domain knowledge","Increasing the number of trials significantly without narrowing ranges","Using a random search strategy for all hyperparameters","Reducing the number of training epochs for each trial"]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'Option A (Using Bayesian optimization in SageMaker Automatic Model Tuning (HPO)) is an efficient strategy that intelligently explores the hyperparameter search space by building a probabilistic model of the objective function. This allows it to converge to optimal configurations much faster than random or grid search, especially with a limited number of trials. Option B (Narrowing hyperparameter ranges based on domain knowledge) helps to focus the search on more promising regions, reducing the overall search space and improving the efficiency of tuning. Option C (Increasing the number of trials significantly without narrowing ranges) can be computationally expensive and may still lead to sparse coverage if the search space is too wide, making it less efficient. Option D (Using a random search strategy for all hyperparameters) is generally more efficient than grid search but less efficient than Bayesian optimization for complex search spaces. Option E (Reducing the number of training epochs for each trial) might lead to underfitting and inaccurate evaluation of hyperparameter combinations, as models might not fully converge.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain15_3',
  NOW(),
  64,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_15_2',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_15_m_15_2_106',
  'A machine learning engineer needs to register different versions of a trained model, track its metadata, and manage its approval status for deployment into production. They also want to ensure that the model''s lineage from training to deployment is maintained. Which Amazon SageMaker services are primarily used for model versioning and lifecycle management? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Amazon SageMaker Model Registry","Amazon SageMaker Experiments","Amazon SageMaker Pipelines","Amazon SageMaker Studio","Amazon SageMaker Feature Store"]'::json,
  '{0,2}',
  'Option A (Amazon SageMaker Model Registry) is specifically designed to catalog models, manage their versions, track metadata, and control their approval status for deployment. It provides a centralized repository for model lifecycle management. Option C (Amazon SageMaker Pipelines) helps create, automate, and manage end-to-end machine learning workflows, including model training, evaluation, and registration into the Model Registry, thus maintaining the model''s lineage. Option B (Amazon SageMaker Experiments) is for tracking and comparing individual ML experiments during development. Option D (Amazon SageMaker Studio) is an IDE for ML development but not a service for model versioning itself. Option E (Amazon SageMaker Feature Store) is for managing and serving features.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain15_3',
  NOW(),
  65,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_15_2',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_15_m_15_2_107',
  'A data scientist is training a large deep learning model on Amazon SageMaker and wants to reduce the model''s size and complexity without significantly impacting its performance. This is crucial for efficient deployment to edge devices or environments with limited resources. Which model optimization techniques are suitable for this purpose? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Model pruning","Hyperparameter tuning","Knowledge distillation","Ensemble learning","Data augmentation"]'::json,
  '{0,2}',
  'Option A (Model pruning) involves removing redundant connections or neurons from a neural network, reducing its size and computational requirements while aiming to maintain performance. Option C (Knowledge distillation) is a technique where a smaller, ''student'' model is trained to mimic the behavior of a larger, more complex ''teacher'' model. This allows the student model to achieve comparable performance with fewer parameters. Option B (Hyperparameter tuning) is for optimizing model performance, not reducing its size. Option D (Ensemble learning) combines multiple models to improve overall performance but typically increases complexity and size. Option E (Data augmentation) is a technique to increase the diversity of the training data, used to improve generalization and prevent overfitting, not to reduce model size.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain15_3',
  NOW(),
  66,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_15_2',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_15_m_15_2_108',
  'A large e-commerce company is developing a new recommendation engine using Amazon SageMaker. They have a massive dataset of customer interactions and product features, requiring distributed training across multiple GPU instances. The data scientists need to efficiently track various model iterations, hyperparameter configurations, and evaluation metrics to compare performance over time and ensure reproducibility. They also want to identify the best performing model configurations without extensive manual effort. Which AWS services and features should they leverage to meet these requirements for tracking and optimization? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Amazon SageMaker Experiments for tracking and comparing training runs.","Amazon SageMaker Automatic Model Tuning (HPO) for hyperparameter optimization.","Amazon SageMaker Debugger to monitor training job resource utilization.","Amazon SageMaker Clarify for post-training bias detection.","Amazon SageMaker JumpStart for pre-built models and solutions."]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'Option A (correct) is Amazon SageMaker Experiments, which allows data scientists to organize, track, compare, and evaluate machine learning experiments. This is crucial for managing various model iterations, hyperparameter configurations, and evaluation metrics for reproducibility and comparison. Option B (correct) is Amazon SageMaker Automatic Model Tuning (HPO), which automates the process of finding the best version of a model by running many training jobs on a dataset using algorithm and hyperparameter ranges that are specified. This directly addresses the need to identify the best performing model configurations without extensive manual effort. Option C (incorrect) is Amazon SageMaker Debugger, which is used for analyzing training job issues like vanishing gradients or overfitting during training, not primarily for tracking experiments or hyperparameter optimization. Option D (incorrect) is Amazon SageMaker Clarify, which is used for detecting potential bias in ML models and explaining their predictions, typically post-training, not for tracking or tuning. Option E (incorrect) is Amazon SageMaker JumpStart, which provides pre-built solutions and models for common use cases, not for tracking custom training runs or optimizing hyperparameters.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain15_3',
  NOW(),
  67,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_15_2',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_15_m_15_2_109',
  'A retail company is developing a demand forecasting model for its inventory management system. The data scientists are experimenting with various model architectures and hyperparameters. They need to efficiently explore a wide range of hyperparameter values to find the optimal combination that minimizes forecasting error, while also being mindful of compute costs for these extensive experiments. Which Amazon SageMaker features should they utilize to achieve efficient hyperparameter optimization and cost savings? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Amazon SageMaker Automatic Model Tuning (HPO) with Bayesian optimization.","Amazon SageMaker Managed Spot Training for the tuning jobs.","Manually running individual training jobs for each hyperparameter combination.","Using a very wide search space with a small number of trials in HPO.","Deploying the model to a production endpoint before hyperparameter tuning."]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'Option A (correct) is Amazon SageMaker Automatic Model Tuning (HPO) with Bayesian optimization. HPO automates the search for the best hyperparameters, and Bayesian optimization is an efficient strategy that intelligently explores the search space, converging faster to optimal configurations than random or grid search, which is crucial for minimizing forecasting error efficiently. Option B (correct) is Amazon SageMaker Managed Spot Training for the tuning jobs. Managed Spot Training allows HPO jobs to run on EC2 Spot Instances, which can significantly reduce compute costs (up to 90%) for long-running or numerous training and tuning jobs, directly addressing the need for cost savings. Option C (incorrect) suggests manually running individual training jobs. This is highly inefficient, time-consuming, and prone to human error, directly contradicting the goal of efficient optimization. Option D (incorrect) proposes using a very wide search space with a small number of trials in HPO. This is an anti-pattern (ap-2.2-001) as it produces sparse coverage and is likely to miss optimal configurations, leading to suboptimal model performance and wasted compute resources. Option E (incorrect) involves deploying the model to a production endpoint before hyperparameter tuning. This is an incorrect workflow; hyperparameter tuning should occur before production deployment to ensure the best possible model is deployed.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain15_3',
  NOW(),
  68,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_15_2',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_15_m_15_2_110',
  'A large-scale image recognition project requires training deep learning models on vast datasets. The training jobs are computationally intensive and can run for extended periods, sometimes days. The data science team is looking for ways to significantly reduce the cost of these long-running training jobs while ensuring that progress is not lost if an instance is interrupted. Which Amazon SageMaker features and instance types should be considered to achieve cost-effectiveness and resilience? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Amazon SageMaker Managed Spot Training with checkpointing enabled.","Utilizing Amazon EC2 GPU instances (e.g., p3, p4, g5) for accelerated training.","Running training jobs on On-Demand EC2 instances without any specific cost optimization.","Using AWS Trainium instances for models not specifically optimized for them.","Limiting the training duration to avoid high costs, potentially sacrificing model accuracy."]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'Option A (correct) is Amazon SageMaker Managed Spot Training with checkpointing enabled. Managed Spot Training leverages EC2 Spot Instances, which can reduce training costs by up to 90%. With checkpointing, interrupted jobs can resume from the last saved state, ensuring progress is not lost, directly addressing both cost-effectiveness and resilience for long-running jobs (anti-pattern ap-2.2-002). Option B (correct) is utilizing Amazon EC2 GPU instances (e.g., p3, p4, g5). These instances are specifically designed to accelerate deep learning training, significantly reducing the overall training time for computationally intensive tasks, which indirectly contributes to cost-effectiveness by completing jobs faster. Option C (incorrect) suggests running training jobs on On-Demand EC2 instances without specific cost optimization. This approach would be significantly more expensive than using Spot Instances and does not offer any cost savings. Option D (incorrect) is using AWS Trainium instances for models not specifically optimized for them. AWS Trainium is designed for high-performance deep learning training, but its benefits are realized when models are specifically optimized for its architecture. Using it without optimization might not yield the expected performance or cost benefits compared to readily available GPU instances. Option E (incorrect) proposes limiting training duration to avoid high costs, potentially sacrificing model accuracy. This is a suboptimal approach as it directly compromises the model''s performance, which is usually a primary goal of ML development.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain15_3',
  NOW(),
  69,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_15_2',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_15_m_15_2_111',
  'A manufacturing company is developing a machine learning model to predict equipment failures based on sensor data. The training process involves large datasets and requires significant computational resources, often running for several hours. The data science team wants to ensure that the training jobs are cost-effective and resilient to potential interruptions, especially for long-running experiments. Which two Amazon SageMaker features should they implement to optimize cost and ensure job continuity? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Enable Amazon SageMaker Managed Spot Training for cost reduction.","Configure checkpointing for training jobs to save model state periodically.","Utilize On-Demand instances exclusively to guarantee uninterrupted training.","Reduce the dataset size to shorten training time, potentially impacting model accuracy.","Manually restart interrupted training jobs from the beginning."]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'Option A (correct) is to enable Amazon SageMaker Managed Spot Training. This feature allows training jobs to run on EC2 Spot Instances, which can significantly reduce compute costs (up to 90%) compared to On-Demand instances, directly addressing the cost-effectiveness requirement. Option B (correct) is to configure checkpointing for training jobs. When used with Managed Spot Training, checkpointing ensures that the model''s state is saved periodically. If a Spot Instance is interrupted, SageMaker can resume the training job from the last saved checkpoint, ensuring job continuity and preventing loss of progress. Option C (incorrect) suggests utilizing On-Demand instances exclusively. While On-Demand instances guarantee uninterrupted training, they are significantly more expensive than Spot Instances and do not address the cost-effectiveness requirement, which is a key constraint. This aligns with anti-pattern ap-2.2-002. Option D (incorrect) proposes reducing the dataset size. This might shorten training time and reduce costs, but it would likely compromise model accuracy and generalization, which is an unacceptable trade-off for a robust predictive model. Option E (incorrect) is to manually restart interrupted training jobs from the beginning. This is highly inefficient, wastes compute resources, and does not leverage SageMaker''s built-in resilience features like checkpointing.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain15_3',
  NOW(),
  70,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_15_2',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_15_m_15_2_112',
  'A pharmaceutical company is developing a model to predict the efficacy of new drug compounds. The dataset is complex, with many features, and the training process is resource-intensive. The data scientists need to find the optimal combination of hyperparameters to maximize model efficacy, but they are concerned about the computational cost and time associated with exhaustively searching a wide range of parameters. They also want to ensure that the search process is efficient. Which two Amazon SageMaker features should they leverage for efficient and cost-effective hyperparameter tuning? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Amazon SageMaker Automatic Model Tuning (HPO) with Bayesian optimization strategy.","Amazon SageMaker Managed Spot Training for running the tuning jobs.","Manually configuring and running each hyperparameter combination as separate training jobs.","Setting a very broad hyperparameter search space with a minimal number of trials.","Using SageMaker Debugger to identify optimal hyperparameters during training."]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'Option A (correct) is Amazon SageMaker Automatic Model Tuning (HPO) with Bayesian optimization strategy. HPO automates the search for optimal hyperparameters, and Bayesian optimization is an intelligent search strategy that efficiently explores the hyperparameter space, converging faster to the best configurations compared to random or grid search, thus saving computational resources and time. Option B (correct) is Amazon SageMaker Managed Spot Training for running the tuning jobs. By using Spot Instances, the cost of running numerous training jobs during hyperparameter tuning can be significantly reduced (up to 90%), making the process much more cost-effective, especially for resource-intensive tasks. Option C (incorrect) suggests manually configuring and running each hyperparameter combination. This is highly inefficient, time-consuming, and prone to error, directly contradicting the goal of efficient tuning. Option D (incorrect) proposes setting a very broad hyperparameter search space with a minimal number of trials. This is an anti-pattern (ap-2.2-001) because it leads to sparse coverage of the search space, making it highly unlikely to find the optimal hyperparameter combination and wasting compute resources on ineffective trials. Option E (incorrect) is using SageMaker Debugger to identify optimal hyperparameters. SageMaker Debugger is used for analyzing and debugging training job issues (e.g., vanishing gradients, overfitting), not for hyperparameter optimization.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain15_3',
  NOW(),
  71,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_15_2',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_15_m_15_2_113',
  'A financial services company is developing a credit risk assessment model. After training, the data science team observes that the model performs exceptionally well on the training data but poorly on new, unseen customer applications, indicating overfitting. They need to implement effective strategies to reduce this overfitting and improve the model''s ability to generalize to new data. Which two regularization techniques or strategies are commonly used to mitigate overfitting in machine learning models? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["L1 (Lasso) regularization to penalize large coefficients and encourage sparsity.","Dropout regularization to randomly ignore a subset of neurons during training.","Increasing the complexity of the model by adding more hidden layers and neurons.","Collecting more data as the primary and immediate solution without other attempts.","Reducing the number of training epochs significantly without monitoring validation loss."]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'Option A (correct) is L1 (Lasso) regularization. L1 regularization adds a penalty equal to the absolute value of the magnitude of coefficients to the loss function, which encourages sparsity in the model by driving some coefficients to zero, effectively performing feature selection and reducing overfitting. Option B (correct) is Dropout regularization. Dropout is a technique where randomly selected neurons are ignored during training. This prevents complex co-adaptations on the training data, forcing the network to learn more robust features and improving generalization. Option C (incorrect) suggests increasing the complexity of the model. This would exacerbate overfitting, making the model even more specialized to the training data and less capable of generalizing to new data. Option D (incorrect) proposes collecting more data as the primary and immediate solution. While more data can sometimes help, it is often costly and time-consuming. As per anti-pattern ap-2.3-001, regularization techniques and early stopping are faster and cheaper first responses to overfitting. Option E (incorrect) is reducing the number of training epochs significantly without monitoring validation loss. This could lead to underfitting if the model doesn''t train long enough to learn the underlying patterns, and it''s not a controlled way to address overfitting.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain15_3',
  NOW(),
  72,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_15_2',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_15_m_15_2_114',
  'A pharmaceutical company is training a deep learning model for drug discovery, which involves extremely large datasets and complex neural network architectures. The training jobs are very long-running and require substantial GPU resources. The data science team needs to ensure that the training process is as cost-efficient as possible without compromising the ability to complete the training, even if instances are temporarily unavailable. Which two Amazon SageMaker features should they leverage to achieve this balance of cost-effectiveness and resilience? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Amazon SageMaker Managed Spot Training with robust checkpointing.","Utilizing distributed training strategies (data parallelism or model parallelism).","Running all training jobs on On-Demand instances to avoid any interruptions.","Manually monitoring Spot Instance interruptions and restarting jobs from scratch.","Limiting the model complexity to reduce training duration and resource needs."]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'Option A (correct) is Amazon SageMaker Managed Spot Training with robust checkpointing. Managed Spot Training allows training jobs to run on EC2 Spot Instances, which offer significant cost savings (up to 90%). With checkpointing, the model''s state is saved periodically, enabling SageMaker to automatically resume training from the last checkpoint if an instance is interrupted, ensuring resilience and job completion. Option B (correct) is utilizing distributed training strategies, such as data parallelism or model parallelism. For extremely large datasets and complex models, distributed training across multiple instances can significantly reduce the overall training time. While not directly a cost-saving mechanism in itself, faster completion of training jobs reduces the total compute hours, indirectly contributing to cost-effectiveness, and is essential for handling the scale of the problem. Option C (incorrect) suggests running all training jobs on On-Demand instances. This approach avoids interruptions but is significantly more expensive and contradicts the goal of cost-efficiency (anti-pattern ap-2.2-002). Option D (incorrect) is manually monitoring Spot Instance interruptions and restarting jobs from scratch. This is highly inefficient, labor-intensive, and defeats the purpose of SageMaker''s managed features like checkpointing. Option E (incorrect) proposes limiting model complexity. While this might reduce resource needs, it could compromise the model''s ability to accurately discover new drug compounds, which is a critical objective for a pharmaceutical company.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain15_3',
  NOW(),
  73,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_15_2',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_15_m_15_2_115',
  'A research team is experimenting with various deep learning models for medical image analysis. They are running numerous training jobs with different architectures and hyperparameter configurations. The team needs a systematic way to track all aspects of their experiments, including code versions, datasets, hyperparameters, and evaluation metrics, to ensure full reproducibility and facilitate collaboration. They also want to easily compare the performance of different model variants. Which two Amazon SageMaker services are essential for achieving comprehensive experiment tracking and management? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Amazon SageMaker Experiments for tracking and comparing ML training runs.","Amazon SageMaker Model Registry for cataloging and versioning trained models.","Amazon SageMaker Debugger for real-time monitoring of training job metrics.","Amazon SageMaker Automatic Model Tuning (HPO) for finding optimal hyperparameters.","Amazon SageMaker Clarify for analyzing model bias and explainability."]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'Option A (correct) is Amazon SageMaker Experiments. This service is specifically designed for tracking and comparing machine learning experiments, allowing the team to log code versions, datasets, hyperparameters, and evaluation metrics, which is crucial for reproducibility and collaboration. Option B (correct) is Amazon SageMaker Model Registry. While Experiments tracks the *runs*, the Model Registry is used to catalog and version trained models, making it easier to manage and deploy specific model artifacts that resulted from successful experiments. This complements experiment tracking by providing a central repository for deployable models. Option C (incorrect) is Amazon SageMaker Debugger. Debugger focuses on identifying and fixing issues *during* model training, such as vanishing gradients or overfitting, not on comprehensive experiment tracking or comparison across runs. Option D (incorrect) is Amazon SageMaker Automatic Model Tuning (HPO), which automates the search for optimal hyperparameters, but is not the primary tool for general experiment tracking and management. Option E (incorrect) is Amazon SageMaker Clarify, which is used for detecting bias and explaining model predictions, not for tracking experiment metadata.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain15_3',
  NOW(),
  74,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_15_2',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_15_m_15_2_116',
  'An autonomous vehicle company is training perception models that process vast amounts of sensor data (camera, LiDAR, radar). These models are extremely complex, requiring distributed training across many GPU instances. The data science team needs to ensure that the training process is efficient and scalable, leveraging the full power of multiple machines. They also want to use a framework that is widely adopted for deep learning and supports advanced distributed training features. Which two Amazon SageMaker capabilities and framework choices are most appropriate for this high-performance distributed training scenario? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Utilizing distributed training strategies like data parallelism or model parallelism within SageMaker.","Choosing deep learning frameworks such as TensorFlow or PyTorch for model development.","Running training jobs on a single, powerful EC2 GPU instance without distributed configurations.","Developing custom distributed training logic using generic Python scripts on multiple EC2 instances.","Relying on Amazon SageMaker Linear Learner for its simplicity in perception tasks."]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'Option A (correct) is utilizing distributed training strategies like data parallelism or model parallelism within SageMaker. For vast datasets and complex models in autonomous driving, distributed training is essential to scale efficiently across many GPU instances, significantly reducing training time. SageMaker provides optimized implementations for these strategies. Option B (correct) is choosing deep learning frameworks such as TensorFlow or PyTorch. These are industry-standard deep learning frameworks that are fully supported by Amazon SageMaker and offer robust features for building complex perception models and leveraging distributed training capabilities. Option C (incorrect) suggests running training jobs on a single, powerful EC2 GPU instance. While powerful, a single instance would be insufficient for the scale and complexity of autonomous vehicle perception models and would not leverage distributed training benefits. Option D (incorrect) proposes developing custom distributed training logic using generic Python scripts on multiple EC2 instances. This approach would introduce significant operational overhead and complexity compared to using SageMaker''s managed distributed training features, which are optimized for performance and ease of use. Option E (incorrect) is relying on Amazon SageMaker Linear Learner. Linear Learner is a simple linear model and is entirely unsuitable for the complex, non-linear patterns required for autonomous vehicle perception tasks.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain15_3',
  NOW(),
  75,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_15_2',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_15_m_15_2_117',
  'A retail company is developing a personalized marketing campaign model. They have a large dataset of customer demographics, purchase history, and browsing behavior. The data scientists are experimenting with various model configurations and hyperparameters to maximize conversion rates. They need a robust system to track all aspects of their experiments, including different datasets, code versions, hyperparameters, and evaluation metrics, to ensure reproducibility and facilitate collaboration among team members. Which two Amazon SageMaker services are crucial for managing and tracking these ML experiments effectively? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Amazon SageMaker Experiments for comprehensive experiment tracking and comparison.","Amazon SageMaker Model Registry for versioning and managing trained model artifacts.","Amazon SageMaker Debugger for real-time monitoring of training job issues.","Amazon SageMaker Clarify for post-training bias analysis.","Amazon SageMaker JumpStart for deploying pre-built marketing models."]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'Option A (correct) is Amazon SageMaker Experiments. This service is specifically designed for comprehensive experiment tracking, allowing data scientists to organize, track, compare, and evaluate machine learning experiments, including logging code versions, datasets, hyperparameters, and evaluation metrics. This ensures reproducibility and facilitates collaboration. Option B (correct) is Amazon SageMaker Model Registry. While Experiments tracks the *runs*, the Model Registry provides a central repository to catalog, version, and manage trained model artifacts. This is crucial for managing the output of successful experiments, making it easier to deploy and share specific model versions. Option C (incorrect) is Amazon SageMaker Debugger. Debugger is used for identifying and fixing issues *during* model training, such as vanishing gradients or overfitting, not for general experiment tracking or management. Option D (incorrect) is Amazon SageMaker Clarify, which is used for detecting bias and explaining model predictions, not for tracking experiment metadata. Option E (incorrect) is Amazon SageMaker JumpStart, which provides pre-built models and solutions, not a service for managing and tracking custom experiments.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain15_3',
  NOW(),
  76,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_15_2',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_15_m_15_2_118',
  'A large-scale predictive maintenance project involves training deep learning models on sensor data from thousands of industrial machines. The training jobs are computationally intensive and require distributed processing across multiple GPU instances. The data science team needs to ensure that the training process is efficient, scalable, and can handle large volumes of data by distributing the workload. They also want to use a popular deep learning framework. Which two Amazon SageMaker capabilities and framework choices are most appropriate for this scenario? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Implementing data parallelism or model parallelism strategies for distributed training.","Using deep learning frameworks like PyTorch or TensorFlow for model development.","Training the model on a single, powerful GPU instance to simplify the setup.","Developing a custom distributed training solution using MPI on generic EC2 instances.","Relying on Amazon SageMaker k-means for its simplicity in distributed processing."]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'Option A (correct) is implementing data parallelism or model parallelism strategies for distributed training. For large datasets and complex models, distributed training is crucial to efficiently utilize multiple GPU instances, significantly reducing training time and handling the scale of the problem. SageMaker provides optimized support for these strategies. Option B (correct) is using deep learning frameworks like PyTorch or TensorFlow. These are industry-standard deep learning frameworks that are fully supported by Amazon SageMaker and offer robust features for building complex models and leveraging distributed training capabilities. Option C (incorrect) suggests training the model on a single, powerful GPU instance. While powerful, a single instance would be insufficient for the scale and complexity of training on data from thousands of industrial machines, and would not leverage distributed training benefits. Option D (incorrect) proposes developing a custom distributed training solution using MPI on generic EC2 instances. This approach would introduce significant operational overhead and complexity compared to using SageMaker''s managed distributed training features, which are optimized for performance and ease of use. Option E (incorrect) is relying on Amazon SageMaker k-means. K-means is an unsupervised clustering algorithm, not a deep learning framework or a general solution for distributed training of complex predictive models.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain15_3',
  NOW(),
  77,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_15_2',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_15_m_15_2_119',
  'A data science team is training a new deep learning model using Amazon SageMaker Training Jobs. The training runs for several hours, and they need to find the optimal hyperparameters. To minimize training costs while ensuring the job can complete even if interrupted, which two features should they leverage? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Utilize SageMaker Managed Spot Training, ensuring checkpointing is enabled for fault tolerance.","Configure SageMaker Automatic Model Tuning (HPO) with Bayesian optimization for efficient hyperparameter search.","Select Amazon EC2 GPU instances from the p3 family, which are optimized for deep learning workloads.","Implement a custom distributed training strategy using AWS Step Functions to manage worker nodes.","Use SageMaker Debugger to analyze training progress and identify potential bottlenecks or overfitting."]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'Option A (Utilize SageMaker Managed Spot Training with checkpointing) is a highly effective way to minimize training costs, as it leverages EC2 Spot Instances, which can be significantly cheaper than On-Demand instances. With checkpointing enabled, interrupted jobs can resume from the last saved state, ensuring completion. Option B (Configure SageMaker Automatic Model Tuning (HPO) with Bayesian optimization) helps find optimal hyperparameters efficiently, reducing the overall number of training jobs and thus costs, especially when using Bayesian optimization which intelligently explores the search space. Option C (Select Amazon EC2 GPU instances from the p3 family) is about instance type selection for performance, not directly about minimizing cost or ensuring job completion if interrupted. Option D (Implement a custom distributed training strategy using AWS Step Functions) would increase operational overhead and development effort, which is not cost-effective compared to SageMaker''s built-in capabilities. Option E (Use SageMaker Debugger) is for analyzing and debugging training jobs, not for cost optimization or fault tolerance.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain15_3',
  NOW(),
  78,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_15_2',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

