INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_16_m_16_2_113',
  'What are the key benefits of using Infrastructure as Code (IaC) for managing Amazon SageMaker resources, such as endpoints and training jobs? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Repeatability, ensuring consistent deployments across environments","Version control, allowing changes to infrastructure to be tracked and rolled back","Manual deployments, which introduce inconsistencies and are difficult to audit","Reduced compute costs for inference, which is an outcome of optimization, not IaC itself","Real-time inference, which is a type of deployment, not a benefit of IaC"]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'Option A (Repeatability) is a core benefit of IaC, as it allows you to deploy the same infrastructure configuration consistently across development, staging, and production environments. Option B (Version control) enables tracking of all infrastructure changes, facilitating collaboration, auditing, and easy rollback to previous states. Option C (Manual deployments) is the opposite of IaC and introduces inconsistencies. Option D (Reduced compute costs) is a potential outcome of optimized infrastructure, not a direct benefit of IaC. Option E (Real-time inference) describes a deployment type, not an advantage of IaC.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain16_3',
  NOW(),
  22,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_16_2',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_16_m_16_2_114',
  'An ML engineer needs to automate the creation and configuration of Amazon SageMaker endpoints and associated resources in a reproducible manner across different environments. (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["AWS CDK, which allows defining cloud infrastructure using familiar programming languages","AWS CodePipeline, an orchestration service for continuous delivery pipelines","AWS CloudFormation, a service for modeling and provisioning AWS resources as code","Amazon EventBridge, a serverless event bus for connecting applications","Manual console operations, which are prone to human error and lack version control for infrastructure"]'::json,
  '{0,2}',
  'Option A (AWS CDK) and Option C (AWS CloudFormation) are both excellent choices for defining and provisioning SageMaker resources as code. AWS CloudFormation provides the underlying declarative language, while AWS CDK offers a higher-level abstraction using familiar programming languages to generate CloudFormation templates. Option B (AWS CodePipeline) is for orchestrating CI/CD workflows, not defining infrastructure. Option D (Amazon EventBridge) is an event bus. Option E (Manual console operations) is an anti-pattern for reproducible deployments.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain16_3',
  NOW(),
  23,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_16_2',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_16_m_16_2_115',
  'Which statements accurately describe the role of Docker in deploying custom ML models to Amazon SageMaker? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["It provides a standardized packaging format for code and dependencies, ensuring portability","It enables environment consistency across development, testing, and production environments","It automatically optimizes models for specific hardware platforms, a function of SageMaker Neo","It manages model versions and approval workflows, which is handled by SageMaker Model Registry","It serves as a real-time inference endpoint, which is a function of SageMaker hosting"]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'Option A (It provides a standardized packaging format for code and dependencies, ensuring portability) is a primary role of Docker, allowing ML models and their environments to be self-contained. Option B (It enables environment consistency across development, testing, and production environments) is another key benefit, preventing ''it works on my machine'' issues. Option C (It automatically optimizes models for specific hardware platforms) describes SageMaker Neo. Option D (It manages model versions and approval workflows) describes SageMaker Model Registry. Option E (It serves as a real-time inference endpoint) describes a SageMaker hosting feature.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain16_3',
  NOW(),
  24,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_16_2',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_16_m_16_2_116',
  'What are the primary advantages of storing custom ML model container images in Amazon ECR for use with Amazon SageMaker? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Secure and highly available storage for Docker images","Seamless integration with Amazon SageMaker for deployment","Automatic model retraining based on data drift, which is a feature of SageMaker Pipelines","Model versioning and lineage tracking, primarily handled by SageMaker Model Registry","Real-time inference hosting capabilities, provided by SageMaker endpoints"]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'Option A (Secure and highly available storage for Docker images) is a core benefit of Amazon ECR, providing robust storage for your container artifacts. Option B (Seamless integration with Amazon SageMaker for deployment) allows SageMaker to easily pull and deploy container images directly from ECR. Option C (Automatic model retraining) is a feature of SageMaker Pipelines. Option D (Model versioning and lineage tracking) is handled by SageMaker Model Registry. Option E (Real-time inference hosting capabilities) is a function of SageMaker endpoints.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain16_3',
  NOW(),
  25,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_16_2',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_16_m_16_2_117',
  'To ensure consistent and repeatable deployments of their Amazon SageMaker inference endpoints, a company mandates the use of Infrastructure as Code. Which tools fulfill this requirement? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["AWS CloudFormation, a service for declarative resource provisioning","AWS CodePipeline, an orchestration service for continuous delivery","AWS CDK, for defining cloud infrastructure using programming languages","Amazon EventBridge, a serverless event bus","SageMaker Model Registry, for managing model versions and metadata"]'::json,
  '{0,2}',
  'Option A (AWS CloudFormation) and Option C (AWS CDK) are the primary Infrastructure as Code (IaC) tools on AWS. CloudFormation allows you to define your infrastructure in templates, while CDK provides a higher-level, code-based approach to generate these templates. Both enable consistent and repeatable deployments. Option B (AWS CodePipeline) orchestrates CI/CD processes but doesn''t define the infrastructure itself. Option D (Amazon EventBridge) is an event bus. Option E (SageMaker Model Registry) is for model governance, not infrastructure definition.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain16_3',
  NOW(),
  26,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_16_2',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_16_m_16_2_118',
  'Which of the following are key benefits of using AWS CloudFormation for managing Amazon SageMaker resources? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Declarative resource management, defining desired state rather than steps","Rollback capabilities, allowing easy reversion to previous stable states","Automatic model compilation for optimized inference, a feature of SageMaker Neo","Serverless inference hosting, which is a SageMaker deployment option","Real-time data streaming, a capability of services like Amazon Kinesis"]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'Option A (Declarative resource management) is a fundamental benefit of CloudFormation, where you specify the desired state of your infrastructure, and CloudFormation handles the provisioning. Option B (Rollback capabilities) allows you to revert your stack to a previous stable state if an update fails, ensuring infrastructure stability. Option C (Automatic model compilation) is a feature of SageMaker Neo. Option D (Serverless inference hosting) is a SageMaker deployment type. Option E (Real-time data streaming) is a data processing capability, not a CloudFormation benefit.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain16_3',
  NOW(),
  27,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_16_2',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_16_m_16_2_119',
  'Which AWS services can be used to provision and manage Amazon SageMaker resources as infrastructure as code? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["AWS CloudFormation","AWS Cloud Development Kit (CDK)","AWS CodeBuild","AWS CodePipeline","Amazon EventBridge"]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'Option A (AWS CloudFormation) and Option B (AWS Cloud Development Kit (CDK)) are the primary AWS services for defining and provisioning infrastructure as code, including Amazon SageMaker resources. CloudFormation uses declarative templates, while CDK allows defining infrastructure using familiar programming languages. Option C (AWS CodeBuild) is a fully managed build service that compiles source code, runs tests, and produces software packages, but it does not directly provision infrastructure. Option D (AWS CodePipeline) is a continuous delivery service that automates release pipelines, orchestrating deployments but not defining the infrastructure itself. Option E (Amazon EventBridge) is a serverless event bus that connects application components, often used for triggering ML pipelines, but not for defining infrastructure.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain16_3',
  NOW(),
  28,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_16_2',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_16_m_16_2_120',
  'Which of the following are benefits of using AWS CloudFormation or AWS CDK for managing Amazon SageMaker resources? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Enabling version control and tracking changes to infrastructure","Automating the provisioning and updating of SageMaker resources","Reducing the computational cost of model training jobs","Automatically retraining models based on data drift","Enhancing data security for Amazon S3 model artifacts"]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'Option A (Enabling version control and tracking changes to infrastructure) is a key benefit of Infrastructure as Code (IaC) tools like AWS CloudFormation and AWS CDK, allowing teams to manage infrastructure changes with the same rigor as application code. Option B (Automating the provisioning and updating of SageMaker resources) is the core purpose of IaC, ensuring consistent and repeatable deployments. Option C (Reducing the computational cost of model training jobs) is related to instance selection and optimization, not directly to IaC. Option D (Automatically retraining models based on data drift) is a function of ML pipeline orchestration and monitoring, not IaC. Option E (Enhancing data security for Amazon S3 model artifacts) is achieved through S3 bucket policies, encryption, and IAM, which can be *configured* by IaC but is not a direct benefit of IaC itself.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain16_3',
  NOW(),
  29,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_16_2',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_16_m_16_2_121',
  'A data science team has deployed an Amazon SageMaker real-time inference endpoint that experiences highly variable traffic throughout the day. The team wants to ensure high availability and cost-efficiency. Which two configurations should they implement? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Configure Application Auto Scaling for the SageMaker endpoint","Define minimum and maximum instance counts for the endpoint","Use Amazon SageMaker Batch Transform for all inference requests","Manually scale endpoint instances based on daily traffic reports","Deploy a multi-model endpoint without any auto-scaling policies"]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'Option A (Configure Application Auto Scaling for the SageMaker endpoint) is crucial for handling variable traffic, allowing the endpoint to automatically adjust its capacity. Option B (Define minimum and maximum instance counts for the endpoint) works in conjunction with Application Auto Scaling to set boundaries for scaling, ensuring cost control and preventing over-provisioning or under-provisioning. Option C (Use Amazon SageMaker Batch Transform for all inference requests) is incorrect because Batch Transform is designed for large, non-interactive datasets and is not suitable for real-time inference with immediate prediction requirements (anti-pattern ap-3.1-001). Option D (Manually scale endpoint instances based on daily traffic reports) would lead to high operational overhead and potential downtime during unexpected traffic spikes, failing to meet the high availability and cost-efficiency goals (anti-pattern ap-3.1-002). Option E (Deploy a multi-model endpoint without any auto-scaling policies) might offer efficiency for hosting multiple models, but without auto-scaling, it would still suffer from the same issues of over- or under-provisioning for variable traffic.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain16_3',
  NOW(),
  30,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_16_2',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

-- ─────────────────────────────────────────
