INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_17_m_17_2_93',
  'A data science team is deploying a new Amazon SageMaker endpoint for real-time inference. They need to monitor the operational health and performance of the endpoint to ensure it meets service level agreements. Which of the following Amazon CloudWatch metrics are directly relevant for monitoring the *operational health* of a SageMaker endpoint? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Invocations","ModelQuality","CPUUtilization","Latency","BiasDrift"]'::json,
  '{0,3}',
  'Option A (Invocations) and Option D (Latency) are direct Amazon CloudWatch metrics provided by Amazon SageMaker endpoints that indicate operational health and performance. Invocations measure the number of requests processed, and Latency measures the time taken to process requests. Option B (ModelQuality) and Option E (BiasDrift) are metrics typically monitored by Amazon SageMaker Model Monitor, which focuses on the quality and fairness of the model''s predictions, not directly the operational health of the endpoint infrastructure itself. Option C (CPUUtilization) is an infrastructure metric, but for SageMaker endpoints, higher-level metrics like Invocations and Latency are more indicative of the ML solution''s operational health from a service perspective, as SageMaker manages the underlying infrastructure. While CPU utilization can be monitored, it''s not as direct for ''operational health'' of the *ML solution* as invocation counts and latency, which are ML-specific performance indicators.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain17_3',
  NOW(),
  1,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_17_2',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_17_m_17_2_94',
  'A Machine Learning Engineering team wants to optimize the cost of their Amazon SageMaker inference endpoints, which experience variable traffic throughout the day. Which strategies can help reduce costs for these production endpoints? (Select THREE.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Implementing auto-scaling policies based on utilization","Using Amazon SageMaker Inference Recommender for optimal instance types","Manually scaling down endpoints during off-peak hours","Configuring SageMaker Model Monitor to detect data drift","Deploying endpoints on AWS Fargate for serverless inference"]'::json,
  '{0,1,2}',
  'Option A (Implementing auto-scaling policies based on utilization) is a highly effective strategy to optimize costs for variable traffic, ensuring resources scale up and down as needed. Option B (Using Amazon SageMaker Inference Recommender for optimal instance types) helps identify the most cost-effective instance types for a given model and latency/throughput requirements, preventing over-provisioning. Option C (Manually scaling down endpoints during off-peak hours) is a direct way to reduce costs, especially for predictable traffic patterns, though it requires operational effort. Option D (Configuring SageMaker Model Monitor to detect data drift) is crucial for model quality and maintenance but does not directly optimize infrastructure costs. Option E (Deploying endpoints on AWS Fargate for serverless inference) is incorrect because Amazon SageMaker endpoints run on EC2 instances or SageMaker''s serverless inference, not directly on AWS Fargate for typical SageMaker real-time inference.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain17_3',
  NOW(),
  2,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_17_2',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_17_m_17_2_95',
  'A company needs to gain better visibility into their AWS machine learning costs and ensure resources are allocated appropriately across different projects. Which AWS services or features are primarily used for monitoring and managing ML-related costs? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["AWS Budgets","AWS CloudTrail","AWS Cost Explorer","Amazon CloudWatch Logs","Amazon SageMaker Model Monitor"]'::json,
  '{0,2}',
  'Option A (AWS Budgets) allows setting custom budgets to track costs and receive alerts when actual or forecasted costs exceed predefined thresholds, which is essential for cost management. Option C (AWS Cost Explorer) provides tools to visualize, understand, and manage AWS costs and usage over time, including filtering by service or tag to analyze ML costs. Option B (AWS CloudTrail) is for auditing API calls and user activity, not primarily for cost monitoring. Option D (Amazon CloudWatch Logs) is for collecting and monitoring logs, not directly for cost management. Option E (Amazon SageMaker Model Monitor) is for monitoring the quality and performance of ML models in production, not for infrastructure cost management.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain17_3',
  NOW(),
  3,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_17_2',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_17_m_17_2_96',
  'A Machine Learning team wants to ensure their SageMaker training jobs and inference endpoints are right-sized for performance and cost. Which AWS services can assist in recommending optimal resource configurations? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["AWS Compute Optimizer","Amazon SageMaker Inference Recommender","AWS CloudFormation","AWS Systems Manager","Amazon CloudWatch Alarms"]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'Option A (AWS Compute Optimizer) helps optimize costs and performance for various AWS resources, including EC2 instances that SageMaker training jobs and endpoints utilize, by recommending optimal instance types. Option B (Amazon SageMaker Inference Recommender) is specifically designed to help choose the best instance type and configuration for SageMaker inference endpoints based on performance and cost benchmarks. Option C (AWS CloudFormation) is an infrastructure as code service for provisioning resources, not for optimization recommendations. Option D (AWS Systems Manager) provides operational insights and automation, but not direct resource optimization recommendations in this context. Option E (Amazon CloudWatch Alarms) are for alerting based on metrics, not for recommending resource configurations.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain17_3',
  NOW(),
  4,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_17_2',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_17_m_17_2_97',
  'To effectively manage and track resource usage and costs for different machine learning projects, a company needs to implement a robust tagging strategy. Which of the following are primary benefits of applying tags to AWS resources used for ML workloads? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Enabling cost allocation and reporting","Automating model retraining schedules","Implementing fine-grained access control with IAM policies","Improving data quality for SageMaker Model Monitor","Facilitating resource organization and searchability"]'::json,
  '{0,4}',
  'Option A (Enabling cost allocation and reporting) is a primary benefit of tagging, allowing organizations to categorize and track costs by project, team, or environment using AWS Cost Explorer. Option E (Facilitating resource organization and searchability) is also a key benefit, as tags help in identifying, grouping, and searching for resources, especially in large AWS environments. Option B (Automating model retraining schedules) is related to ML operations but not directly a benefit of tagging. Option C (Implementing fine-grained access control with IAM policies) can use tags, but the primary mechanism is IAM policies themselves, not tagging as a direct benefit. Option D (Improving data quality for SageMaker Model Monitor) is unrelated to tagging; Model Monitor focuses on the statistical properties of data.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain17_3',
  NOW(),
  5,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_17_2',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_17_m_17_2_98',
  'A data scientist is running a series of Amazon SageMaker training jobs and wants to ensure cost efficiency, especially for non-critical experiments that can tolerate interruptions. Which approaches can help reduce the cost of these training jobs? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Utilizing Amazon SageMaker Spot Instances","Configuring SageMaker Model Monitor for data quality","Using Amazon SageMaker Managed Spot Training","Implementing SageMaker ML Lineage Tracking","Enabling SageMaker Debugger for performance profiling"]'::json,
  '{0,2}',
  'Option A (Utilizing Amazon SageMaker Spot Instances) is a direct way to reduce costs for fault-tolerant training jobs, as Spot Instances offer significant discounts compared to On-Demand instances. Option C (Using Amazon SageMaker Managed Spot Training) further enhances cost savings by automatically managing Spot Instance interruptions and resuming training from checkpoints, making it easier to leverage Spot Instances. Option B (Configuring SageMaker Model Monitor for data quality) is for monitoring deployed models, not for optimizing training job costs. Option D (Implementing SageMaker ML Lineage Tracking) is for tracking metadata and lineage, not for cost optimization. Option E (Enabling SageMaker Debugger for performance profiling) helps optimize model training performance and debugging, which can indirectly save costs by reducing training time, but it''s not a direct cost-saving mechanism like Spot Instances.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain17_3',
  NOW(),
  6,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_17_2',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_17_m_17_2_99',
  'A machine learning team is setting up Amazon CloudWatch Alarms for their SageMaker endpoints to proactively detect issues. Which types of issues can be effectively detected and alerted on using CloudWatch Alarms directly integrated with SageMaker endpoint metrics? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["High inference latency","Model bias drift","Low invocation rate (indicating traffic drop)","Data quality issues in input features","High CPU utilization of the underlying instance"]'::json,
  '{0,2}',
  'Option A (High inference latency) and Option C (Low invocation rate) are direct Amazon CloudWatch metrics available for SageMaker endpoints. CloudWatch Alarms can be configured to trigger when these metrics cross a defined threshold, indicating performance degradation or a drop in traffic. Option B (Model bias drift) and Option D (Data quality issues in input features) are detected by Amazon SageMaker Model Monitor, which then publishes its own metrics to CloudWatch, but these are not direct SageMaker endpoint metrics. Option E (High CPU utilization of the underlying instance) is an infrastructure metric that can be monitored via CloudWatch, but for SageMaker endpoints, higher-level metrics like Invocations and Latency are more indicative of the ML solution''s operational health from a service perspective.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain17_3',
  NOW(),
  7,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_17_2',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_17_m_17_2_100',
  'When optimizing the cost of Amazon SageMaker real-time inference endpoints, it''s crucial to select the right instance type. Which factors are important to consider when choosing an instance type for a SageMaker endpoint to balance cost and performance? (Select THREE.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Required throughput (inferences per second)","Desired latency (response time)","Model size and memory requirements","The number of SageMaker Studio users","The region of your S3 data lake"]'::json,
  '{0,1,2}',
  'Option A (Required throughput) and Option B (Desired latency) are critical performance requirements that directly influence the choice of instance type, as larger or more powerful instances can handle higher throughput and lower latency. Option C (Model size and memory requirements) dictates the minimum memory an instance must have to load and serve the model, impacting the instance family and size. Option D (The number of SageMaker Studio users) is irrelevant to endpoint instance type selection; it relates to development environment access. Option E (The region of your S3 data lake) is relevant for data transfer costs and latency for training data, but not directly for selecting the inference endpoint instance type itself, assuming the model artifact is already in SageMaker''s region.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain17_3',
  NOW(),
  8,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_17_2',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_17_m_17_2_101',
  'A machine learning team needs to monitor the performance of their SageMaker endpoints and identify bottlenecks. Which types of metrics and tools are most effective for this purpose? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Endpoint latency metrics in Amazon CloudWatch","Data quality metrics from SageMaker Model Monitor","Throughput metrics in Amazon CloudWatch","Model bias metrics from SageMaker Clarify","VPC Flow Logs for network traffic analysis"]'::json,
  '{0,2}',
  'Option A (Endpoint latency metrics in Amazon CloudWatch) directly measures the response time of the endpoint, which is a key indicator of performance and potential bottlenecks. Option C (Throughput metrics in Amazon CloudWatch) measures the number of requests processed per unit of time, another critical performance metric. Option B (Data quality metrics from SageMaker Model Monitor) and Option D (Model bias metrics from SageMaker Clarify) are related to model quality and fairness, not directly to the operational performance or bottlenecks of the endpoint infrastructure. Option E (VPC Flow Logs for network traffic analysis) provides network traffic details but is generally too low-level for direct endpoint performance monitoring, which is better served by CloudWatch''s endpoint-specific metrics.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain17_3',
  NOW(),
  9,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_17_2',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_17_m_17_2_102',
  'Which Amazon CloudWatch metrics are most indicative of potential cost optimization opportunities for an Amazon SageMaker real-time inference endpoint that is currently over-provisioned? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["CPUUtilization and MemoryUtilization","ModelLatency and Invocations","GPUUtilization and DiskReadBytes","DiskQueueDepth and NetworkBytesIn","VariantName and EndpointConfigName"]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'Option A (CPUUtilization and MemoryUtilization) are crucial infrastructure metrics that, when consistently low, indicate over-provisioning and potential for cost optimization through right-sizing. Option B (ModelLatency and Invocations) are also important. Low Invocations combined with low CPU/Memory can signal over-provisioning, while ModelLatency is a performance metric that needs to be balanced with cost. Option C (GPUUtilization and DiskReadBytes) are relevant for specific instance types or workloads, but CPU/Memory are more general indicators of over-provisioning. Option D (DiskQueueDepth and NetworkBytesIn) are lower-level infrastructure metrics less directly tied to the *potential for cost optimization due to over-provisioning* compared to utilization. Option E (VariantName and EndpointConfigName) are dimensions, not metrics, used to filter monitoring data.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain17_3',
  NOW(),
  10,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_17_2',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_17_m_17_2_103',
  'Which AWS services and features can be leveraged to monitor the operational health and resource utilization of Amazon SageMaker endpoints, enabling proactive identification of performance bottlenecks or under-utilization? (Select THREE.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Amazon CloudWatch metrics","Amazon CloudWatch Alarms","Amazon CloudWatch Dashboards","AWS Secrets Manager","Amazon SageMaker ML Lineage Tracking"]'::json,
  '{0,1,2}',
  'Option A (Amazon CloudWatch metrics) provides raw data points for various aspects of SageMaker endpoint performance and resource usage (e.g., CPUUtilization, Invocations, ModelLatency). Option B (Amazon CloudWatch Alarms) allows setting thresholds on these metrics to trigger notifications or automated actions when performance deviates. Option C (Amazon CloudWatch Dashboards) provides a customizable visual interface to monitor these metrics and alarms in a consolidated view. Option D (AWS Secrets Manager) is for managing secrets and credentials, not for operational monitoring. Option E (Amazon SageMaker ML Lineage Tracking) is for tracking the lineage of ML artifacts, not for real-time operational health or resource utilization monitoring.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain17_3',
  NOW(),
  11,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_17_2',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_17_m_17_2_104',
  'To proactively identify underutilized Amazon SageMaker endpoint instances for right-sizing, which types of metrics should be primarily observed to determine if an instance type is larger than necessary? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["CPUUtilization and MemoryUtilization","ModelLatency and InvocationError","DataQualityViolations and BiasDrift","DiskReadBytes and NetworkBytesOut","EndpointStatus and VariantStatus"]'::json,
  '{0,3}',
  'Option A (CPUUtilization and MemoryUtilization) are direct indicators of how much compute and memory resources an instance is actually using. Consistently low values suggest over-provisioning. Option D (DiskReadBytes and NetworkBytesOut) can also indicate underutilization if the workload is not I/O or network-bound, suggesting a smaller instance might suffice. Option B (ModelLatency and InvocationError) are performance and error metrics, which indicate if the endpoint is performing well or failing, but not directly if the instance itself is over-sized. Option C (DataQualityViolations and BiasDrift) are metrics from SageMaker Model Monitor, focused on model quality and data integrity, not infrastructure utilization. Option E (EndpointStatus and VariantStatus) are status indicators, not performance or utilization metrics.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain17_3',
  NOW(),
  12,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_17_2',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_17_m_17_2_105',
  'What are key benefits of implementing auto-scaling for Amazon SageMaker real-time inference endpoints in a production environment? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Automatically adjusting instance count based on traffic demand","Reducing operational overhead for capacity management","Detecting model drift and retraining models automatically","Encrypting data at rest and in transit for inference requests","Providing detailed audit logs of API calls to SageMaker"]'::json,
  '{0,1}',
  'Option A (Automatically adjusting instance count based on traffic demand) is a core benefit of auto-scaling, ensuring the endpoint can handle varying loads. Option B (Reducing operational overhead for capacity management) is another significant benefit, as it eliminates the need for manual scaling decisions and interventions. Option C (Detecting model drift and retraining models automatically) is a function of SageMaker Model Monitor and retraining pipelines, not auto-scaling. Option D (Encrypting data at rest and in transit for inference requests) is handled by AWS KMS and network configurations, not auto-scaling. Option E (Providing detailed audit logs of API calls to SageMaker) is a function of AWS CloudTrail, not auto-scaling.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain17_3',
  NOW(),
  13,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_17_2',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_17_m_17_2_106',
  'Which Amazon CloudWatch metrics are crucial for monitoring the performance and error rates of a production Amazon SageMaker real-time inference endpoint to ensure service reliability? (Select THREE.)',
  'multiple',
  '["ModelLatency","Invocations","InvocationError","CPUUtilization","MemoryUtilization"]'::json,
  '{0,1,2}',
  'Option A (ModelLatency) measures the time taken for the model to process an inference request, directly indicating performance. Option B (Invocations) tracks the number of successful inference requests, which is key for throughput monitoring. Option C (InvocationError) counts the number of failed inference requests, directly indicating the error rate and service reliability issues. Option D (CPUUtilization) and Option E (MemoryUtilization) are infrastructure metrics that indicate resource usage but are not direct measures of model performance or error rates, though they can correlate with them.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain17_3',
  NOW(),
  14,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_17_2',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_17_m_17_2_107',
  'Which approaches contribute to cost optimization for Amazon SageMaker inference in production environments by ensuring efficient resource utilization? (Select THREE.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Implementing SageMaker Endpoint Auto Scaling","Right-sizing inference instances based on observed metrics","Leveraging Amazon EC2 Spot Instances for real-time endpoints","Using Amazon CloudWatch Alarms to detect underutilization","Configuring SageMaker Model Monitor for data quality"]'::json,
  '{0,1,3}',
  'Option A (Implementing SageMaker Endpoint Auto Scaling) automatically adjusts the number of instances to match demand, preventing over-provisioning during low traffic. Option B (Right-sizing inference instances based on observed metrics) ensures that the chosen instance type and size are appropriate for the workload, avoiding unnecessary costs from oversized instances. Option D (Using Amazon CloudWatch Alarms to detect underutilization) helps identify when instances are not fully utilized, prompting actions like right-sizing or scaling down. Option C (Leveraging Amazon EC2 Spot Instances for real-time endpoints) is generally not recommended for real-time inference due to potential interruptions, which can impact availability and latency. Option E (Configuring SageMaker Model Monitor for data quality) is for monitoring model performance and data integrity, not directly for infrastructure cost optimization.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain17_3',
  NOW(),
  15,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_17_2',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
