INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_15_m_15_3_116',
  'A data scientist is evaluating a binary classification model. Order the following steps to effectively interpret the model''s performance using common metrics.',
  'ordering',
  '["Calculate precision and recall for the positive class.","Generate a confusion matrix from the model''s predictions.","Determine the overall accuracy of the model.","Plot the Receiver Operating Characteristic (ROC) curve and calculate AUC."]'::json,
  '{1,2,0,3}',
  'To effectively interpret a binary classification model''s performance, the first logical step is to generate a confusion matrix (Option B), which provides a detailed breakdown of true positives, true negatives, false positives, and false negatives. From the confusion matrix, one can then determine the overall accuracy (Option C). Following this, calculating precision and recall (Option A) provides insight into the model''s performance for the positive class, especially useful in imbalanced datasets. Finally, plotting the ROC curve and calculating AUC (Option D) offers a comprehensive view of the model''s ability to distinguish between classes across various thresholds.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain15_3',
  NOW(),
  1,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_15_3',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_15_m_15_3_117',
  'An ML engineer suspects a model is overfitting during training. Order the following common techniques to address overfitting, starting with the most straightforward approach.',
  'ordering',
  '["Implement L1 or L2 regularization.","Reduce the complexity of the model architecture.","Utilize early stopping during training.","Perform cross-validation to ensure generalization."]'::json,
  '{2,0,1,3}',
  'When a model is overfitting, a straightforward initial step is to utilize early stopping (Option C), which halts training when validation performance starts to degrade. If overfitting persists, implementing L1 or L2 regularization (Option A) can penalize large weights and simplify the model. Further, reducing the complexity of the model architecture (Option B) by removing layers or neurons can directly combat overfitting. Finally, performing cross-validation (Option D) is a robust method to ensure the model generalizes well to unseen data and to confirm the effectiveness of the previous steps.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain15_3',
  NOW(),
  2,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_15_3',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_15_m_15_3_118',
  'A data scientist has trained a binary classification model and needs to interpret its performance using a confusion matrix. Order the steps to derive key insights from the matrix.',
  'ordering',
  '["Calculate the F1-score to balance precision and recall.","Identify True Positives, True Negatives, False Positives, and False Negatives.","Determine the overall accuracy.","Compute precision and recall for the positive class."]'::json,
  '{1,2,3,0}',
  'To derive key insights from a confusion matrix, the first step is to identify the raw counts of True Positives, True Negatives, False Positives, and False Negatives (Option B). Once these values are known, the overall accuracy (Option C) can be easily determined. Subsequently, computing precision and recall for the positive class (Option D) provides more nuanced insights into the model''s performance regarding false positives and false negatives. Finally, calculating the F1-score (Option A) combines precision and recall into a single metric, offering a balanced view of the model''s performance, especially useful for imbalanced datasets.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain15_3',
  NOW(),
  3,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_15_3',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_15_m_15_3_119',
  'An ML team wants to use Amazon SageMaker Debugger to monitor their training jobs for anomalies. Order the following steps to set up and utilize SageMaker Debugger.',
  'ordering',
  '["Analyze the collected debugging data using SageMaker Studio.","Configure SageMaker Debugger rules and hook configuration in the training job definition.","Launch the SageMaker Training Job.","Review Debugger reports and take corrective actions if anomalies are detected."]'::json,
  '{1,2,0,3}',
  'To use Amazon SageMaker Debugger, the initial step is to configure the Debugger rules and hook configuration within the SageMaker Training Job definition (Option B). After the configuration is set, the SageMaker Training Job can be launched (Option C), which will automatically collect the specified tensors. Once data is collected, the next step is to analyze this debugging data, typically using SageMaker Studio''s built-in tools (Option A). Finally, based on the analysis, the team can review the Debugger reports and take corrective actions (Option D) to improve model training or address anomalies.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain15_3',
  NOW(),
  4,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_15_3',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_15_m_15_3_120',
  'A company needs to analyze potential bias in their trained ML model using Amazon SageMaker Clarify. Order the steps to perform a post-training bias analysis.',
  'ordering',
  '["Define sensitive features and target labels for bias analysis.","Configure and run a SageMaker Clarify processing job.","Interpret the bias report generated by SageMaker Clarify.","Prepare the input dataset for analysis, ensuring it includes features and predictions."]'::json,
  '{0,3,1,2}',
  'To perform a post-training bias analysis with Amazon SageMaker Clarify, the first step is to define the sensitive features (e.g., gender, age) and target labels (e.g., prediction outcome) that Clarify should analyze for bias (Option A). Next, prepare the input dataset (Option D) which should contain both the features used for prediction and the model''s predictions themselves. With the data and definitions ready, configure and run a SageMaker Clarify processing job (Option B). Finally, interpret the bias report (Option C) generated by Clarify to understand the identified biases and their impact.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain15_3',
  NOW(),
  5,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_15_3',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_15_m_15_3_121',
  'A data scientist observes that their model is consistently underperforming on both training and validation datasets, indicating underfitting. Order the following common strategies to address model underfitting.',
  'ordering',
  '["Increase the complexity of the model architecture.","Add more relevant features to the dataset.","Reduce regularization strength if applied.","Train the model for more epochs."]'::json,
  '{1,0,3,2}',
  'When a model is underfitting, the first step is often to add more relevant features to the dataset (Option B), as a lack of informative features can prevent the model from learning patterns. If the model still underfits, increasing the complexity of the model architecture (Option A) by adding more layers or neurons can give it more capacity to learn. Training the model for more epochs (Option D) allows it more time to converge and learn from the data. Finally, if regularization was previously applied, reducing its strength (Option C) can prevent the model from being overly constrained and allow it to fit the training data better.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain15_3',
  NOW(),
  6,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_15_3',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_15_m_15_3_122',
  'After training a binary classification model, an ML engineer needs to adjust the prediction threshold to optimize for a specific business outcome, such as minimizing false positives. Order the steps to tune the threshold.',
  'ordering',
  '["Evaluate the model''s performance metrics (e.g., precision, recall) at the new threshold.","Generate predicted probabilities for the test dataset.","Select a new threshold based on the desired balance of metrics.","Plot precision-recall curves or ROC curves to visualize trade-offs."]'::json,
  '{1,3,2,0}',
  'To tune a model''s prediction threshold, the first step is to generate predicted probabilities for the test dataset (Option B), as thresholds are applied to these probabilities. Next, plotting precision-recall curves or ROC curves (Option D) helps visualize the trade-offs between different metrics at various thresholds. Based on this visualization and the business objective, a new threshold can be selected (Option C). Finally, evaluate the model''s performance metrics (Option A) at this chosen threshold to confirm it meets the desired business outcome.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain15_3',
  NOW(),
  7,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_15_3',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

