INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_15_m_15_3_129',
  'A machine learning engineer is setting up Amazon SageMaker Debugger to monitor a complex deep learning training job. The goal is to detect anomalies in real-time by observing specific tensors and system metrics, ensuring the model converges correctly and efficiently. To achieve this, the engineer needs to correctly configure the debugger. Order the following steps to properly set up SageMaker Debugger for real-time analysis.',
  'ordering',
  '["Attach a debugger hook to the SageMaker estimator, specifying the collections to save.","Define SageMaker Debugger rules for anomaly detection, such as `LossNotDecreasing` or `Overfit`. ","Launch the SageMaker Training Job with the configured estimator and debugger hook.","Analyze debugger output and rule evaluations in SageMaker Studio to identify issues."]'::json,
  '{0,1,2,3}',
  'To effectively use Amazon SageMaker Debugger for real-time analysis, the steps must be followed in a specific order. Option A (correct) is the initial step, where a debugger hook is attached to the SageMaker estimator. This hook specifies which tensors and system metrics should be collected during training. Option B (correct) follows, as rules for anomaly detection are defined to automatically monitor the collected data for specific patterns or issues. Option C (correct) is next, where the SageMaker Training Job is launched, allowing the debugger to start collecting data and evaluating rules. Finally, Option D (correct) involves analyzing the debugger output and rule evaluations, typically within SageMaker Studio, to understand training progress and diagnose any detected anomalies.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain15_3',
  NOW(),
  14,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_15_3',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_15_m_15_3_130',
  'A financial institution needs to ensure its loan approval model, trained on Amazon SageMaker, is fair and does not exhibit bias against specific demographic groups. The data scientists want to analyze the model for pre-training and post-training bias using SageMaker Clarify. They aim to systematically identify and mitigate potential biases throughout the model lifecycle. Order the steps they should take to conduct this comprehensive bias analysis.',
  'ordering',
  '["Define sensitive features (e.g., age, gender) and the facet for pre-training bias analysis.","Prepare the dataset and the trained model artifact or endpoint for SageMaker Clarify analysis.","Configure a SageMaker Clarify processing job for post-training bias analysis, including metrics.","Analyze the generated bias reports and explainability plots to identify and quantify biases."]'::json,
  '{1,0,2,3}',
  'To conduct a comprehensive bias analysis using Amazon SageMaker Clarify, a structured approach is essential. Option B (correct) is the foundational first step, requiring the preparation of the input dataset and the trained model (or its endpoint) that Clarify will analyze. Option A (correct) follows, where sensitive attributes and the specific facet (the feature to check for bias) are defined for pre-training bias detection. Option C (correct) then involves configuring the Clarify processing job specifically for post-training bias, which assesses bias in the model''s predictions. Finally, Option D (correct) is the concluding step, where the data scientists review the detailed bias reports and explainability plots generated by Clarify to understand and address any identified fairness issues.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain15_3',
  NOW(),
  15,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_15_3',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_15_m_15_3_131',
  'When evaluating a multi-class classification model''s performance, a data scientist generates a confusion matrix. This matrix provides a detailed breakdown of correct and incorrect predictions for each class. To systematically interpret this matrix and understand the model''s strengths and weaknesses for each class, order the following interpretation steps.',
  'ordering',
  '["Identify the true positives, true negatives, false positives, and false negatives for each individual class.","Calculate precision, recall, and F1-score for each class based on the identified counts.","Determine the overall accuracy of the model by summing the diagonal elements and dividing by the total.","Analyze misclassification patterns by examining off-diagonal elements to understand common confusions."]'::json,
  '{2,0,1,3}',
  'Interpreting a confusion matrix systematically is crucial for understanding a multi-class classification model''s performance. Option C (correct) is the initial step, as overall accuracy provides a high-level view of correct predictions. Option A (correct) then involves breaking down the matrix to identify the specific counts of true positives, true negatives, false positives, and false negatives for each class, which are fundamental for more granular analysis. Option B (correct) follows, where these counts are used to calculate per-class metrics like precision, recall, and F1-score, offering insights into the model''s performance for each category. Finally, Option D (correct) involves analyzing the off-diagonal elements to understand specific misclassification patterns, revealing which classes are most often confused with others.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain15_3',
  NOW(),
  16,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_15_3',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_15_m_15_3_132',
  'A machine learning team has trained a complex deep learning model on a large dataset using Amazon SageMaker. During evaluation, they observe that the model performs exceptionally well on the training data but poorly on unseen validation data, indicating severe overfitting. To systematically address this issue, order the following mitigation strategies from initial, less resource-intensive responses to more resource-intensive solutions.',
  'ordering',
  '["Implement regularization techniques like L1/L2 penalties or dropout layers in the model architecture.","Utilize early stopping during training, monitoring validation loss to prevent further overfitting.","Perform hyperparameter tuning with SageMaker Automatic Model Tuning (HPO) to find optimal parameters.","Collect more diverse and representative training data to improve generalization."]'::json,
  '{1,0,2,3}',
  'Addressing overfitting requires a systematic approach, starting with less resource-intensive methods. Option B (correct) is often the first and simplest step: utilizing early stopping to halt training when validation performance starts to degrade, preventing the model from learning noise in the training data. Option A (correct) follows, where regularization techniques such as L1/L2 penalties or dropout layers are added to the model to constrain complexity and improve generalization. Option C (correct) involves more extensive effort, using SageMaker Automatic Model Tuning to systematically search for hyperparameter combinations that yield better generalization. Finally, Option D (correct) is generally the most resource-intensive and time-consuming solution, involving the collection of more diverse data, which is pursued if other regularization methods prove insufficient. This aligns with anti-pattern ap-2.3-001, which advises against immediately collecting more data without trying regularization first.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain15_3',
  NOW(),
  17,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_15_3',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_15_m_15_3_133',
  'A data scientist is evaluating a binary classification model trained on Amazon SageMaker. The model shows high accuracy but poor performance on the minority class. To understand the model''s behavior better and potentially tune its threshold, arrange the following steps in the logical order for analyzing its performance using a confusion matrix.',
  'ordering',
  '["Calculate Precision, Recall, and F1-score for each class.","Identify the actual positive and negative instances in the test set.","Determine the model''s predictions (positive/negative) for the test set.","Construct the confusion matrix using True Positives, True Negatives, False Positives, and False Negatives."]'::json,
  '{1,2,3,0}',
  'To analyze model performance with a confusion matrix, the logical first step is to understand the ground truth. Option B (Identify the actual positive and negative instances in the test set) establishes this baseline. Next, Option C (Determine the model''s predictions (positive/negative) for the test set) involves obtaining the model''s output. With both actuals and predictions, Option D (Construct the confusion matrix using True Positives, True Negatives, False Positives, and False Negatives) can be performed to categorize the outcomes. Finally, Option A (Calculate Precision, Recall, and F1-score for each class) uses the values from the confusion matrix to derive key performance metrics, which are crucial for understanding performance on minority classes.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain15_3',
  NOW(),
  18,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_15_3',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_15_m_15_3_134',
  'An ML engineer needs to debug a training job running on Amazon SageMaker that is exhibiting unexpected behavior, such as a sudden drop in accuracy. Order the steps to effectively use Amazon SageMaker Debugger to analyze and identify the root cause.',
  'ordering',
  '["Configure SageMaker Debugger to save tensors and rules for anomaly detection.","Review Debugger reports and visualizations in SageMaker Studio or S3.","Launch the SageMaker Training Job with Debugger enabled.","Analyze the detected anomalies and tensor values to pinpoint issues like vanishing gradients or overfitting."]'::json,
  '{0,2,1,3}',
  'Before starting a training job, SageMaker Debugger must be configured. Option A (Configure SageMaker Debugger to save tensors and rules for anomaly detection) sets up the monitoring. Once configured, Option C (Launch the SageMaker Training Job with Debugger enabled) initiates the training process where Debugger collects data. After the job runs, Option B (Review Debugger reports and visualizations in SageMaker Studio or S3) allows the engineer to examine the collected data and rule evaluations. Finally, Option D (Analyze the detected anomalies and tensor values to pinpoint issues like vanishing gradients or overfitting) involves interpreting these reports to diagnose the problem.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain15_3',
  NOW(),
  19,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_15_3',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_15_m_15_3_135',
  'A company wants to ensure fairness and explainability for a new credit risk assessment model deployed on Amazon SageMaker. Order the steps to use Amazon SageMaker Clarify for post-training bias detection and feature attribution.',
  'ordering',
  '["Configure SageMaker Clarify to generate bias reports and explainability reports.","Review the generated reports to identify potential biases and understand feature importance.","Define the sensitive attributes and target labels for bias analysis.","Run the SageMaker Clarify processing job on the trained model and dataset."]'::json,
  '{2,0,3,1}',
  'The first step in using SageMaker Clarify for bias detection is to define what constitutes a sensitive attribute and the target outcome. Option C (Define the sensitive attributes and target labels for bias analysis) establishes these parameters. Next, Option A (Configure SageMaker Clarify to generate bias reports and explainability reports) specifies the types of analyses to perform. With the configuration set, Option D (Run the SageMaker Clarify processing job on the trained model and dataset) executes the analysis. Finally, Option B (Review the generated reports to identify potential biases and understand feature importance) allows the data scientist to interpret the findings and take action.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain15_3',
  NOW(),
  20,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_15_3',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_15_m_15_3_136',
  'A machine learning team has observed that their model performs well on training data but poorly on unseen validation data, indicating overfitting. Order the following actions to systematically address this issue, prioritizing common and cost-effective solutions first.',
  'ordering',
  '["Implement regularization techniques (e.g., L1, L2, dropout) or early stopping during training.","Perform cross-validation to get a more robust estimate of model performance.","Collect more diverse and representative training data.","Analyze model complexity and reduce the number of features or model parameters if necessary."]'::json,
  '{1,0,3,2}',
  'When overfitting is suspected, the first step is to confirm it with robust evaluation. Option B (Perform cross-validation to get a more robust estimate of model performance) helps ensure the issue isn''t just a validation set anomaly. Next, Option A (Implement regularization techniques (e.g., L1, L2, dropout) or early stopping during training) are common and cost-effective first responses to overfitting. If regularization is insufficient, Option D (Analyze model complexity and reduce the number of features or model parameters if necessary) directly addresses the model''s capacity to overfit. Option C (Collect more diverse and representative training data) is often effective but can be time-consuming and expensive, making it a later step if simpler methods fail, aligning with anti-pattern [ap-2.3-001].',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain15_3',
  NOW(),
  21,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_15_3',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_15_m_15_3_137',
  'A data scientist is evaluating a new forecasting model for time-series data. Order the following steps to ensure a comprehensive evaluation process.',
  'ordering',
  '["Split the dataset into training, validation, and test sets, ensuring temporal order is maintained.","Select appropriate evaluation metrics such as RMSE, MAE, or MAPE for time-series data.","Train the model on the training data and tune hyperparameters using the validation set.","Evaluate the final model performance on the unseen test set and compare against a baseline."]'::json,
  '{0,1,2,3}',
  'The evaluation process begins with preparing the data. Option A (Split the dataset into training, validation, and test sets, ensuring temporal order is maintained) is crucial for time-series models to prevent data leakage. Next, Option B (Select appropriate evaluation metrics such as RMSE, MAE, or MAPE for time-series data) defines how the model''s performance will be measured. Option C (Train the model on the training data and tune hyperparameters using the validation set) is the core training and refinement phase. Finally, Option D (Evaluate the final model performance on the unseen test set and compare against a baseline) provides an unbiased assessment of the model''s generalization capability.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain15_3',
  NOW(),
  22,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_15_3',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_15_m_15_3_138',
  'A team is developing a new image classification model using Amazon SageMaker. They want to track and compare different experiments to identify the best performing model configuration. Order the steps to effectively use Amazon SageMaker Experiments for this purpose.',
  'ordering',
  '["Define an experiment and associate training jobs with it.","Compare experiment runs based on metrics, hyperparameters, and artifacts.","Run multiple training jobs with varying hyperparameters or model architectures.","Analyze the results to select the best performing model for deployment."]'::json,
  '{0,2,1,3}',
  'To use SageMaker Experiments, the first step is to organize the work. Option A (Define an experiment and associate training jobs with it) creates the container for tracking. Next, Option C (Run multiple training jobs with varying hyperparameters or model architectures) executes the different trials within the experiment. Once runs are complete, Option B (Compare experiment runs based on metrics, hyperparameters, and artifacts) allows for side-by-side analysis. Finally, Option D (Analyze the results to select the best performing model for deployment) uses the insights gained to make a decision.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain15_3',
  NOW(),
  23,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_15_3',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

