INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_16_m_16_2_137',
  'An MLOps team at a global retail company is responsible for deploying a new recommendation model to Amazon SageMaker for real-time inference. They are committed to using Infrastructure as Code (IaC) with AWS CloudFormation to ensure consistent, repeatable, and auditable deployments across multiple environments. The model artifacts are already stored in Amazon S3, and a custom Docker image is available in Amazon ECR. The team needs to define and deploy the SageMaker endpoint.',
  'ordering',
  '["Define the `AWS::SageMaker::Endpoint` resource, referencing the endpoint configuration.","Define the `AWS::SageMaker::Model` resource, specifying the container image and model data S3 URI.","Deploy the CloudFormation stack.","Define the `AWS::SageMaker::EndpointConfig` resource, specifying instance types and model variants."]'::json,
  '{1,3,0,2}',
  'When deploying a SageMaker endpoint using CloudFormation, resources must be defined in a specific order due to their dependencies. Option B (Define the `AWS::SageMaker::Model` resource) is the initial step, as it represents the trained model and its artifacts, which are prerequisites for an endpoint configuration. Option D (Define the `AWS::SageMaker::EndpointConfig` resource) comes next, as it specifies the hosting parameters, including instance types and the model(s) to be hosted. This configuration depends on a defined model. Option A (Define the `AWS::SageMaker::Endpoint` resource) is the final definition, as it creates the actual endpoint and references the previously defined endpoint configuration. Finally, Option C (Deploy the CloudFormation stack) is the action that provisions all these resources in the correct sequence. Attempting to deploy without defining these dependencies in order would lead to CloudFormation deployment failures.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain16_3',
  NOW(),
  16,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_16_2',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_16_m_16_2_138',
  'A media streaming service is experiencing highly variable traffic to its personalized content recommendation model deployed on an Amazon SageMaker real-time endpoint. During peak hours, the endpoint struggles to keep up, while during off-peak, resources are over-provisioned, leading to unnecessary costs. The MLOps team needs to implement auto-scaling to optimize performance and cost efficiency for this critical endpoint.',
  'ordering',
  '["Define a scaling policy (e.g., target tracking for CPU utilization or invocations).","Create an IAM role with permissions for Application Auto Scaling to manage SageMaker endpoints.","Register the SageMaker endpoint variant as a scalable target with Application Auto Scaling.","Apply the scaling policy to the registered scalable target.","Monitor the endpoint''s metrics and auto-scaling events in Amazon CloudWatch."]'::json,
  '{1,2,0,3,4}',
  'To set up auto-scaling for a SageMaker endpoint, a logical sequence of steps is required. Option B (Create an IAM role) is the initial prerequisite, as Application Auto Scaling needs specific permissions to modify SageMaker endpoint capacities. Option C (Register the SageMaker endpoint variant as a scalable target) identifies the specific resource that Application Auto Scaling will manage. Option A (Define a scaling policy) specifies the conditions under which scaling should occur (e.g., target CPU utilization). Option D (Apply the scaling policy to the registered scalable target) links the defined policy to the endpoint variant, enabling auto-scaling. Finally, Option E (Monitor the endpoint''s metrics and auto-scaling events in Amazon CloudWatch) is crucial for verifying the effectiveness of the auto-scaling configuration and making adjustments if needed. Skipping or misordering these steps would prevent auto-scaling from functioning correctly, leading to continued over- or under-provisioning, which aligns with anti-pattern [ap-3.1-002].',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain16_3',
  NOW(),
  17,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_16_2',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_16_m_16_2_139',
  'An e-commerce platform wants to perform A/B testing for two different versions of its product recommendation model on a single Amazon SageMaker endpoint. The goal is to gradually shift traffic to the better-performing model version without downtime. The MLOps team uses CloudFormation for managing their SageMaker infrastructure and needs to define the endpoint configuration to support this A/B testing strategy.',
  'ordering',
  '["Define `ProductionVariant` for Model A, specifying initial traffic weight and instance details.","Define `ProductionVariant` for Model B, specifying initial traffic weight and instance details.","Define the `AWS::SageMaker::EndpointConfig` resource.","Define `AWS::SageMaker::Model` for Model A (old version).","Define `AWS::SageMaker::Model` for Model B (new version)."]'::json,
  '{3,4,2,0,1}',
  'To set up an A/B testing endpoint configuration in CloudFormation, models must be defined before they can be referenced in variants. Option D (Define `AWS::SageMaker::Model` for Model A) and Option E (Define `AWS::SageMaker::Model` for Model B) are the initial steps, as both model versions must exist as SageMaker Model resources. Option C (Define the `AWS::SageMaker::EndpointConfig` resource) is the next logical step, as it''s the container for the variants. Option A (Define `ProductionVariant` for Model A) and Option B (Define `ProductionVariant` for Model B) then specify how each model will be hosted, including their respective instance types and initial traffic distribution. The order of defining Model A and B, or their respective variants, can be flexible as long as they precede the `EndpointConfig` definition and are within it. This sequence ensures that the endpoint configuration correctly references the available models for A/B testing.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain16_3',
  NOW(),
  18,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_16_2',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_16_m_16_2_140',
  'A manufacturing company uses a computer vision model for quality control on its assembly line. The model needs to be deployed to an AWS IoT Greengrass edge device with limited compute resources. To achieve optimal performance and reduce inference latency on the edge, the MLOps team decides to use Amazon SageMaker Neo to compile the trained model for the specific target hardware. They need to define the compilation process.',
  'ordering',
  '["Create a SageMaker Neo compilation job, specifying the input model, target instance, and output S3 location.","Train the machine learning model using a supported framework (e.g., TensorFlow, PyTorch).","Deploy the compiled model to the target edge device or SageMaker endpoint.","Upload the trained model artifacts to an Amazon S3 bucket."]'::json,
  '{1,3,0,2}',
  'The process of optimizing and deploying a model with SageMaker Neo follows a clear sequence. Option B (Train the machine learning model) is the fundamental first step, as a trained model is required for compilation. Option D (Upload the trained model artifacts to an Amazon S3 bucket) is necessary next, as SageMaker Neo requires the model artifacts to be accessible from S3 for the compilation job. Option A (Create a SageMaker Neo compilation job) is then performed, where the trained model is submitted for optimization, specifying the target hardware and software. Finally, Option C (Deploy the compiled model to the target edge device or SageMaker endpoint) makes the optimized model available for inference. This ensures the model is first trained, then made accessible, then optimized, and finally deployed.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain16_3',
  NOW(),
  19,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_16_2',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_16_m_16_2_141',
  'A data science team at a large logistics company has developed hundreds of small, frequently updated models for predicting delivery times. To reduce operational overhead and cost, they want to consolidate these models onto a single Amazon SageMaker Multi-Model Endpoint (MME). The MLOps team needs to set up the MME infrastructure using CloudFormation to ensure scalability and maintainability.',
  'ordering',
  '["Define the `AWS::SageMaker::Model` resource using the SageMaker Multi-Model Endpoint container.","Create an Amazon S3 bucket to store the individual model artifacts.","Define the `AWS::SageMaker::EndpointConfig` resource, specifying the MME model and instance types.","Deploy the CloudFormation stack to provision the MME.","Upload individual model artifacts to the designated S3 bucket."]'::json,
  '{1,0,2,3,4}',
  'Setting up a SageMaker Multi-Model Endpoint (MME) with CloudFormation requires a specific order of resource definition and preparation. Option B (Create an Amazon S3 bucket to store the individual model artifacts) is the initial prerequisite, as this bucket will host all the individual models. Option A (Define the `AWS::SageMaker::Model` resource using the SageMaker Multi-Model Endpoint container) is the next step, where a special SageMaker Model resource is created to serve as the MME''s primary container, referencing the S3 bucket. Option C (Define the `AWS::SageMaker::EndpointConfig` resource) then specifies the hosting parameters for the MME, including instance types. Option D (Deploy the CloudFormation stack) provisions the MME infrastructure. Finally, Option E (Upload individual model artifacts to the designated S3 bucket) is performed after the infrastructure is ready, allowing the MME to dynamically load and serve these models. While individual models can be uploaded anytime after the S3 bucket is created, the MME infrastructure must be in place first.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain16_3',
  NOW(),
  20,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_16_2',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_16_m_16_2_142',
  'An MLOps team needs to update a critical production Amazon SageMaker real-time endpoint with a new, improved model version. They are using AWS CloudFormation for infrastructure management and must ensure the update process is automated, traceable, and minimizes downtime. The new model has been trained and its artifacts are in Amazon S3, and a new Docker image is in Amazon ECR.',
  'ordering',
  '["Update the `AWS::SageMaker::Model` resource in CloudFormation to reference the new model artifacts and ECR image.","Train and evaluate the new model version.","Apply the updated CloudFormation stack to initiate the endpoint update.","Update the `AWS::SageMaker::EndpointConfig` resource to reference the new model."]'::json,
  '{1,0,3,2}',
  'Updating a SageMaker endpoint with a new model via CloudFormation requires a logical progression. Option B (Train and evaluate the new model version) is the initial step, ensuring the new model is ready for deployment. Option A (Update the `AWS::SageMaker::Model` resource) is next, as the new model artifacts and container image must be registered as a SageMaker Model. Option D (Update the `AWS::SageMaker::EndpointConfig` resource) then modifies the endpoint configuration to point to this newly defined model. This step is crucial for SageMaker to know which model version to serve. Finally, Option C (Apply the updated CloudFormation stack) triggers the deployment process, which SageMaker handles as an in-place update or by creating new variants, depending on the configuration. This sequence ensures that the new model is properly defined and referenced before the endpoint update is initiated.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain16_3',
  NOW(),
  21,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_16_2',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_16_m_16_2_143',
  'A data scientist needs to containerize a custom machine learning model for deployment on Amazon SageMaker using Docker and Amazon ECR. Arrange the following steps in the correct order to achieve this.',
  'ordering',
  '["Push the Docker image to Amazon ECR.","Build the Docker image locally.","Write a Dockerfile to define the container environment.","Tag the Docker image with the Amazon ECR repository URI."]'::json,
  '{2,1,3,0}',
  'Option C (Write a Dockerfile to define the container environment) is the initial step, as the Dockerfile specifies how the image is built. Option B (Build the Docker image locally) follows, as the Dockerfile is used to create the image. Option D (Tag the Docker image with the Amazon ECR repository URI) is necessary before pushing, as it associates the local image with the remote repository. Finally, Option A (Push the Docker image to Amazon ECR) uploads the tagged image to the registry, making it available for SageMaker.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain16_3',
  NOW(),
  22,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_16_2',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

